ML_PAGE("tutorials", "<header class=\"page-header theme-exam\"><span class=\"eyebrow\">60 TUTORIAL QUESTIONS · 18 REFLECTIONS · EXAM-READY ANSWERS</span><h2>Tutorial Questions</h2><p>Every question from the course tutorial sets, sorted into the textbook chapter that teaches it. The course numbers its <b>topics</b> differently from the textbook&#39;s <b>chapters</b>, so one tutorial can span two chapters. Try each question first, then open the exam-ready answer: formula, substitution, result and interpretation for calculations; define, explain, apply and evaluate for concepts.</p></header><details class=\"ff-index tut-map\"><summary>Which tutorial topic belongs to which chapter?</summary><div class=\"scroll-x\"><table class=\"comparison-table\"><thead><tr><th>Tutorial</th><th>Title</th><th>Chapters in this app</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><b>Topics 3 and 4</b></td><td>Modern Portfolio Concepts and CAPM</td><td>Chapter 5: Modern Portfolio Concepts</td></tr><tr><td><b>Topic 5</b></td><td>Common Stocks</td><td>Chapter 6: Common Stocks, Chapter 7: Analyzing Common Stocks</td></tr><tr><td><b>Topic 6</b></td><td>Stock Valuation</td><td>Chapter 8: Stock Valuation</td></tr><tr><td><b>Topic 7</b></td><td>Market Efficiency and Technical Analysis</td><td>Chapter 9: Market Efficiency and Behavioral Finance</td></tr><tr><td><b>Topics 8 and 9</b></td><td>Fixed-Income Securities and Bond Valuation</td><td>Chapter 10: Fixed-Income Securities, Chapter 11: Bond Valuation</td></tr><tr><td><b>Topics 10 and 11</b></td><td>Mutual Funds, ETFs and Options</td><td>Chapter 12: Mutual Funds and Exchange-Traded Funds, Chapter 14: Options: Puts and Calls</td></tr></tbody></table></div></details><div class=\"ff-controls tut-controls\"><div class=\"ff-chips\" role=\"group\" aria-label=\"Filter by chapter\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"ff-chip on\" data-tut-chip=\"all\">All chapters</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"ff-chip\" data-tut-chip=\"5\">Ch 5</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"ff-chip\" data-tut-chip=\"6\">Ch 6</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"ff-chip\" data-tut-chip=\"7\">Ch 7</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"ff-chip\" data-tut-chip=\"8\">Ch 8</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"ff-chip\" data-tut-chip=\"9\">Ch 9</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"ff-chip\" data-tut-chip=\"10\">Ch 10</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"ff-chip\" data-tut-chip=\"11\">Ch 11</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"ff-chip\" data-tut-chip=\"12\">Ch 12</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"ff-chip\" data-tut-chip=\"14\">Ch 14</button></div><div class=\"ff-chips\" role=\"group\" aria-label=\"Filter by question type\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"ff-chip on\" data-tut-type=\"all\">All types</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"ff-chip\" data-tut-type=\"Concept\">Concept</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"ff-chip\" data-tut-type=\"Calculation\">Calculation</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"ff-chip\" data-tut-type=\"Reflection\">Reflection</button></div></div><p class=\"ff-empty tut-empty\" hidden>No tutorial questions match these filters.</p><div class=\"tut-group theme-l5\" data-tut-group=\"5\"><h3 class=\"tut-group-title\"><span>Chapter 5</span> Modern Portfolio Concepts <small>13 questions</small></h3><div class=\"question-bank\"><details class=\"bank-question tut-q\" data-tut-ch=\"5\" data-tut-t=\"Concept\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q1</span><div><div class=\"tut-tags\"><span class=\"tut-topic\">Topics 3 and 4 · Q1</span><span class=\"tut-type tut-concept\">Concept</span></div><strong class=\"tut-title\">Portfolio objectives and efficiency</strong></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body markdown-body compact\"><p>Define an investment portfolio. Distinguish a growth-oriented portfolio from an income-oriented portfolio. What makes a portfolio efficient, and why can the efficient portfolio differ between investors?</p><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal the exam-ready answer</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><h4>Definitions</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Investment portfolio:</strong> a collection of investments (shares, bonds, sukuk, funds and so on) assembled to meet one or more investment goals. Weights $w_i$ show the proportion of wealth in each asset, and $\\sum w_i = 1$.</li>\n<li><strong>Growth-oriented portfolio:</strong> aims at long-term capital appreciation. It holds mainly shares of firms that reinvest profits, pay little dividend and offer price growth.</li>\n<li><strong>Income-oriented portfolio:</strong> aims at regular cash flow. It holds dividend-paying shares, bonds, sukuk and similar assets.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Growth versus income</h4>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Feature</th>\n<th>Growth-oriented</th>\n<th>Income-oriented</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Main return source</td>\n<td>Capital gains</td>\n<td>Dividends and interest or profit payments</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Typical holdings</td>\n<td>Growth shares, small and mid caps</td>\n<td>Dividend shares, bonds, sukuk</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Risk and volatility</td>\n<td>Higher</td>\n<td>Lower</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Suits</td>\n<td>Long horizon, no need for current cash</td>\n<td>Retirees, those needing regular cash</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h4>What makes a portfolio efficient</h4>\n<p>An <strong>efficient portfolio</strong> gives the highest expected return for a given level of risk, or equivalently the lowest risk for a given expected return. It is judged by the trade-off, not by return alone.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Portfolio $A$ with $E(R)=8\\%$ and $\\sigma=10\\%$ is dominated by $B$ with $E(R)=10\\%$ and $\\sigma=10\\%$: same risk, more return.</li>\n<li>$B$ and $C$ (with $E(R)=12\\%$ and $\\sigma=20\\%$): $C$ pays more but carries more risk, so neither dominates the other; both can lie on the frontier.</li>\n<li>The set of all efficient portfolios forms the <strong>efficient frontier</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Why the efficient portfolio differs between investors</h4>\n<p>The frontier lists the efficient choices. The investor's own <strong>risk tolerance</strong> picks one of them, because investors differ in:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>age and investment horizon;</li>\n<li>income needs and liquidity needs;</li>\n<li>willingness and ability to bear losses.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>A retiree needing income picks a low-risk point on the frontier. A young investor with a long horizon picks a higher-risk, higher-return point. Neither is wrong.</p>\n<p><strong>Limitation:</strong> efficiency is measured with estimated returns and risks, which can change, so \"efficient\" holds only under those estimates.</p>\n<p><strong>Conclusion:</strong> a portfolio is efficient when no other portfolio offers more return for the same risk or less risk for the same return, and which efficient portfolio is <em>best</em> depends on the investor.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question tut-q\" data-tut-ch=\"5\" data-tut-t=\"Concept\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q2</span><div><div class=\"tut-tags\"><span class=\"tut-topic\">Topics 3 and 4 · Q2</span><span class=\"tut-type tut-concept\">Concept</span></div><strong class=\"tut-title\">Portfolio return and portfolio risk</strong></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body markdown-body compact\"><p>Explain how expected portfolio return is calculated. Why is portfolio standard deviation generally not a simple weighted average of the individual assets' standard deviations? Include covariance or correlation in your explanation.</p><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal the exam-ready answer</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><h4>Expected portfolio return</h4>\n<p>Expected portfolio return is the <strong>weighted average</strong> of the assets' expected returns:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$E(R_p)=\\sum_{i=1}^{n} w_i\\,E(R_i)$$</div>\n<p>Here $w_i$ is the proportion invested in asset $i$ and $E(R_i)$ is its expected return. Return is <em>linear</em> in the weights, so combining assets never changes the averaging: with no short selling or borrowing ($0\\le w_i\\le 1$), a portfolio cannot earn more than its best asset or less than its worst.</p>\n<h4>Why risk is not a weighted average</h4>\n<p>Weighted-average standard deviation ignores how the assets move <strong>together</strong>. For two assets $A$ and $B$:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\sigma_p^2=w_A^2\\sigma_A^2+w_B^2\\sigma_B^2+2\\,w_Aw_B\\,\\sigma_A\\sigma_B\\,\\rho_{AB}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\sigma_p=\\sqrt{\\sigma_p^2}$$</div>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Covariance:</strong> $\\operatorname{Cov}(A,B)=\\rho_{AB}\\,\\sigma_A\\sigma_B$. It measures whether the returns move in the same direction. The last term of the variance equation is $2\\,w_Aw_B\\operatorname{Cov}(A,B)$.</li>\n<li><strong>Correlation:</strong> $\\rho_{AB}$ is the standardised covariance, with $-1\\le\\rho_{AB}\\le 1$.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Mechanism</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>If $\\rho_{AB}=+1$, the last term is as large as possible and $\\sigma_p$ equals the weighted average: $w_A\\sigma_A+w_B\\sigma_B$.</li>\n<li>If $\\rho_{AB}&lt;1$, the last term is smaller, so <strong>$\\sigma_p&lt;w_A\\sigma_A+w_B\\sigma_B$</strong>. When one asset falls, the other tends not to fall by as much, so the ups and downs partly cancel.</li>\n<li>Therefore diversification benefit comes from the <strong>correlation term</strong>, not from the standard deviations alone.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Limitation:</strong> the correlation is estimated from past data and can rise in a crisis, so the risk reduction is not guaranteed.</p>\n<p><strong>Conclusion:</strong> expected return is a simple weighted average, but portfolio risk depends on weights, individual risks <em>and</em> correlation, so $\\sigma_p\\ne\\sum w_i\\sigma_i$ unless $\\rho=+1$.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question tut-q\" data-tut-ch=\"5 2 4\" data-tut-t=\"Concept\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q3</span><div><div class=\"tut-tags\"><span class=\"tut-topic\">Topics 3 and 4 · Q3</span><span class=\"tut-type tut-concept\">Concept</span><span class=\"tut-also\">Also Ch 2</span><span class=\"tut-also\">Also Ch 4</span></div><strong class=\"tut-title\">Correlation and diversification</strong></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body markdown-body compact\"><p>Interpret correlation coefficients of $+1$, $0$ and $-1$. Explain how correlation affects diversification benefits. What additional benefits and risks may arise when a Malaysian investor diversifies internationally?</p><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal the exam-ready answer</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><h4>Interpreting the correlation coefficient</h4>\n<p>The <strong>correlation coefficient</strong> $\\rho$ measures how two assets' returns move together, on a scale from $-1$ to $+1$.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>$\\rho$</th>\n<th>Meaning</th>\n<th>Effect on diversification</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>$+1$</td>\n<td>Perfect positive: the returns move together in a perfect positive linear relationship</td>\n<td>None. $\\sigma_p$ is the weighted average of the two $\\sigma$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>$0$</td>\n<td>Uncorrelated: no linear relationship</td>\n<td>Good. Risk falls below the weighted average</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>$-1$</td>\n<td>Perfect negative: always move in opposite directions</td>\n<td>Maximum. Risk can be reduced to zero with the right weights</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h4>How correlation affects diversification</h4>\n<p>From $\\sigma_p^2=w_A^2\\sigma_A^2+w_B^2\\sigma_B^2+2w_Aw_B\\sigma_A\\sigma_B\\rho_{AB}$, a lower $\\rho$ shrinks the last term and so lowers $\\sigma_p$ with the same weights, the same standard deviations and the same expected return. The <strong>lower the correlation, the larger the benefit</strong>. Five Malaysian banks are five shares, but with a high $\\rho$ they diversify little.</p>\n<h4>International diversification for a Malaysian investor</h4>\n<p><strong>Additional benefits</strong>\n- Economic cycles, interest rates and policies differ across countries, so foreign markets may have lower correlation with Bursa Malaysia.\n- Access to industries and companies that Bursa lacks (such as large technology firms) and to fast-growing economies.\n- Less concentration in one country's recession, politics or ringgit-specific shocks.\n- Possible gain if foreign currencies appreciate against the ringgit.</p>\n<p><strong>Additional risks</strong>\n- <strong>Currency risk:</strong> the return in ringgit is $1+R_{\\text{RM}}=(1+R_{\\text{foreign}})(1+R_{\\text{FX}})$. A gain in the foreign currency can be wiped out by depreciation.\n- <strong>Higher correlation in crises:</strong> markets tend to fall together in a global crisis, exactly when diversification is needed.\n- <strong>Government-policy and political risk</strong>, such as taxes or capital controls.\n- <strong>Costs and convenience:</strong> higher transaction and custody costs, time-zone differences, less information and regulatory protection.</p>\n<p><strong>Conclusion:</strong> correlation, not the number of holdings, drives diversification, and international exposure can lower portfolio risk if the correlation is low and currency and other foreign risks are controlled <em>(currency link: Chapters 2 and 4)</em>.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question tut-q\" data-tut-ch=\"5 4\" data-tut-t=\"Concept\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q4</span><div><div class=\"tut-tags\"><span class=\"tut-topic\">Topics 3 and 4 · Q4</span><span class=\"tut-type tut-concept\">Concept</span><span class=\"tut-also\">Also Ch 4</span></div><strong class=\"tut-title\">Components of risk and beta</strong></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body markdown-body compact\"><p>Distinguish diversifiable or unsystematic risk from undiversifiable or systematic risk. Define beta and interpret beta values of $0.6$, $1.0$ and $1.4$. Why does a diversified investor focus mainly on systematic risk?</p><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal the exam-ready answer</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><h4>Two components of total risk</h4>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th></th>\n<th>Unsystematic (diversifiable)</th>\n<th>Systematic (undiversifiable)</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Source</td>\n<td>Firm- or industry-specific events: management error, strike, product failure, lawsuit</td>\n<td>Market-wide factors: recession, interest rates, inflation, political events</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Affects</td>\n<td>One firm or sector</td>\n<td>All securities, to different degrees</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Can diversification remove it?</td>\n<td>Yes, largely</td>\n<td>No</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Measure</td>\n<td>Firm-specific (residual) variance, not explained by the market</td>\n<td>Beta</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Total risk $=$ systematic risk $+$ unsystematic risk. Adding more securities reduces portfolio risk until it reaches a floor, the market (systematic) risk.</p>\n<h4>Beta</h4>\n<p><strong>Beta</strong> ($\\beta$) measures the sensitivity of an asset's return to the market's return:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\beta_i=\\dfrac{\\operatorname{Cov}(R_i,R_m)}{\\operatorname{Var}(R_m)}$$</div>\n<p>The market has $\\beta_m=1$. As a rule, a market move of $\\Delta R_m$ moves the asset by about $\\beta_i\\,\\Delta R_m$.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Beta</th>\n<th>Interpretation</th>\n<th>If the market moves $\\pm 10\\%$</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>$0.6$</td>\n<td>Defensive: less sensitive than the market</td>\n<td>about $\\pm 6\\%$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>$1.0$</td>\n<td>Moves with the market</td>\n<td>about $\\pm 10\\%$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>$1.4$</td>\n<td>Aggressive: amplifies market moves</td>\n<td>about $\\pm 14\\%$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Beta is not the same as standard deviation: it measures only the market-related part of risk.</p>\n<h4>Why a diversified investor focuses on systematic risk</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Unsystematic risk cancels out across many holdings, so it can be removed at almost no cost.</li>\n<li>Systematic risk remains, and it cannot be avoided by holding more securities.</li>\n<li>Under CAPM the market rewards only the risk that cannot be diversified away, so only beta earns a risk premium: $R_i=R_f+\\beta_i[E(R_m)-R_f]$.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Limitation:</strong> a beta is estimated from past data, is unstable over time and says nothing about total volatility or about a poorly diversified portfolio.</p>\n<p><strong>Conclusion:</strong> unsystematic risk can be diversified and is unrewarded, whereas beta captures the risk that remains and is priced.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question tut-q\" data-tut-ch=\"5\" data-tut-t=\"Concept\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q5</span><div><div class=\"tut-tags\"><span class=\"tut-topic\">Topics 3 and 4 · Q5</span><span class=\"tut-type tut-concept\">Concept</span></div><strong class=\"tut-title\">MPT, CAPM and the Security Market Line</strong></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body markdown-body compact\"><p>Explain the efficient frontier and the role of investor indifference curves in selecting an optimal portfolio. Then state the CAPM equation and explain what the Security Market Line shows. How does the SML differ from the efficient frontier?</p><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal the exam-ready answer</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><h4>Efficient frontier (Modern Portfolio Theory)</h4>\n<p>Plot every attainable portfolio with $\\sigma_p$ on the horizontal axis and $E(R_p)$ on the vertical axis.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The <strong>efficient frontier</strong> is the upper-left boundary: the portfolios with the highest expected return for each level of risk.</li>\n<li>Portfolios below or to the right of the frontier are inefficient, because another portfolio gives more return for the same risk or less risk for the same return.</li>\n<li>Portfolios outside the attainable set cannot be achieved with the available assets.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Indifference curves and the optimal portfolio <em>(beyond the chapter notes)</em></h4>\n<p>An <strong>indifference (utility) curve</strong> joins combinations of risk and return that give an investor the same satisfaction.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>A risk-averse investor needs more return for more risk, so the curves slope upward and curve upward as risk grows.</li>\n<li>A more risk-averse investor has steeper curves.</li>\n<li>Curves higher and to the left give greater satisfaction.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The <strong>optimal portfolio</strong> is the point where the <em>highest attainable</em> indifference curve just touches (is tangent to) the efficient frontier. Two investors share the same frontier but different curves, so they choose different optimal portfolios.</p>\n<h4>CAPM and the Security Market Line</h4>\n<p>The CAPM gives the required return for an asset's systematic risk:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$R_i=R_f+\\beta_i\\bigl[E(R_m)-R_f\\bigr]$$</div>\n<p>where $R_f$ is the risk-free rate and $E(R_m)-R_f$ is the market risk premium.</p>\n<p>The <strong>SML</strong> is the graph of this equation, with beta on the horizontal axis and required return on the vertical axis. It is a straight line with intercept $R_f$ and slope $E(R_m)-R_f$.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>A security on the line is fairly priced.</li>\n<li>A security <strong>above</strong> the line has an expected return higher than required (positive alpha, undervalued).</li>\n<li>A security <strong>below</strong> the line is overpriced.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>SML versus efficient frontier</h4>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th></th>\n<th>Efficient frontier</th>\n<th>SML</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Horizontal axis</td>\n<td>Standard deviation $\\sigma_p$ (total risk)</td>\n<td>Beta (systematic risk only)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Vertical axis</td>\n<td>Expected portfolio return</td>\n<td>Required return</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Applies to</td>\n<td>Portfolios</td>\n<td>Individual securities and portfolios</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Theory</td>\n<td>Portfolio optimisation</td>\n<td>CAPM</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Question answered</td>\n<td>Which combinations are efficient?</td>\n<td>Is the return adequate for the systematic risk?</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Limitation:</strong> both depend on estimated inputs and on CAPM assumptions, such as a single risk factor and a stable beta.</p>\n<p><strong>Conclusion:</strong> the frontier constructs efficient portfolios, indifference curves choose among them, and the SML judges whether a security's return pays for its beta.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question tut-q\" data-tut-ch=\"5\" data-tut-t=\"Calculation\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q6</span><div><div class=\"tut-tags\"><span class=\"tut-topic\">Topics 3 and 4 · Q6</span><span class=\"tut-type tut-calculation\">Calculation</span></div><strong class=\"tut-title\">Weighted portfolio return and beta</strong></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body markdown-body compact\"><p>A portfolio invests $40\\%$ in Share A, $35\\%$ in Share B and $25\\%$ in a sukuk fund. Their expected returns are $8\\%$, $12\\%$ and $5\\%$, while their betas are $0.70$, $1.20$ and $0.20$ respectively. Calculate the portfolio's expected return and beta. Interpret both results.</p><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal the exam-ready answer</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><h4>Given</h4>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Asset</th>\n<th>Weight $w_i$</th>\n<th>$E(R_i)$</th>\n<th>$\\beta_i$</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Share A</td>\n<td>$0.40$</td>\n<td>$8\\%$</td>\n<td>$0.70$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Share B</td>\n<td>$0.35$</td>\n<td>$12\\%$</td>\n<td>$1.20$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Sukuk fund</td>\n<td>$0.25$</td>\n<td>$5\\%$</td>\n<td>$0.20$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h4>Expected portfolio return</h4>\n<p><strong>Formula</strong></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$E(R_p)=\\sum_{i=1}^{3} w_i\\,E(R_i)$$</div>\n<p><strong>Substitution</strong></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$E(R_p)=0.40(8\\%)+0.35(12\\%)+0.25(5\\%)=3.20\\%+4.20\\%+1.25\\%$$</div>\n<p><strong>Result</strong></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{E(R_p)=8.65\\%}$$</div>\n<h4>Portfolio beta</h4>\n<p><strong>Formula</strong></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\beta_p=\\sum_{i=1}^{3} w_i\\,\\beta_i$$</div>\n<p><strong>Substitution</strong></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\beta_p=0.40(0.70)+0.35(1.20)+0.25(0.20)=0.28+0.42+0.05$$</div>\n<p><strong>Result</strong></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\beta_p=0.75}$$</div>\n<h4>Interpretation</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Return:</strong> the portfolio is expected to earn $8.65\\%$, between the lowest ($5\\%$) and highest ($12\\%$) asset returns, because return is a weighted average.</li>\n<li><strong>Beta:</strong> $\\beta_p=0.75&lt;1$, so the portfolio is a defensive one. If the market rises or falls by $10\\%$, the portfolio is expected to move about $7.5\\%$ in the same direction.</li>\n<li>Share B contributes the most to both return ($4.20\\%$ of $8.65\\%$) and beta ($0.42$ of $0.75$). The sukuk fund pulls the beta down.</li>\n<li>Beta measures market sensitivity only. It does not give the portfolio's total standard deviation.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Exam tip:</strong> use the weights, not a simple average. The unweighted average beta would be $0.70$, which is wrong; also do not use $\\beta_p$ to state total volatility.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question tut-q\" data-tut-ch=\"5\" data-tut-t=\"Calculation\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q7</span><div><div class=\"tut-tags\"><span class=\"tut-topic\">Topics 3 and 4 · Q7</span><span class=\"tut-type tut-calculation\">Calculation</span></div><strong class=\"tut-title\">Two-asset portfolio risk</strong></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body markdown-body compact\"><p>A portfolio invests $60\\%$ in Asset M and $40\\%$ in Asset N. Expected returns are $9\\%$ and $14\\%$, standard deviations are $12\\%$ and $18\\%$, and the correlation coefficient is $-0.25$. Calculate the expected portfolio return and standard deviation. Explain the source of the risk reduction.</p><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal the exam-ready answer</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><h4>Given</h4>\n<p>$w_M=0.60$, $w_N=0.40$, $E(R_M)=9\\%$, $E(R_N)=14\\%$, $\\sigma_M=12\\%$, $\\sigma_N=18\\%$, $\\rho_{MN}=-0.25$.</p>\n<h4>Expected portfolio return</h4>\n<p><strong>Formula</strong></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$E(R_p)=w_M\\,E(R_M)+w_N\\,E(R_N)$$</div>\n<p><strong>Substitution</strong></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$E(R_p)=0.60(9\\%)+0.40(14\\%)=5.40\\%+5.60\\%$$</div>\n<p><strong>Result</strong></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{E(R_p)=11.00\\%}$$</div>\n<h4>Portfolio standard deviation</h4>\n<p><strong>Formula</strong></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\sigma_p=\\sqrt{w_M^2\\sigma_M^2+w_N^2\\sigma_N^2+2\\,w_Mw_N\\,\\sigma_M\\sigma_N\\,\\rho_{MN}}$$</div>\n<p><strong>Substitution</strong> (working in percentage units, so the variance is in $\\%^2$)</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\begin{aligned} \\sigma_p^2&amp;=(0.60)^2(12)^2+(0.40)^2(18)^2+2(0.60)(0.40)(12)(18)(-0.25)\\\\ &amp;=51.84+51.84-25.92\\\\ &amp;=77.76 \\end{aligned}$$</div>\n<p><strong>Result</strong></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\sigma_p=\\sqrt{77.76}=8.82\\%}$$</div>\n<h4>Source of the risk reduction</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>The weighted average of the two standard deviations is $0.60(12\\%)+0.40(18\\%)=14.40\\%$, but the portfolio's $\\sigma_p$ is only $8.82\\%$.</li>\n<li>The cross term is $-25.92$ instead of $+103.68$ at $\\rho=+1$. Any $\\rho&lt;1$ drives the reduction (at $\\rho=0$, $\\sigma_p=10.18\\%$), and the negative sign adds to it. When one asset does badly, the other tends to do slightly better or less badly, so part of the fluctuations offset each other.</li>\n<li>The expected return of $11.00\\%$ is not reduced at all: diversification lowers risk without lowering expected return.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Exam tip:</strong> do not average the standard deviations, and remember the $2$ in front of the covariance term. Square the weights in the first two terms and keep the negative sign of $\\rho$.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question tut-q\" data-tut-ch=\"5\" data-tut-t=\"Calculation\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q8</span><div><div class=\"tut-tags\"><span class=\"tut-topic\">Topics 3 and 4 · Q8</span><span class=\"tut-type tut-calculation\">Calculation</span></div><strong class=\"tut-title\">Correlation scenarios and risk reduction</strong></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body markdown-body compact\"><p>Use the same weights and standard deviations as in Question 7. Calculate the portfolio standard deviation if the correlation between M and N is:</p>\n<p>(a) $+1$</p>\n<p>(b) $-1$</p>\n<p>Compare the results with Question 7 and explain why a correlation of $-1$ is difficult to sustain in practice.</p><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal the exam-ready answer</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><h4>Given</h4>\n<p>$w_M=0.60$, $w_N=0.40$, $\\sigma_M=12\\%$, $\\sigma_N=18\\%$. The first two variance terms are unchanged: $51.84+51.84=103.68$, and $2\\,w_Mw_N\\,\\sigma_M\\sigma_N=103.68$.</p>\n<p><strong>Formula</strong></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\sigma_p^2=w_M^2\\sigma_M^2+w_N^2\\sigma_N^2+2\\,w_Mw_N\\,\\sigma_M\\sigma_N\\,\\rho_{MN}$$</div>\n<h4>(a) $\\rho_{MN}=+1$</h4>\n<p><strong>Substitution</strong></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\sigma_p^2=51.84+51.84+103.68(1)=207.36$$</div>\n<p><strong>Result</strong></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\sigma_p=\\sqrt{207.36}=14.40\\%}$$</div>\n<p>This equals the weighted average $0.60(12\\%)+0.40(18\\%)$.</p>\n<h4>(b) $\\rho_{MN}=-1$</h4>\n<p><strong>Substitution</strong></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\sigma_p^2=51.84+51.84+103.68(-1)=0$$</div>\n<p><strong>Result</strong></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\sigma_p=0\\%}$$</div>\n<p>The weights are exactly hedging: $0.60(12\\%)=0.40(18\\%)=7.20\\%$, so the two risks cancel completely.</p>\n<h4>Comparison</h4>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>$\\rho_{MN}$</th>\n<th>$\\sigma_p$</th>\n<th>Position</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>$+1$</td>\n<td>$14.40\\%$</td>\n<td>Weighted average, no diversification benefit</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>$-0.25$ (Q7)</td>\n<td>$8.82\\%$</td>\n<td>Substantial reduction</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>$-1$</td>\n<td>$0.00\\%$</td>\n<td>Risk eliminated</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>As $\\rho$ falls, $\\sigma_p$ falls, with the same expected return of $11.00\\%$ in every case.</p>\n<h4>Why $\\rho=-1$ is hard to sustain</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Two different assets are exposed to some common economic factors (growth, interest rates, inflation), so a perfectly opposite relationship is almost never found.</li>\n<li>Correlations are estimates from past data. They change over time and tend to <strong>rise in crises</strong>.</li>\n<li>A riskless M+N mix earning $11\\%$ while $R_f$ is $3\\%$ would be an arbitrage; trading would change prices and correlations <em>(beyond the chapter notes)</em>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Exam tip:</strong> at $\\rho=+1$ the formula reduces to the weighted average, which is a useful check. At $\\rho=-1$ the result is the absolute value, $\\lvert w_M\\sigma_M-w_N\\sigma_N\\rvert$, and cannot be negative.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question tut-q\" data-tut-ch=\"5\" data-tut-t=\"Calculation\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q9</span><div><div class=\"tut-tags\"><span class=\"tut-topic\">Topics 3 and 4 · Q9</span><span class=\"tut-type tut-calculation\">Calculation</span></div><strong class=\"tut-title\">CAPM and relative attractiveness</strong></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body markdown-body compact\"><p>The risk-free rate is $3\\%$ and the expected market return is $9\\%$. Share P has a beta of $1.40$ and an analyst forecasts a return of $13\\%$. Share Q has a beta of $0.80$ and a forecast return of $7\\%$. Calculate the CAPM required return for each share. On CAPM assumptions, identify which share appears relatively attractive and explain the decision.</p><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal the exam-ready answer</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><h4>Given</h4>\n<p>$R_f=3\\%$, $E(R_m)=9\\%$, so the market risk premium is $E(R_m)-R_f=6\\%$.</p>\n<p><strong>Formula</strong></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$R_i=R_f+\\beta_i\\bigl[E(R_m)-R_f\\bigr]$$</div>\n<h4>Share P</h4>\n<p><strong>Substitution</strong></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$R_P=3\\%+1.40(9\\%-3\\%)=3\\%+8.40\\%$$</div>\n<p><strong>Result</strong></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{R_P=11.40\\%}$$</div>\n<h4>Share Q</h4>\n<p><strong>Substitution</strong></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$R_Q=3\\%+0.80(9\\%-3\\%)=3\\%+4.80\\%$$</div>\n<p><strong>Result</strong></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{R_Q=7.80\\%}$$</div>\n<h4>Alpha (forecast minus required return)</h4>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Share</th>\n<th>Forecast</th>\n<th>CAPM required</th>\n<th>$\\alpha=$ forecast $-$ required</th>\n<th>Position</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>P</td>\n<td>$13\\%$</td>\n<td>$11.40\\%$</td>\n<td>$+1.60\\%$</td>\n<td>Above the SML</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Q</td>\n<td>$7\\%$</td>\n<td>$7.80\\%$</td>\n<td>$-0.80\\%$</td>\n<td>Below the SML</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h4>Interpretation and decision</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Share P appears relatively attractive:</strong> its forecast return of $13\\%$ exceeds the $11.40\\%$ needed to compensate for its beta of $1.40$. It plots above the SML and looks undervalued, so buy (or hold).</li>\n<li><strong>Share Q appears unattractive:</strong> its forecast of $7\\%$ is below the $7.80\\%$ required for its beta, so it plots below the SML and looks overpriced. Avoid it, or sell if held.</li>\n<li>Q's lower beta does not rescue it: the comparison is return against <em>required</em> return for the risk, not low risk alone.</li>\n<li>This holds only on CAPM assumptions and if the analyst forecasts and betas are reliable.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Exam tip:</strong> beta multiplies the <strong>premium</strong> $E(R_m)-R_f$, not the market return. Using $3\\%+1.40\\times 9\\%$ is the most common error.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question tut-q\" data-tut-ch=\"5 2 4 12\" data-tut-t=\"Calculation\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q10</span><div><div class=\"tut-tags\"><span class=\"tut-topic\">Topics 3 and 4 · Q10</span><span class=\"tut-type tut-calculation\">Calculation</span><span class=\"tut-also\">Also Ch 2</span><span class=\"tut-also\">Also Ch 4</span><span class=\"tut-also\">Also Ch 12</span></div><strong class=\"tut-title\">Integrated portfolio review</strong></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body markdown-body compact\"><p>A Malaysian investor holds $50\\%$ in a Bursa Malaysia equity fund with beta $1.20$, $30\\%$ in a global equity fund with beta $0.90$, and $20\\%$ in a sukuk fund with beta $0.20$. The portfolio beta is above the investor's new target of about $0.75$. Evaluate a proposal to change the weights to $30\\%$, $30\\%$ and $40\\%$ respectively. Calculate the new beta and discuss expected return, diversification, currency risk, liquidity and information needed before implementation.</p><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal the exam-ready answer</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><h4>Given</h4>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Fund</th>\n<th>Current weight</th>\n<th>Proposed weight</th>\n<th>$\\beta_i$</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Bursa Malaysia equity</td>\n<td>$50\\%$</td>\n<td>$30\\%$</td>\n<td>$1.20$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Global equity</td>\n<td>$30\\%$</td>\n<td>$30\\%$</td>\n<td>$0.90$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Sukuk</td>\n<td>$20\\%$</td>\n<td>$40\\%$</td>\n<td>$0.20$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Formula</strong></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\beta_p=\\sum_i w_i\\,\\beta_i$$</div>\n<h4>Current beta</h4>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\beta_p=0.50(1.20)+0.30(0.90)+0.20(0.20)=0.60+0.27+0.04=\\boxed{0.91}$$</div>\n<h4>New beta</h4>\n<p><strong>Substitution</strong></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\beta_p^{\\text{new}}=0.30(1.20)+0.30(0.90)+0.40(0.20)=0.36+0.27+0.08$$</div>\n<p><strong>Result</strong></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\beta_p^{\\text{new}}=0.71}$$</div>\n<p>The new beta of $0.71$ is below the current $0.91$ and slightly below the target of about $0.75$ (a shortfall of $0.04$). The change is effective, and it slightly overshoots. A mix such as $35\\%$, $30\\%$, $35\\%$ gives $0.76$.</p>\n<h4>Evaluation</h4>\n<p><strong>Expected return.</strong> The proposal moves $20\\%$ of wealth from a beta-$1.20$ fund to a beta-$0.20$ fund. Under CAPM, a lower beta lowers the required (and expected) return. As an illustration using Question 9's inputs ($R_f=3\\%$, $E(R_m)=9\\%$):</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$R_{\\text{current}}=3\\%+0.91(6\\%)=8.46\\%,\\qquad R_{\\text{new}}=3\\%+0.71(6\\%)=7.26\\%$$</div>\n<p>So expected return falls by about $1.20\\%$ a year. The investor accepts lower return for lower market risk, which suits the new target. This figure is illustrative, and the funds' own return forecasts are needed.</p>\n<p><strong>Diversification.</strong> Sukuk usually has a low correlation with equities, so the move may reduce total risk. But beta captures only market sensitivity, not total risk, and the equity share of the portfolio falls from $80\\%$ to $60\\%$. The two equity funds become equally weighted ($30\\%$ each), which is more balanced than $50\\%$ and $30\\%$. If the sukuk fund is Malaysian (an assumption), domestic exposure stays at $70\\%$ ($50\\%+20\\%$ before, $30\\%+40\\%$ after); only the asset mix changes, from equity to sukuk.</p>\n<p><strong>Currency risk.</strong> The global fund stays at $30\\%$, so the <em>amount</em> of foreign-currency exposure is unchanged. Its share of risk may rise because the domestic equity fund shrinks. Check whether the global fund hedges the ringgit.</p>\n<p><strong>Liquidity.</strong> Unit-trust funds can usually be redeemed, but check redemption time, exit fees and sales charges, and the liquidity of the sukuk fund's holdings (credit quality and bid-ask spreads). Switching costs reduce the benefit.</p>\n<p><strong>Information needed before implementation</strong>\n- expected returns, standard deviations and correlations of the three funds;\n- how reliable each beta is (estimation period, benchmark, stability);\n- fund fees, switching costs, taxes and the investor's horizon and income needs;\n- the duration and credit quality of the sukuk fund;\n- why the target is $0.75$, and the investor's capacity for loss.</p>\n<p><strong>Recommendation:</strong> the proposal reduces beta to $0.71$, near the target, and is reasonable, provided costs, the sukuk fund's quality and the investor's return needs are checked first.</p>\n<p><strong>Exam tip:</strong> do not stop after calculating the beta. Marks are also given for linking the result to the return, diversification, currency, liquidity and information points.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question tut-q\" data-tut-ch=\"5\" data-tut-t=\"Reflection\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">R1</span><div><div class=\"tut-tags\"><span class=\"tut-topic\">Topics 3 and 4 · Reflection 1</span><span class=\"tut-type tut-reflection\">Reflection</span></div><strong class=\"tut-title\">Number of assets vs co-movement</strong></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body markdown-body compact\"><p>Which matters more for diversification: the number of assets or the way their returns move together?</p><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal the exam-ready answer</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><p>The way their returns move together matters more. Portfolio variance depends on the correlation (covariance) term, so adding assets helps only if they are not highly correlated with each other. Forty shares from the same banking sector all react to the same interest-rate and credit shocks, so they add little protection. A few assets with low or negative correlation can lower risk more than many assets that move together. Number still helps up to a point, since unsystematic risk falls as holdings rise, but it levels off at the market (systematic) risk.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question tut-q\" data-tut-ch=\"5\" data-tut-t=\"Reflection\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">R2</span><div><div class=\"tut-tags\"><span class=\"tut-topic\">Topics 3 and 4 · Reflection 2</span><span class=\"tut-type tut-reflection\">Reflection</span></div><strong class=\"tut-title\">Low volatility but unsuitable</strong></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body markdown-body compact\"><p>Why can a portfolio with low total volatility still be unsuitable for a particular investor?</p><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal the exam-ready answer</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><p>Volatility is only one dimension of suitability. A low-volatility portfolio, such as one mostly in a sukuk or money-market fund, may not earn enough return to meet a long-term goal like retirement, or to beat inflation, so it exposes the investor to purchasing-power risk. It may also fail on the investor's liquidity needs, tax position, income requirement or time horizon. The efficient frontier shows efficient choices, but the investor's own objectives and risk tolerance decide which one fits. A portfolio can therefore be efficient and still not be the right one for this investor.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question tut-q\" data-tut-ch=\"5\" data-tut-t=\"Reflection\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">R3</span><div><div class=\"tut-tags\"><span class=\"tut-topic\">Topics 3 and 4 · Reflection 3</span><span class=\"tut-type tut-reflection\">Reflection</span></div><strong class=\"tut-title\">Greatest uncertainty in Question 9</strong></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body markdown-body compact\"><p>Which CAPM assumption or input creates the greatest uncertainty in Question 9?</p><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal the exam-ready answer</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><p>The expected market return, and so the market risk premium, together with the estimated betas, create the greatest uncertainty. Both are estimated from past data and neither is observed directly. The decision is sensitive to them: if $E(R_m)$ were $11\\%$ instead of $9\\%$, the premium would be $8\\%$ and Share P's required return would be $3\\%+1.40(8\\%)=14.20\\%$, above its $13\\%$ forecast. The decision would flip from attractive to overpriced. The analyst's return forecasts and the assumption that beta is the only priced risk also add uncertainty, so the conclusion should be treated as tentative.</p></div></details></div></details></div></div><div class=\"tut-group theme-l6\" data-tut-group=\"6\"><h3 class=\"tut-group-title\"><span>Chapter 6</span> Common Stocks <small>7 questions</small></h3><div class=\"question-bank\"><details class=\"bank-question tut-q\" data-tut-ch=\"6 2\" data-tut-t=\"Concept\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q1</span><div><div class=\"tut-tags\"><span class=\"tut-topic\">Topic 5 · Q1</span><span class=\"tut-type tut-concept\">Concept</span><span class=\"tut-also\">Also Ch 2</span></div><strong class=\"tut-title\">Common stock ownership and investment appeal</strong></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body markdown-body compact\"><p>Explain why common stock represents an ownership claim. Discuss the principal benefits and risks of common stock investment. Distinguish a public offering, a rights offering and classified common stock, and explain why each matters to an investor.</p><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal the exam-ready answer</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><h4>1. Why common stock is an ownership claim</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>A <strong>common share</strong> is an ownership interest in a corporation. If a company has $1{,}000$ shares outstanding, holding $10$ shares means owning $1\\%$ of the equity.</li>\n<li>The claim is <strong>residual</strong>: creditors and other senior claimants are paid first, and common shareholders own what is left.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Residual amount for common owners} = \\text{amount available} - \\text{senior claims}$$</div>\n<ul>\n<li>Example: if $\\text{RM}1$ million remains after all senior claims and there are $100{,}000$ shares, each share has a residual claim of $\\text{RM}10$. If nothing remains, the claim pays nothing.</li>\n<li>Ownership gives legal rights (usually voting on certain matters and receiving <em>declared</em> dividends). It does <strong>not</strong> entitle the holder to a share of the company's cash today: cash leaves the corporation only through a dividend, a buyback or another authorised transaction. Limited liability caps the loss on fully paid shares at the amount invested.</li>\n</ul>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th></th>\n<th>Creditor</th>\n<th>Common shareholder</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Relationship</td>\n<td>Lender</td>\n<td>Residual owner</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Payment</td>\n<td>Contractual (subject to default)</td>\n<td>Dividends if declared, plus price change</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Priority</td>\n<td>Ahead of equity</td>\n<td>After senior claims</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Upside</td>\n<td>Normally capped at contractual payments</td>\n<td>Shares in business growth, not guaranteed</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h4>2. Benefits and risks</h4>\n<p><strong>Benefits</strong>\n- <strong>Two sources of return:</strong> cash dividends and capital gain.</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$HPR = \\dfrac{D + (P_1 - P_0)}{P_0}$$</div>\n<ul>\n<li>Long-run growth potential and participation in business success.</li>\n<li>Liquidity for listed shares, and a wide choice of companies, sectors and strategies (income, growth, capital accumulation).</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Risks</strong>\n- No guaranteed dividends and no guaranteed return; a dividend does not ensure a positive total return.\n- <strong>Residual position:</strong> the shareholder is paid last, so equity can fall sharply when the business fails.\n- Market, inflation, financing and company-specific risks; company-specific risk can be diversified, market risk cannot.\n- Liquidity differs between shares, and liquidity does not prevent a price loss.</p>\n<h4>3. Public offering, rights offering and classified stock</h4>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Item</th>\n<th>What it is</th>\n<th>Why it matters to an investor</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Public offering</td>\n<td>Shares sold to the investing public (a primary issue creates new shares, so the proceeds go to the company; a secondary sale may simply pay existing holders)</td>\n<td>Shows where the money goes (Chapter 2: primary versus secondary), and new shares can increase the number of shares outstanding</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Rights offering</td>\n<td>Existing shareholders are offered the chance to buy new shares, usually in proportion to current holdings and on stated terms</td>\n<td>Subscribing preserves ownership percentage; not subscribing risks <strong>dilution</strong> (for example $100/1{,}000 = 10\\%$ falls to $100/1{,}200 = 8.33\\%$ after $200$ new shares)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Classified common stock</td>\n<td>Share classes with different votes, dividends or other rights</td>\n<td>Same label can carry very different control and cash rights: $1{,}000$ shares with ten votes each control more votes than $5{,}000$ one-vote shares</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<ul>\n<li>A rights offering does not oblige the holder to buy, and whether <em>value</em> is diluted depends on the issue price, whether rights are tradable and how the proceeds are used.</li>\n<li>For classified stock, read the company's terms; a label alone does not tell the rights.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Conclusion:</strong> a share is a residual ownership claim that can produce dividends and capital gains, but it carries the risk of being paid last, and the way shares are issued and classified determines how much of the company and its control an investor really holds.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question tut-q\" data-tut-ch=\"6\" data-tut-t=\"Concept\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q2</span><div><div class=\"tut-tags\"><span class=\"tut-topic\">Topic 5 · Q2</span><span class=\"tut-type tut-concept\">Concept</span></div><strong class=\"tut-title\">Common stock values and market information</strong></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body markdown-body compact\"><p>Distinguish par value, book value, market value and investment value. Define market capitalisation. Why can market value differ substantially from book value, and which value is most directly relevant to an investor deciding the maximum price to pay?</p><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal the exam-ready answer</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><h4>1. Four meanings of value</h4>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Term</th>\n<th>What it measures</th>\n<th>What it does <strong>not</strong> establish</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Par value</td>\n<td>Stated nominal amount in the company's share documents</td>\n<td>Fair price or likely return</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Book value</td>\n<td>Accounting common equity (assets less liabilities and preferred claims); <strong>per share</strong> it is common equity divided by shares outstanding</td>\n<td>Current market value of the assets or liquidation proceeds</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Market value</td>\n<td>Price at which the share trades now</td>\n<td>That every investor believes the price is justified</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Investment (intrinsic) value</td>\n<td>An analyst's estimate of what the share is worth, given expected future benefits and their risk</td>\n<td>A certain or universally agreed number</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Example: common equity of $\\text{RM}12$ million and $3$ million shares give a book value of $\\text{RM}4$ per share.</p>\n<h4>2. Market capitalisation</h4>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Market capitalisation} = \\text{current share price} \\times \\text{shares outstanding}$$</div>\n<ul>\n<li>It is the market value of the <strong>equity of the whole company</strong>, not the price of one share.</li>\n<li>Company A at $\\text{RM}5$ with $100$ million shares is worth $\\text{RM}500$ million; Company B at $\\text{RM}50$ with $2$ million shares is worth $\\text{RM}100$ million. B has the more expensive share, A the larger company.</li>\n<li>It is not enterprise value: debt is not included.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>3. Why market value can differ from book value</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Book value records historical cost and accounting rules; the market prices <strong>expected future earnings</strong> and risk.</li>\n<li>Intangibles (brands, technology, human capital) and growth prospects are often missing from the balance sheet, so a profitable, growing firm can trade well above book.</li>\n<li>Assets may be worth more or less than their recorded cost, and investors' required returns and sentiment change over time.</li>\n<li>A weak firm can trade below book value because the market expects poor returns on its assets.</li>\n<li>A premium or discount to book value alone does not prove mispricing.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>4. Which value for the maximum price to pay?</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Investment (intrinsic) value</strong> is the most directly relevant. The market price is what the investor <em>would pay today</em>; investment value is what the share is <em>worth</em> to that investor given the cash flows expected and the risk. The maximum price should not exceed the investment value.</li>\n<li>A share is attractive only if the estimated value exceeds the price, and that estimate depends on assumptions that must be tested (Chapters 7 and 8).</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Conclusion:</strong> par and book value are accounting figures, market value is today's price, and investment value, an estimate of worth, is the benchmark for the highest price an investor should pay.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question tut-q\" data-tut-ch=\"6\" data-tut-t=\"Concept\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q3</span><div><div class=\"tut-tags\"><span class=\"tut-topic\">Topic 5 · Q3</span><span class=\"tut-type tut-concept\">Concept</span></div><strong class=\"tut-title\">Stock splits, repurchases and dividends</strong></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body markdown-body compact\"><p>Explain the economic effects of a stock split and a share repurchase. Distinguish cash dividends from stock dividends and explain the date of record, ex-dividend date and payment date. Why might a company retain earnings rather than distribute them?</p><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal the exam-ready answer</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><h4>1. Stock split</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>A $k$-for-$1$ split multiplies shares by $k$ and divides the price by about $k$.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$N_{\\text{new}} = k \\times N_{\\text{old}},\\qquad P_{\\text{new}} \\approx \\dfrac{P_{\\text{old}}}{k}$$</div>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Economic effect:</strong> none by itself. The holding value, ownership percentage and the company's value are unchanged: splitting a cake changes the number of pieces, not the amount of cake. Per-share earnings and dividends fall proportionally.</li>\n<li>Why it matters: a lower price can improve marketability (for example for small investors), and the actual price may move because of the signal the split sends, but the split itself is not a gain. A reverse split does the opposite.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>2. Share repurchase (buyback)</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>The company buys its own shares, reducing shares outstanding (retired or held as treasury stock).</li>\n<li><strong>Effects:</strong> continuing holders own a larger percentage of the remaining shares, and EPS can rise because there are fewer shares, provided net income is not cut much by the interest lost on the cash spent (or paid on new debt).</li>\n<li><strong>Caution:</strong> the buyback uses cash or borrowing. A larger share of a smaller or more indebted company is not automatically more wealth. The result depends on the price paid, financing, the opportunity cost of the cash and future performance.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>3. Cash dividends versus stock dividends</h4>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th></th>\n<th>Cash dividend</th>\n<th>Stock dividend</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Form</td>\n<td>Cash paid to shareholders</td>\n<td>Extra shares (for example $10\\%$ gives $10$ new shares per $100$ held)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Company cash</td>\n<td>Leaves the company</td>\n<td>Stays in the company</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Shareholder wealth</td>\n<td>Receives cash; the share price tends to fall by about that amount on the ex-date</td>\n<td>More shares at a proportionally lower adjusted price; total wealth is not raised by $10\\%$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Ownership percentage</td>\n<td>Unchanged</td>\n<td>Unchanged</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>A stock dividend is similar to a small split: more pieces, same business.</p>\n<h4>4. The dividend dates</h4>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Declaration date:</strong> the board announces the dividend and its terms.</li>\n<li><strong>Ex-dividend date:</strong> a buyer on or after this date does <strong>not</strong> receive the announced dividend; the seller keeps it. The share price typically adjusts down roughly by the dividend.</li>\n<li><strong>Date of record:</strong> the company checks its register to find who is entitled. The ex-date is set before the record date to allow for settlement <em>(beyond the chapter notes)</em>: with $T+2$ settlement it is normally one market day earlier, but the gap depends on market rules.</li>\n<li><strong>Payment date:</strong> the dividend is actually paid.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>The order of the dates is: declaration, ex-dividend, record, payment.</p>\n<h4>5. Why retain earnings?</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Fund growth:</strong> projects with returns above the shareholders' required return create more value inside the firm than the same cash paid out.</li>\n<li><strong>Reduce debt</strong> or build a cash cushion in uncertain periods.</li>\n<li><strong>Flexibility and stability:</strong> cutting a dividend later is costly, so firms avoid committing to payments they may not sustain.</li>\n<li>Retained earnings are an economic claim of shareholders, not an immediate payment. They help only if reinvested at a good return, otherwise paying out may be better.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Conclusion:</strong> splits and stock dividends change the number of shares, not value; buybacks change ownership shares but spend cash; and dividends move cash out only on the dates stated, while retention is sensible only when the firm can reinvest it profitably.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question tut-q\" data-tut-ch=\"6\" data-tut-t=\"Concept\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q4</span><div><div class=\"tut-tags\"><span class=\"tut-topic\">Topic 5 · Q4</span><span class=\"tut-type tut-concept\">Concept</span></div><strong class=\"tut-title\">Types of common stocks and investment strategies</strong></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body markdown-body compact\"><p>Compare blue-chip, income, growth, speculative, cyclical, defensive and small-cap stocks. Relate these categories to buy-and-hold, current-income and short-term speculative strategies. Why must the investor's objective and risk capacity guide the choice?</p><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal the exam-ready answer</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><h4>1. Categories compared</h4>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Category</th>\n<th>Main idea</th>\n<th>Caution</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Blue chip</td>\n<td>Established business with a strong operating record</td>\n<td>A record supports confidence but does not remove risk</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Income</td>\n<td>Relatively large, regular cash dividends</td>\n<td>Judge sustainability and total return, not yield alone</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Growth</td>\n<td>Higher expected growth in earnings or operations; little paid as dividends</td>\n<td>Price already contains the expectation; disappointing growth hurts</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Speculative</td>\n<td>Highly uncertain prospects</td>\n<td>Large upside and large downside together</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Cyclical</td>\n<td>Earnings sensitive to the economic cycle</td>\n<td>Timing is hard; earnings swing widely</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Defensive</td>\n<td>Demand and earnings less sensitive to downturns</td>\n<td>Not immune to price falls</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Small cap</td>\n<td>Small market capitalisation (slides: under USD$2$ billion small, USD$2$ to $10$ billion mid, over USD$10$ billion large; illustrative only)</td>\n<td>Can bring lower liquidity and higher volatility, but small does not mean superior</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>These labels overlap (a share can be large, growth and cyclical at once) and are a starting hypothesis, not a replacement for analysis.</p>\n<h4>2. Relating categories to strategies</h4>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Strategy</th>\n<th>Typical fit</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Buy-and-hold (long-term quality growth)</td>\n<td>Blue chip and growth shares; low trading, but not a guarantee and not conservative if the portfolio is undiversified</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Current income</td>\n<td>Income and defensive shares; check dividend reliability, concentration and inflation</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Short-term speculation</td>\n<td>Speculative, cyclical and some small-cap shares; high return potential, high risk and heavy trading costs</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h4>3. Why objective and risk capacity must guide the choice</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Objective:</strong> preserve purchasing power, accumulate capital or earn income; each points to a different mix.</li>\n<li><strong>Risk capacity:</strong> the ability to bear a loss without harming one's goals (horizon, income, other assets), distinct from willingness to take risk.</li>\n<li>Example: Investor A needs cash in six months; even a high-quality dividend share may fall or cut its dividend at the wrong time, so shares may not fit. Investor B has a long horizon and a diversified portfolio and can place part in growth shares.</li>\n<li>Choosing by label or by past return alone ignores the person who bears the risk.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Conclusion:</strong> the stock categories describe sources of return and risk, the strategies are ways to pursue goals, and only the investor's objective and capacity to lose decide which combination is suitable.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question tut-q\" data-tut-ch=\"6\" data-tut-t=\"Calculation\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q6</span><div><div class=\"tut-tags\"><span class=\"tut-topic\">Topic 5 · Q6</span><span class=\"tut-type tut-calculation\">Calculation</span></div><strong class=\"tut-title\">Stock split and shareholder wealth</strong></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body markdown-body compact\"><p>A shareholder owns $800$ shares priced at $\\text{RM}18.00$ each. The company announces a $2$-for-$1$ stock split. Before the split, earnings per share are $\\text{RM}1.20$ and dividends per share are $\\text{RM}0.60$. Assuming no new information or market reaction, calculate the shareholder's shares, theoretical price per share, total position value, adjusted earnings per share and adjusted dividends per share after the split. Interpret the result.</p><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal the exam-ready answer</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><h4>Given</h4>\n<p>$N_{\\text{old}} = 800$, $P_{\\text{old}} = \\text{RM}18.00$, $k = 2$, $EPS_{\\text{old}} = \\text{RM}1.20$, $DPS_{\\text{old}} = \\text{RM}0.60$.</p>\n<h4>Formulas</h4>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\begin{aligned}\nN_{\\text{new}} &amp;= k \\times N_{\\text{old}}, &amp; P_{\\text{new}} &amp;\\approx \\dfrac{P_{\\text{old}}}{k}\\\\\nEPS_{\\text{new}} &amp;= \\dfrac{EPS_{\\text{old}}}{k}, &amp; DPS_{\\text{new}} &amp;= \\dfrac{DPS_{\\text{old}}}{k}\n\\end{aligned}$$</div>\n<h4>Calculation</h4>\n<p>Shares after the split:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$N_{\\text{new}} = 2 \\times 800 = \\boxed{1{,}600\\ \\text{shares}}$$</div>\n<p>Theoretical price:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$P_{\\text{new}} = \\dfrac{18.00}{2} = \\boxed{\\text{RM}9.00}$$</div>\n<p>Total position value:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\begin{aligned}\n\\text{Before} &amp;= 800 \\times 18.00 = \\text{RM}14{,}400\\\\\n\\text{After} &amp;= 1{,}600 \\times 9.00 = \\boxed{\\text{RM}14{,}400}\n\\end{aligned}$$</div>\n<p>Adjusted per-share figures:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$EPS_{\\text{new}} = \\dfrac{1.20}{2} = \\boxed{\\text{RM}0.60},\\qquad DPS_{\\text{new}} = \\dfrac{0.60}{2} = \\boxed{\\text{RM}0.30}$$</div>\n<h4>Interpretation</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>The investor holds twice as many shares, each worth half as much, so the position is unchanged at $\\text{RM}14{,}400$. Ownership percentage is also unchanged.</li>\n<li>Total earnings and dividends received are the same: $800 \\times 1.20 = 1{,}600 \\times 0.60 = \\text{RM}960$ of earnings attributable and $800 \\times 0.60 = 1{,}600 \\times 0.30 = \\text{RM}480$ of dividends.</li>\n<li>Valuation ratios are unchanged: $P/E = 18.00/1.20 = 9.00/0.60 = 15$, and the dividend yield stays $0.60/18.00 = 0.30/9.00 = 3.33\\%$.</li>\n<li>A split is cosmetic: more pieces of the same cake. The real market price may move afterwards because of the signal the split sends or greater marketability, but that is a market reaction, not part of the split.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Exam tip:</strong> the common mistake is to treat the split as a gain or to leave EPS and DPS unadjusted, so that the P/E and yield seem to change; shares go up, and price, EPS and DPS all go down by the same factor.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question tut-q\" data-tut-ch=\"6 7\" data-tut-t=\"Calculation\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q7</span><div><div class=\"tut-tags\"><span class=\"tut-topic\">Topic 5 · Q7</span><span class=\"tut-type tut-calculation\">Calculation</span><span class=\"tut-also\">Also Ch 7</span></div><strong class=\"tut-title\">Dividend and market ratios</strong></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body markdown-body compact\"><p>A Bursa Malaysia share trades at $\\text{RM}4.80$. Its earnings per share are $\\text{RM}0.40$ and annual dividends per share are $\\text{RM}0.20$. Calculate the dividend yield, dividend payout ratio and price-to-earnings ratio. If an investor buys the share on its ex-dividend date, is the buyer entitled to the dividend that has just been declared? Explain.</p><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal the exam-ready answer</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><h4>Given</h4>\n<p>$P = \\text{RM}4.80$, $EPS = \\text{RM}0.40$, $DPS = \\text{RM}0.20$.</p>\n<h4>Dividend yield</h4>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Dividend yield} = \\dfrac{DPS}{P} = \\dfrac{0.20}{4.80} = \\boxed{4.17\\%}$$</div>\n<h4>Dividend payout ratio</h4>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Payout ratio} = \\dfrac{DPS}{EPS} = \\dfrac{0.20}{0.40} = \\boxed{50\\%}$$</div>\n<p>The retention ratio is $1 - 0.50 = 50\\%$.</p>\n<h4>Price-to-earnings ratio</h4>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$P/E = \\dfrac{P}{EPS} = \\dfrac{4.80}{0.40} = \\boxed{12\\ \\text{times}}$$</div>\n<h4>Interpretation</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Yield $4.17\\%$:</strong> each ringgit invested at today's price produces about $\\text{RM}0.0417$ of annual cash dividend. It is not the total return, because the capital gain or loss is excluded.</li>\n<li><strong>Payout $50\\%$:</strong> half of earnings is paid out and half retained for reinvestment; the dividend is covered twice by earnings, which suggests it is not stretched (cash flow and debt still need checking).</li>\n<li><strong>P/E $12$:</strong> investors pay $\\text{RM}12$ for each $\\text{RM}1$ of current earnings. Whether that is high or low depends on growth, risk and peers (Chapter 7).</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Ex-dividend date</h4>\n<p><strong>No.</strong> On or after the ex-dividend date a new buyer does <strong>not</strong> receive the declared dividend; it belongs to the seller, who was the owner before the ex-date. The buyer is entitled only if the purchase is made <em>before</em> the ex-date, so that the buyer is on the register at the date of record. The share price normally falls by about the dividend on the ex-date, so a buyer before the ex-date is not getting free money, because the price already contains the right to the dividend.</p>\n<p><strong>Exam tip:</strong> the yield uses the <strong>price</strong> in the denominator and the payout ratio uses <strong>EPS</strong>; swapping them ($0.20/0.40$ for the yield) is the standard error. Also do not state that buying on the ex-date entitles you to the dividend.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question tut-q\" data-tut-ch=\"6\" data-tut-t=\"Calculation\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q8</span><div><div class=\"tut-tags\"><span class=\"tut-topic\">Topic 5 · Q8</span><span class=\"tut-type tut-calculation\">Calculation</span></div><strong class=\"tut-title\">Foreign share return in ringgit</strong></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body markdown-body compact\"><p>A Malaysian investor buys a foreign share for $\\text{USD}40$ when the exchange rate is $\\text{RM}4.20$ per $\\text{USD}$. One year later, the investor receives a $\\text{USD}1$ dividend and sells the share for $\\text{USD}44$ when the exchange rate is $\\text{RM}4.00$ per $\\text{USD}$. Calculate the total holding-period return in US dollars and in ringgit. Explain how the currency movement affected the investor.</p><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal the exam-ready answer</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><h4>Given</h4>\n<p>$P_0 = \\text{USD}40$, $P_1 = \\text{USD}44$, $D = \\text{USD}1$, $S_0 = \\text{RM}4.20$ per $\\text{USD}$, $S_1 = \\text{RM}4.00$ per $\\text{USD}$. Assume the dividend is converted at the year-end rate $S_1$, and ignore costs and taxes.</p>\n<h4>Return in US dollars</h4>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$R_{\\text{USD}} = \\dfrac{D + (P_1 - P_0)}{P_0} = \\dfrac{1 + (44 - 40)}{40} = \\dfrac{5}{40} = \\boxed{12.5\\%}$$</div>\n<p>This is a $10\\%$ capital gain plus a $2.5\\%$ dividend yield.</p>\n<h4>Return in ringgit</h4>\n<p>Convert every cash flow into ringgit (the year-end proceeds are the sale price plus the dividend, $44 + 1 = 45$ US dollars):</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\begin{aligned}\n\\text{Cost} &amp;= 40 \\times 4.20 = \\text{RM}168.00\\\\\n\\text{Proceeds} &amp;= 45 \\times 4.00 = \\text{RM}180.00\n\\end{aligned}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$R_{\\text{RM}} = \\dfrac{180 - 168}{168} = \\dfrac{12}{168} = \\boxed{7.14\\%}$$</div>\n<h4>Check with the compounding formula</h4>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$R_{\\text{currency}} = \\dfrac{S_1}{S_0} - 1 = \\dfrac{4.00}{4.20} - 1 = -4.76\\%$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$1 + R_{\\text{RM}} = (1 + R_{\\text{USD}})(1 + R_{\\text{currency}}) = 1.125 \\times 0.9524 \\approx 1.0714 \\Rightarrow 7.14\\%$$</div>\n<h4>Interpretation</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>The share gave a $12.5\\%$ return in its own currency, but the investor measures wealth in ringgit and received only $7.14\\%$.</li>\n<li>The US dollar <strong>weakened</strong> from $\\text{RM}4.20$ to $\\text{RM}4.00$, a $4.76\\%$ fall. Converting at the old rate would have given $45 \\times 4.20 = \\text{RM}189.00$, a $12.5\\%$ ringgit return; the currency fall cost $189 - 180 = \\text{RM}9.00$ per share, which is the difference.</li>\n<li>The ringgit return is not $12.5\\% - 4.76\\%$ exactly: the two effects compound ($12.5\\% \\times (-4.76\\%)$ is the interaction), so the approximation $R_{\\text{local}} + R_{\\text{currency}} = 7.74\\%$ is slightly high.</li>\n<li>Foreign investment therefore adds currency risk on top of the share's own risk. A good local return can become a poor or negative home-currency return, and a strengthening US dollar would have helped.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Exam tip:</strong> define the quote direction ($\\text{RM}$ per $\\text{USD}$) before using it, convert the dividend and the sale price at the <strong>end</strong> rate and the cost at the <strong>start</strong> rate, and do not add the two returns.</p></div></details></div></details></div></div><div class=\"tut-group theme-l7\" data-tut-group=\"7\"><h3 class=\"tut-group-title\"><span>Chapter 7</span> Analyzing Common Stocks <small>6 questions</small></h3><div class=\"question-bank\"><details class=\"bank-question tut-q\" data-tut-ch=\"7\" data-tut-t=\"Concept\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q5</span><div><div class=\"tut-tags\"><span class=\"tut-topic\">Topic 5 · Q5</span><span class=\"tut-type tut-concept\">Concept</span></div><strong class=\"tut-title\">Security analysis and financial evidence</strong></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body markdown-body compact\"><p>Explain the top-down sequence of economic, industry and company analysis. Describe the roles of the balance sheet, income statement and statement of cash flows. How do financial ratios assist investors, and why should ratios be compared across time, competitors and industry standards?</p><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal the exam-ready answer</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><h4>1. Top-down analysis</h4>\n<div class=\"math-flow\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Economy → Industry → Company → Financial evidence → Forecasts and valuation\"><span>Economy</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Industry</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Company</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Financial evidence</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Forecasts and valuation</span></div>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Stage</th>\n<th>Question</th>\n<th>Examples of evidence</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Economy</td>\n<td>Which macro forces move demand, costs and financing?</td>\n<td>GDP, interest rates, inflation, exchange rates, fiscal and monetary policy</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Industry</td>\n<td>Which industries gain or lose, and why?</td>\n<td>Competition, regulation, technology, life cycle (development, expansion, maturity, decline)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Company</td>\n<td>Can <strong>this</strong> firm turn those forces into profit and cash?</td>\n<td>Business model, management, financial statements, ratios</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The same event affects firms differently (higher fuel prices hurt an airline but may help an energy producer), so each step must be linked to a mechanism. The purpose is to estimate intrinsic value and compare it with the price.</p>\n<h4>2. The three statements</h4>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Statement</th>\n<th>Shows</th>\n<th>Role</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Balance sheet</td>\n<td>Assets, liabilities and equity <strong>at a date</strong> ($\\text{Assets} = \\text{Liabilities} + \\text{Equity}$)</td>\n<td>Financial position: liquidity, leverage, resources</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Income statement</td>\n<td>Revenue and expenses <strong>over a period</strong></td>\n<td>Profitability: net income, margins, EPS</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Cash-flow statement</td>\n<td>Operating, investing and financing cash movements</td>\n<td>Whether profit becomes cash; ability to pay bills and fund growth</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>A firm can report profit but weak operating cash if customers have not paid or inventory has grown, so the statements must be read together.</p>\n<h4>3. How ratios help</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>A ratio makes a relationship visible: liquidity (current ratio), activity (turnovers), leverage and coverage (debt-equity, times interest earned), profitability (margin, ROA, ROE) and shareholder ratios (EPS, P/E, PEG, P/B).</li>\n<li>They scale figures so firms of different size can be compared and highlight where to investigate.</li>\n<li>A ratio is a question, not a verdict: a rise can have more than one explanation.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>4. Why compare across time, competitors and industry standards</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Over time:</strong> shows direction (improving or deteriorating) and breaks in trend.</li>\n<li><strong>Against competitors:</strong> shows relative position, because strategy and market conditions are shared.</li>\n<li><strong>Against industry standards:</strong> a normal ratio differs by business model (a retailer and a utility have different working-capital and leverage norms).</li>\n<li>Use the same definition each time (year-end versus average balances; which debt measure), otherwise differences are artefacts.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Limitation and conclusion:</strong> ratios use historical accounting figures that can be distorted by accounting choices, seasonality or one-off items, so they inform but do not replace judgement. Good analysis moves from economy to industry to company, uses all three statements, and judges ratios against time, peers and the industry.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question tut-q\" data-tut-ch=\"7\" data-tut-t=\"Calculation\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q9</span><div><div class=\"tut-tags\"><span class=\"tut-topic\">Topic 5 · Q9</span><span class=\"tut-type tut-calculation\">Calculation</span></div><strong class=\"tut-title\">Integrated financial ratio analysis</strong></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body markdown-body compact\"><p>A company reports current assets $\\text{RM}600$ million, inventory $\\text{RM}200$ million, current liabilities $\\text{RM}300$ million, total assets $\\text{RM}1{,}500$ million, total debt $\\text{RM}600$ million, shareholders' equity $\\text{RM}900$ million, sales $\\text{RM}1{,}800$ million, EBIT $\\text{RM}180$ million, interest expense $\\text{RM}40$ million and net income $\\text{RM}90$ million. Calculate the current ratio, quick ratio, debt-to-equity ratio, times interest earned, net profit margin, total asset turnover, return on assets and return on equity. Verify ROE using the three-part DuPont relationship and interpret the company's position.</p><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal the exam-ready answer</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><h4>Given (RM million)</h4>\n<p>$CA = 600$, $\\text{Inventory} = 200$, $CL = 300$, $TA = 1{,}500$, $\\text{Debt} = 600$, $E = 900$, $\\text{Sales} = 1{,}800$, $EBIT = 180$, $\\text{Interest} = 40$, $NI = 90$. All balances are year-end figures, as in the chapter slides.</p>\n<h4>Liquidity</h4>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Current ratio} = \\dfrac{CA}{CL} = \\dfrac{600}{300} = \\boxed{2.00\\ \\text{times}}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Quick ratio} = \\dfrac{CA - \\text{Inventory}}{CL} = \\dfrac{600 - 200}{300} = \\boxed{1.33\\ \\text{times}}$$</div>\n<p><em>(beyond the chapter notes: the quick ratio is an extension)</em>.</p>\n<h4>Leverage and coverage</h4>\n<p>The course defines debt-equity with long-term debt; here the question gives <strong>total debt</strong>, so that figure is used.</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\dfrac{\\text{Debt}}{\\text{Equity}} = \\dfrac{600}{900} = \\boxed{0.67\\ \\text{times}}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Times interest earned} = \\dfrac{EBIT}{\\text{Interest}} = \\dfrac{180}{40} = \\boxed{4.50\\ \\text{times}}$$</div>\n<h4>Profitability and efficiency</h4>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Net profit margin} = \\dfrac{NI}{\\text{Sales}} = \\dfrac{90}{1{,}800} = \\boxed{5.00\\%}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Total asset turnover} = \\dfrac{\\text{Sales}}{TA} = \\dfrac{1{,}800}{1{,}500} = \\boxed{1.20\\ \\text{times}}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$ROA = \\dfrac{NI}{TA} = \\dfrac{90}{1{,}500} = \\boxed{6.00\\%},\\qquad ROE = \\dfrac{NI}{E} = \\dfrac{90}{900} = \\boxed{10.00\\%}$$</div>\n<h4>DuPont verification</h4>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$ROE = \\underbrace{\\dfrac{NI}{\\text{Sales}}}_{\\text{margin}} \\times \\underbrace{\\dfrac{\\text{Sales}}{TA}}_{\\text{turnover}} \\times \\underbrace{\\dfrac{TA}{E}}_{\\text{equity multiplier}}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\begin{aligned}\nROE &amp;= 5.00\\% \\times 1.20 \\times \\dfrac{1{,}500}{900}\\\\\n&amp;= 5.00\\% \\times 1.20 \\times 1.6667 = \\boxed{10.00\\%}\n\\end{aligned}$$</div>\n<p>This matches $90/900$. Also $ROA = 5.00\\% \\times 1.20 = 6.00\\%$ and $ROE = ROA \\times 1.6667 = 10.00\\%$.</p>\n<h4>Interpretation</h4>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Area</th>\n<th>Reading</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Liquidity</td>\n<td>Current ratio $2.00$ and quick ratio $1.33$ show that even without inventory the firm has $\\text{RM}1.33$ of liquid assets for each $\\text{RM}1$ of current liabilities; short-term obligations look comfortably covered</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Leverage</td>\n<td>Debt is $\\text{RM}0.67$ for each $\\text{RM}1$ of equity ($40\\%$ of assets), and EBIT covers interest $4.5$ times, so debt is moderate and serviceable</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Profitability</td>\n<td>A net margin of only $5\\%$ is thin; ROA of $6\\%$ is modest</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Efficiency</td>\n<td>Turnover of $1.20$ means each ringgit of assets generates $\\text{RM}1.20$ of sales</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Return to owners</td>\n<td>Leverage (equity multiplier $1.67$) lifts ROE from the $6\\%$ ROA to $10\\%$, a moderate boost; the low base comes from the thin $5\\%$ margin</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Overall:</strong> a financially safe, liquid and moderately geared firm whose weakness is thin profitability. Improvement must come from margin or asset efficiency.</li>\n<li>The ratios need comparison with past years and peers, and the cash-flow statement should confirm that profit is turning into cash. High safety does not make it a good investment without considering the price.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Exam tip:</strong> the DuPont chain must use the same profit, sales, asset and equity bases so that the factors cancel; quick ratio excludes inventory from the numerator only, and times interest earned uses EBIT, not net income.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question tut-q\" data-tut-ch=\"7 6\" data-tut-t=\"Calculation\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q10</span><div><div class=\"tut-tags\"><span class=\"tut-topic\">Topic 5 · Q10</span><span class=\"tut-type tut-calculation\">Calculation</span><span class=\"tut-also\">Also Ch 6</span></div><strong class=\"tut-title\">Comparing two common stocks</strong></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body markdown-body compact\"><p>Share A trades at $\\text{RM}6.00$ with EPS of $\\text{RM}0.50$, expected EPS growth of $10\\%$, book value per share of $\\text{RM}3.00$ and annual dividends per share of $\\text{RM}0.20$. Share B trades at $\\text{RM}4.50$ with EPS of $\\text{RM}0.30$, expected EPS growth of $15\\%$, book value per share of $\\text{RM}3.00$ and annual dividends per share of $\\text{RM}0.09$. Calculate P/E, PEG, price-to-book, dividend yield and payout ratio for both. Evaluate which appears more suitable for an income-oriented investor and which may appeal to a growth-oriented investor. State the additional evidence needed.</p><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal the exam-ready answer</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><h4>Formulas</h4>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\begin{aligned}\nP/E &amp;= \\dfrac{P}{EPS}\\\\\nPEG &amp;= \\dfrac{P/E}{g\\ (\\text{in percent})}\\\\\nP/B &amp;= \\dfrac{P}{BVPS}\\\\\n\\text{Yield} &amp;= \\dfrac{DPS}{P}\\\\\n\\text{Payout} &amp;= \\dfrac{DPS}{EPS}\n\\end{aligned}$$</div>\n<h4>Share A</h4>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\begin{aligned}\nP/E &amp;= \\dfrac{6.00}{0.50} = 12.0\\\\\nPEG &amp;= \\dfrac{12.0}{10} = 1.20\\\\\nP/B &amp;= \\dfrac{6.00}{3.00} = 2.00\\\\\n\\text{Yield} &amp;= \\dfrac{0.20}{6.00} = 3.33\\%\\\\\n\\text{Payout} &amp;= \\dfrac{0.20}{0.50} = 40\\%\n\\end{aligned}$$</div>\n<h4>Share B</h4>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\begin{aligned}\nP/E &amp;= \\dfrac{4.50}{0.30} = 15.0\\\\\nPEG &amp;= \\dfrac{15.0}{15} = 1.00\\\\\nP/B &amp;= \\dfrac{4.50}{3.00} = 1.50\\\\\n\\text{Yield} &amp;= \\dfrac{0.09}{4.50} = 2.00\\%\\\\\n\\text{Payout} &amp;= \\dfrac{0.09}{0.30} = 30\\%\n\\end{aligned}$$</div>\n<h4>Summary</h4>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Measure</th>\n<th class=\"ta-r\">Share A</th>\n<th class=\"ta-r\">Share B</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>P/E</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">$12.0$</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">$15.0$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>PEG</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">$1.20$</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">$1.00$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>P/B</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">$2.00$</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">$1.50$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Dividend yield</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">$3.33\\%$</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">$2.00\\%$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Payout ratio</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">$40\\%$</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">$30\\%$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Expected EPS growth</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">$10\\%$</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">$15\\%$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h4>Evaluation</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Income-oriented investor: Share A.</strong> It has the higher yield ($3.33\\%$ against $2.00\\%$) and the higher payout ($40\\%$ against $30\\%$), so more of earnings becomes cash. Its lower P/E also means a lower price for each ringgit of earnings. A yield of $3.33\\%$ is still modest, so A is only the better of two, not a high-income share.</li>\n<li><strong>Growth-oriented investor: Share B.</strong> Expected growth is higher ($15\\%$ against $10\\%$), it retains more earnings ($70\\%$ against $60\\%$), and its PEG of $1.00$ is lower than A's $1.20$, so B's higher P/E ($15.0$) is justified by faster growth. It also has a lower P/B ($1.50$ against $2.00$). Under the slide rule of thumb ($PEG$ around $1$), B looks fairly priced for its growth and A slightly fully valued.</li>\n<li>A low P/E, P/B or PEG does not prove undervaluation.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Additional evidence needed</h4>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Reliability of the growth forecasts:</strong> B's $15\\%$ is higher than A's, so how credible are they, and for how long (the PEG is only as good as the growth rate)?</li>\n<li><strong>Profitability and ROE:</strong> EPS divided by book value gives about $16.7\\%$ for A and $10\\%$ for B. A earns more on its equity, which supports its higher P/B. Growth that can be financed internally is roughly $ROE \\times \\text{retention}$ (beyond the chapter notes): $16.7\\% \\times 0.60 = 10\\%$ for A, but only $10\\% \\times 0.70 = 7\\%$ for B, so B's forecast needs outside capital or a higher ROE.</li>\n<li><strong>Cash flow and dividend sustainability:</strong> does free cash flow cover the dividends, and what is the trend in dividends?</li>\n<li><strong>Debt and coverage:</strong> leverage, interest cover and liquidity.</li>\n<li><strong>Risk:</strong> earnings volatility, cyclicality, beta, and industry position and competitors.</li>\n<li><strong>Peers and history:</strong> compare these ratios with industry averages and each company's own past; also fees, taxes and the portfolio fit of each share.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Conclusion:</strong> A suits income seekers and B appeals to growth seekers, but the choice must wait for evidence that B's growth is achievable and A's dividend is sustainable.</p>\n<p><strong>Exam tip:</strong> PEG uses the growth rate as a percentage figure ($10$, not $0.10$), and P/B divides by book value <strong>per share</strong>. A common mistake is to call the share with the lower P/E automatically cheaper without considering growth and risk.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question tut-q\" data-tut-ch=\"7 8\" data-tut-t=\"Reflection\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">R1</span><div><div class=\"tut-tags\"><span class=\"tut-topic\">Topic 5 · Reflection 1</span><span class=\"tut-type tut-reflection\">Reflection</span><span class=\"tut-also\">Also Ch 8</span></div><strong class=\"tut-title\">Measure most vulnerable to unrealistic expectations</strong></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body markdown-body compact\"><p>Which common-stock measure is most vulnerable to unrealistic investor expectations?</p><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal the exam-ready answer</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><p>The <strong>market price</strong>, and the multiples built on it, are the most vulnerable to unrealistic expectations, with <strong>P/E</strong> and <strong>PEG</strong> the clearest cases. The price is what investors will pay for expected future earnings, so over-optimistic growth lifts the price (and the P/E) while current earnings stay unchanged, and a disappointment reverses it sharply. The PEG is even more exposed because its denominator, the expected growth rate, is a forecast: a small error in the growth estimate changes the answer a great deal. Dividend yield is also affected by price, but book value and EPS are historical accounting figures and are less dependent on sentiment. A high multiple is therefore evidence of what investors expect, not proof that it will be delivered.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question tut-q\" data-tut-ch=\"7\" data-tut-t=\"Reflection\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">R2</span><div><div class=\"tut-tags\"><span class=\"tut-topic\">Topic 5 · Reflection 2</span><span class=\"tut-type tut-reflection\">Reflection</span></div><strong class=\"tut-title\">Profit but financial distress</strong></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body markdown-body compact\"><p>Why can a company report profit but still face financial distress?</p><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal the exam-ready answer</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><p>Profit is an accounting measure and is not the same as cash. Revenue is recognised when earned even if customers have not paid, so receivables can rise while cash does not arrive; growth in inventory also absorbs cash. If the firm has short-term liabilities, loan instalments or interest to pay, it can run out of cash although the income statement shows a profit. High debt makes this worse because interest and principal are fixed commitments and covenants or refinancing may fail when cash is weak. Weak liquidity ratios, falling receivables and inventory turnover, and operating cash flow below net income are the warning signs. This is why the cash-flow statement and the balance sheet must be read together with the income statement.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question tut-q\" data-tut-ch=\"7 8\" data-tut-t=\"Reflection\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">R3</span><div><div class=\"tut-tags\"><span class=\"tut-topic\">Topic 5 · Reflection 3</span><span class=\"tut-type tut-reflection\">Reflection</span><span class=\"tut-also\">Also Ch 8</span></div><strong class=\"tut-title\">Evidence for a more reliable recommendation in Question 10</strong></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body markdown-body compact\"><p>What evidence would make your recommendation in Question 10 more reliable?</p><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal the exam-ready answer</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><p>The recommendation rests on one year of ratios and two growth forecasts, so it needs more evidence. First, the quality of the forecasts: analyst estimates, the company's history of meeting guidance, and the drivers of the $10\\%$ and $15\\%$ growth. Second, profitability and efficiency (ROE and its DuPont components), to test whether the growth can be financed from retained earnings. Third, cash flow and balance-sheet evidence: free cash flow cover for the dividend, debt, interest cover and liquidity. Fourth, risk and context: volatility, cyclicality, the industry's position and peer and historical ratios, plus an intrinsic-value estimate (Chapter 8) to compare with the price. Finally, the investor's own objective, horizon and risk capacity should decide which share is suitable.</p></div></details></div></details></div></div><div class=\"tut-group theme-l8\" data-tut-group=\"8\"><h3 class=\"tut-group-title\"><span>Chapter 8</span> Stock Valuation <small>13 questions</small></h3><div class=\"question-bank\"><details class=\"bank-question tut-q\" data-tut-ch=\"8 7\" data-tut-t=\"Concept\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q1</span><div><div class=\"tut-tags\"><span class=\"tut-topic\">Topic 6 · Q1</span><span class=\"tut-type tut-concept\">Concept</span><span class=\"tut-also\">Also Ch 7</span></div><strong class=\"tut-title\">The future and the valuation forecast</strong></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body markdown-body compact\"><p>Why does a company's future matter more than its past when valuing common stock? Explain how an investor can move from historical information to forecasts of sales, profit, earnings per share, dividends and future share price. Identify the main assumptions at each stage.</p><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal the exam-ready answer</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><h4>Why the future matters more than the past</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>A share is a <strong>claim on future benefits</strong>: dividends and the price at which it can later be sold. Last year's profit has already happened; a buyer today acquires a claim to future dividends and a future sale price.</li>\n<li><strong>Intrinsic value</strong> is the present value of those <em>expected</em> future benefits, discounted at the required return. History is <em>evidence</em> for forecasting, not a guarantee that it will repeat.</li>\n<li>Extending a past surge forever overstates value; a past loss does not by itself prove that future cash flows will be poor.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>From history to a share-price forecast (the forecast chain)</h4>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Stage</th>\n<th>How it is forecast</th>\n<th>Main assumption</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Sales</td>\n<td>$\\text{Sales}_1 = \\text{Sales}_0(1+g_s)$</td>\n<td>Demand, prices, market share and capacity support the growth rate $g_s$; the trend is not just extended naively</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Net profit</td>\n<td>$\\text{Sales}_1 \\times \\text{net margin}$</td>\n<td>Costs, competition and operating leverage keep the margin at the assumed level</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>EPS</td>\n<td>$\\text{Net profit} \\div \\text{shares}$</td>\n<td>Share count unchanged (no dilution or buy-backs); no preferred dividends</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Dividend</td>\n<td>$D_1 = EPS_1 \\times \\text{payout ratio}$</td>\n<td>Dividend policy stays stable; the retained portion is reinvested productively</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Future price</td>\n<td>$P_1 = EPS_1 \\times (P/E)_1$</td>\n<td>The P/E at the sale date reflects the growth, risk and interest rates then; this is the least certain input</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The historical analysis in Chapter 7 (ratios, margins, growth, industry and economic conditions) supplies the starting values at each stage.</p>\n<h4>Evaluation</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Errors <strong>compound</strong> along the chain: a small error in sales growth or margin feeds into EPS, the dividend and the price.</li>\n<li>$D_1$ and $P_1$ arrive at year-end, so they must be discounted at the required return before they are compared with today's price.</li>\n<li>Use base, optimistic and pessimistic scenarios instead of one point estimate.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Conclusion:</strong> value is created by future cash flows, so a sound valuation starts from history but is only as reliable as the explicit assumptions behind each forecast step.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question tut-q\" data-tut-ch=\"8\" data-tut-t=\"Concept\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q2</span><div><div class=\"tut-tags\"><span class=\"tut-topic\">Topic 6 · Q2</span><span class=\"tut-type tut-concept\">Concept</span></div><strong class=\"tut-title\">Intrinsic value and required return</strong></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body markdown-body compact\"><p>Distinguish market price from intrinsic value and explain the required rate of return. State the two decision tests involving expected return and intrinsic value. Why can two rational investors estimate different intrinsic values for the same share?</p><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal the exam-ready answer</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><h4>Definitions</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Market price ($P_0$):</strong> the price observed today, set by trading between buyers and sellers. It is a fact.</li>\n<li><strong>Intrinsic value ($V_0$):</strong> an investor's <em>estimate</em> of what the share is worth, the present value of expected future benefits. It depends on forecasts and is not observable.</li>\n<li><strong>Required return ($k$):</strong> the return an investor demands for bearing the share's risk and for waiting. It is the discount rate and the hurdle. Under CAPM:</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$k = r_f + \\beta\\,[E(R_m) - r_f]$$</div>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Expected return ($E(R)$):</strong> the return the investor <em>forecasts</em> from buying at the current price.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$E(R) = \\dfrac{D_1 + (P_1 - P_0)}{P_0}$$</div>\n<h4>The two decision tests</h4>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Test</th>\n<th>Compare</th>\n<th>Decision</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Return test</td>\n<td>$E(R)$ with $k$</td>\n<td>$E(R) &gt; k$: attractive (buy); $E(R) &lt; k$: unattractive</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Value test</td>\n<td>$V_0$ with $P_0$</td>\n<td>$V_0 &gt; P_0$: undervalued (buy); $V_0 &lt; P_0$: overvalued</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The two tests are algebraically equivalent: $E(R) &gt; k$ exactly when $\\dfrac{D_1 + P_1}{1+k} &gt; P_0$. If they disagree, there is an arithmetic or timing error.</p>\n<h4>Why rational investors disagree</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Different forecasts</strong> of sales, margins, dividends, growth rate and terminal price.</li>\n<li><strong>Different required returns</strong>, because of different beta estimates, risk-free rate, market premium, or personal risk tolerance and tax position.</li>\n<li><strong>Different time horizons and models</strong> (constant growth versus multi-stage, P/E versus dividends).</li>\n<li><strong>Different information and judgement</strong> about the business.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Intrinsic value is therefore a <em>range</em>, not a single number, and a small change in $k$ or $g$ can change it materially.</p>\n<p><strong>Conclusion:</strong> buy only when the share's expected return exceeds the required return, or equivalently when the investor's own intrinsic value exceeds the market price, and remember that both depend on assumptions.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question tut-q\" data-tut-ch=\"8\" data-tut-t=\"Concept\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q3</span><div><div class=\"tut-tags\"><span class=\"tut-topic\">Topic 6 · Q3</span><span class=\"tut-type tut-concept\">Concept</span></div><strong class=\"tut-title\">Dividend valuation models</strong></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body markdown-body compact\"><p>Compare the zero-growth, constant-growth and variable-growth dividend valuation models. Explain their assumptions, the types of companies for which each model is most suitable, and why the condition required return greater than growth rate is essential in the constant-growth model.</p><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal the exam-ready answer</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><h4>Starting point</h4>\n<p>All three are special cases of the <strong>general dividend model</strong>, where $D_t$ is the dividend at the end of year $t$:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$V_0 = \\sum_{t=1}^{\\infty}\\dfrac{E(D_t)}{(1+k)^t}$$</div>\n<h4>Comparison</h4>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th></th>\n<th>Zero growth</th>\n<th>Constant growth (Gordon)</th>\n<th>Variable (two-stage) growth</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Formula</td>\n<td>$V_0 = \\tfrac{D}{k}$</td>\n<td>$V_0 = \\tfrac{D_1}{k-g}$, with $D_1 = D_0(1+g)$</td>\n<td>Discount each high-growth dividend, add the discounted terminal value $P_n$ (formula below)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Assumption</td>\n<td>A level dividend forever, first payment one year away</td>\n<td>One steady growth rate $g$ forever</td>\n<td>Unusual growth $g_1$ for $n$ years, then stable growth $g_2$ forever</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Suited to</td>\n<td>Preference-share-like, very mature or regulated firms with a flat payout</td>\n<td>Mature, stable dividend payers (utilities, established banks)</td>\n<td>Growth firms moving towards maturity</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Main risk</td>\n<td>The dividend may not stay flat</td>\n<td>$V_0$ is very sensitive to $g$ and $k$</td>\n<td>The terminal value dominates the result</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Variable-growth (two-stage) value, with the terminal value dated at the transition year $n$:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$V_0 = \\sum_{t=1}^{n}\\dfrac{D_t}{(1+k)^t} + \\dfrac{P_n}{(1+k)^n}, \\qquad P_n = \\dfrac{D_{n+1}}{k-g_2}$$</div>\n<h4>Why $k &gt; g$ is essential</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Each year's dividend is multiplied by $\\tfrac{1+g}{1+k}$ in present-value terms. The infinite series converges only if that ratio is below one, that is, if $k &gt; g$.</li>\n<li>If $k \\le g$, the denominator is zero or negative. This does <strong>not</strong> mean the share is worth a negative or infinite amount; it means the perpetual constant-growth model is <strong>inappropriate</strong>, and a multi-stage model should be used instead.</li>\n<li>No firm can grow faster than the economy for ever, so a long-run $g$ must be modest.</li>\n<li>As $k - g$ becomes small, $V_0$ becomes extremely sensitive to small changes in either rate.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Evaluation</h4>\n<p>In the two-stage model, discount the high-growth dividends one by one and put the terminal value at the transition date. Check the timing and that nothing is counted twice.</p>\n<p><strong>Conclusion:</strong> choose the model that matches the dividend pattern, and treat the $k &gt; g$ condition as a validity test of the model, not just an algebraic detail.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question tut-q\" data-tut-ch=\"8 7\" data-tut-t=\"Concept\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q4</span><div><div class=\"tut-tags\"><span class=\"tut-topic\">Topic 6 · Q4</span><span class=\"tut-type tut-concept\">Concept</span><span class=\"tut-also\">Also Ch 7</span></div><strong class=\"tut-title\">P/E multiples and relative valuation</strong></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body markdown-body compact\"><p>Explain how expected growth, market conditions, financial leverage, inflation and dividend policy can affect a company's P/E ratio. Define a relative P/E multiple. Why can a high P/E or a rising market P/E be misleading if interpreted without examining earnings and risk?</p><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal the exam-ready answer</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><h4>Definition</h4>\n<p>The <strong>price-earnings ratio</strong> is $P/E = \\dfrac{P_0}{EPS}$: how many ringgit investors pay for each ringgit of earnings. A price from a forecast is $P = EPS \\times P/E$.</p>\n<h4>What drives a company's P/E <em>(the drivers are beyond the chapter notes)</em></h4>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Factor</th>\n<th>Effect on the P/E</th>\n<th>Reason</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Expected growth</td>\n<td>Higher growth, higher P/E</td>\n<td>Investors pay now for larger future earnings and dividends</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Market conditions</td>\n<td>Optimism and low interest rates raise it</td>\n<td>A lower required return $k$ raises value per unit of earnings</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Financial leverage</td>\n<td>Higher debt, usually lower P/E</td>\n<td>Greater financial risk raises $k$ and makes earnings less certain</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Inflation</td>\n<td>High or volatile inflation lowers it</td>\n<td>It raises interest rates and $k$, and distorts reported earnings</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Dividend policy</td>\n<td>Higher payout, higher P/E if growth is unchanged</td>\n<td>Cash is paid sooner; but a high payout with weak growth can mean few investment opportunities</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Under constant growth, $\\dfrac{P_0}{E_1} = \\dfrac{\\text{payout}}{k-g}$ links the P/E to payout, growth and required return.</p>\n<h4>Relative P/E <em>(beyond the chapter notes)</em></h4>\n<p>The <strong>relative P/E</strong> divides the company's P/E by the market's (or an industry's) P/E:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Relative P/E} = \\dfrac{(P/E)_{\\text{company}}}{(P/E)_{\\text{market}}}$$</div>\n<p>A value above $1$ means the share trades at a premium to the market, and below $1$ at a discount. Compare it with the company's own history.</p>\n<h4>Why a high or rising P/E can mislead</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Depressed earnings:</strong> a small $EPS$ in the denominator produces a high P/E even though nothing has improved; negative or near-zero $EPS$ makes it meaningless.</li>\n<li><strong>One-off items</strong> and accounting choices distort $EPS$.</li>\n<li>A high P/E may reflect <strong>high expected growth, low risk or simply an expensive price</strong>; the ratio alone cannot tell them apart.</li>\n<li>A <strong>rising market P/E</strong> may reflect falling interest rates and optimism, or only a market ahead of earnings, so it is not proof that shares are cheap or dear.</li>\n<li>P/E ignores risk and balance-sheet differences between firms.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Conclusion:</strong> use P/E only against comparable firms, the company's own history and the market, and read it together with earnings quality, growth and risk.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question tut-q\" data-tut-ch=\"8\" data-tut-t=\"Concept\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q5</span><div><div class=\"tut-tags\"><span class=\"tut-topic\">Topic 6 · Q5</span><span class=\"tut-type tut-concept\">Concept</span></div><strong class=\"tut-title\">Alternative valuation approaches</strong></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body markdown-body compact\"><p>Compare the dividend valuation model, free cash flow to equity approach, P/E approach and the price-to-cash-flow, price-to-sales and price-to-book procedures. What does each method use as its main value driver, and why should an analyst cross-check a valuation with more than one method?</p><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal the exam-ready answer</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><h4>Comparison</h4>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Method</th>\n<th>Main value driver</th>\n<th>Best used when</th>\n<th>Key weakness</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Dividend valuation model</td>\n<td>Expected dividends, discounted at $k$</td>\n<td>Stable dividend payers</td>\n<td>Little use for firms paying no dividend; very sensitive to $g$ and $k$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Free cash flow to equity <em>(beyond the chapter notes)</em></td>\n<td>Cash left for shareholders after operating needs, capital expenditure and net borrowing, discounted at $k$</td>\n<td>Low or no dividends, but positive cash generation</td>\n<td>Needs detailed forecasts; capital expenditure is hard to estimate</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>P/E</td>\n<td>$EPS \\times$ chosen P/E</td>\n<td>Profitable firms with comparable peers</td>\n<td>Distorted by temporary earnings; ignores risk differences</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Price to cash flow (P/CF)</td>\n<td>Cash flow per share $\\times$ P/CF</td>\n<td>Earnings distorted by depreciation or accounting choices</td>\n<td>The definition of cash flow must match across firms</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Price to sales (P/S)</td>\n<td>Sales per share $\\times$ P/S</td>\n<td>Earnings are negative or erratic</td>\n<td>Sales say nothing about margins or profit</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Price to book (P/B)</td>\n<td>Book value per share $\\times$ P/B</td>\n<td>Asset-heavy firms such as banks</td>\n<td>Book value can differ widely from economic value, particularly with intangibles</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Free cash flow to equity</strong> is, in textbook form, $FCFE = \\text{net income} + \\text{depreciation} - \\text{capital expenditure} - \\Delta\\text{working capital} + \\text{net borrowing}$, and the equity value is the present value of expected FCFE.</p>\n<p>The first two are <strong>intrinsic</strong> methods (cash flows discounted at $k$); the multiples are <strong>relative</strong> methods (price compared with a benchmark).</p>\n<h4>Why cross-check</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Every method rests on different assumptions, so each has different blind spots.</li>\n<li>Agreement gives confidence; disagreement reveals which assumption is driving the answer.</li>\n<li>Agreement is only independent confirmation if the methods do <strong>not</strong> share the same optimistic forecast.</li>\n<li>Use multiples to test a discounted-cash-flow answer, and discounted cash flows to test whether a multiple is justified.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Conclusion:</strong> choose the method that fits the business, then confirm it with at least one independent approach before acting.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question tut-q\" data-tut-ch=\"8\" data-tut-t=\"Calculation\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q6</span><div><div class=\"tut-tags\"><span class=\"tut-topic\">Topic 6 · Q6</span><span class=\"tut-type tut-calculation\">Calculation</span></div><strong class=\"tut-title\">From operating forecast to expected return</strong></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body markdown-body compact\"><p>A Bursa Malaysia company expects next year's sales to be $\\text{RM}500$ million and its net profit margin to be $6\\%$. It expects $100$ million shares outstanding, a dividend payout ratio of $40\\%$ and a year-end P/E of $14$. The share currently trades at $\\text{RM}3.50$. Forecast next year's net earnings, EPS, dividend per share and year-end share price. Calculate the expected one-year holding-period return, assuming the dividend is received at year-end.</p><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal the exam-ready answer</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><h4>Part 1: Forecast chain</h4>\n<p><strong>Given:</strong> sales $=\\text{RM}500$ million, margin $=6\\%$, shares $=100$ million, payout $=40\\%$, year-end $P/E = 14$, $P_0 = \\text{RM}3.50$.</p>\n<p><strong>Net earnings</strong></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Net profit} = \\text{sales} \\times \\text{net margin} = 500 \\times 0.06 = \\boxed{\\text{RM}30\\text{ million}}$$</div>\n<p><strong>EPS</strong></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$EPS_1 = \\dfrac{\\text{net profit}}{\\text{shares}} = \\dfrac{30}{100} = \\boxed{\\text{RM}0.30}$$</div>\n<p><strong>Dividend per share</strong></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$D_1 = EPS_1 \\times \\text{payout} = 0.30 \\times 0.40 = \\boxed{\\text{RM}0.12}$$</div>\n<p><strong>Year-end price</strong></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$P_1 = EPS_1 \\times (P/E)_1 = 0.30 \\times 14 = \\boxed{\\text{RM}4.20}$$</div>\n<h4>Part 2: Expected one-year holding-period return</h4>\n<p><strong>Formula</strong></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$E(R) = \\dfrac{D_1 + (P_1 - P_0)}{P_0}$$</div>\n<p><strong>Substitution</strong></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$E(R) = \\dfrac{0.12 + (4.20 - 3.50)}{3.50} = \\dfrac{0.82}{3.50} = \\boxed{23.43\\%}$$</div>\n<p>This splits into a dividend yield of $\\tfrac{0.12}{3.50} = 3.43\\%$ and a capital gain of $\\tfrac{0.70}{3.50} = 20.00\\%$.</p>\n<h4>Interpretation</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>The expected return is about $23.4\\%$, and most of it ($20.00\\%$ of the $23.43\\%$) comes from the price rising, which depends on the <strong>year-end P/E of $14$</strong>.</li>\n<li>The share now trades on a P/E of $\\tfrac{3.50}{0.30} = 11.67$ (today's price divided by forecast $EPS_1$, a forward multiple). The whole $20.00\\%$ capital gain comes from the multiple on the same $EPS_1$ rising from $11.67$ to the year-end $14$; no earnings growth beyond the forecast is involved.</li>\n<li>To judge whether this is attractive, compare $23.43\\%$ with the required return $k$ (for example from CAPM). It is a forecast, not a promise.</li>\n<li>If the year-end P/E were only $11.67$, the price would stay at $\\text{RM}3.50$ and the return would fall to the dividend yield of $3.43\\%$. The P/E assumption is the key risk.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Exam tip:</strong> the common mistake is to apply the P/E to <strong>this year's</strong> EPS, or to forget that $D_1$ and $P_1$ both arrive at year-end and must be compared with $P_0$ on the same date. Also divide the profit in millions by the shares in millions.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question tut-q\" data-tut-ch=\"8 5\" data-tut-t=\"Calculation\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q7</span><div><div class=\"tut-tags\"><span class=\"tut-topic\">Topic 6 · Q7</span><span class=\"tut-type tut-calculation\">Calculation</span><span class=\"tut-also\">Also Ch 5</span></div><strong class=\"tut-title\">Required return and investment test</strong></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body markdown-body compact\"><p>The risk-free rate is $3\\%$, the expected market return is $9\\%$ and a share has a beta of $1.20$. Calculate its required return using CAPM. The share costs $\\text{RM}3.40$ today and is expected to pay a $\\text{RM}0.18$ dividend and sell for $\\text{RM}3.85$ in one year. Calculate its expected return and evaluate it using both the return test and the intrinsic-value logic.</p><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal the exam-ready answer</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><h4>Part 1: Required return (CAPM)</h4>\n<p><strong>Given:</strong> $r_f = 3\\%$, $E(R_m) = 9\\%$, $\\beta = 1.20$.</p>\n<p><strong>Formula</strong></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$k = r_f + \\beta\\,[E(R_m) - r_f]$$</div>\n<p><strong>Substitution</strong></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$k = 3\\% + 1.20\\,(9\\% - 3\\%) = 3\\% + 7.2\\% = \\boxed{10.20\\%}$$</div>\n<p>The market risk premium is $6\\%$; beta multiplies the <strong>premium</strong>, not the market return.</p>\n<h4>Part 2: Expected return</h4>\n<p><strong>Given:</strong> $P_0 = \\text{RM}3.40$, $D_1 = \\text{RM}0.18$, $P_1 = \\text{RM}3.85$.</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$E(R) = \\dfrac{D_1 + (P_1 - P_0)}{P_0} = \\dfrac{0.18 + (3.85 - 3.40)}{3.40} = \\dfrac{0.63}{3.40} = \\boxed{18.53\\%}$$</div>\n<p>This is a dividend yield of $5.29\\%$ plus a capital gain of $13.24\\%$.</p>\n<h4>Part 3: Evaluation</h4>\n<p><strong>Return test</strong></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$E(R) = 18.53\\% &gt; k = 10.20\\%$$</div>\n<p>The expected return exceeds the hurdle by about $8.33$ percentage points, so the share is attractive.</p>\n<p><strong>Intrinsic-value test</strong> (discount the payoff at $k$)</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$V_0 = \\dfrac{D_1 + P_1}{1+k} = \\dfrac{0.18 + 3.85}{1.102} = \\dfrac{4.03}{1.102} = \\boxed{\\text{RM}3.66}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$V_0 = \\text{RM}3.66 &gt; P_0 = \\text{RM}3.40$$</div>\n<p>The share is estimated to be undervalued by about $\\text{RM}0.26$.</p>\n<h4>Interpretation</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Both tests give the same answer: <strong>buy</strong> under these forecasts. They must always agree, because $E(R) &gt; k$ is the same condition as $V_0 &gt; P_0$.</li>\n<li>The margin is large, but it depends on the forecast price of $\\text{RM}3.85$ and on the beta and market-premium inputs. If the sale price were only $\\text{RM}3.45$, then $E(R) = \\tfrac{0.18 + 0.05}{3.40} = 6.76\\%$, below $10.20\\%$, and the conclusion would reverse.</li>\n<li>CAPM gives a required return from <strong>systematic risk only</strong>; it is not a forecast of what the share will earn.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Exam tip:</strong> the common mistake is to compare today's price with next year's price directly ($\\text{RM}3.40 &lt; \\text{RM}3.85$), ignoring the dividend, the wait and the required return, or to use $9\\%$ instead of the premium of $6\\%$ in CAPM.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question tut-q\" data-tut-ch=\"8\" data-tut-t=\"Calculation\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q8</span><div><div class=\"tut-tags\"><span class=\"tut-topic\">Topic 6 · Q8</span><span class=\"tut-type tut-calculation\">Calculation</span></div><strong class=\"tut-title\">Zero-growth dividend valuation</strong></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body markdown-body compact\"><p>A mature company is expected to pay a constant annual dividend of $\\text{RM}0.30$ indefinitely. If investors require $9\\%$, estimate the share's intrinsic value using the zero-growth model. The market price is $\\text{RM}3.60$. Interpret the valuation and identify one reason the conclusion could be unreliable.</p><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal the exam-ready answer</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><h4>Valuation</h4>\n<p><strong>Given:</strong> $D = \\text{RM}0.30$ every year-end for ever, $k = 9\\%$, $P_0 = \\text{RM}3.60$.</p>\n<p><strong>Formula</strong> (a level perpetuity, first payment one year from now)</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$V_0 = \\dfrac{D}{k}$$</div>\n<p><strong>Substitution</strong></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$V_0 = \\dfrac{0.30}{0.09} = \\boxed{\\text{RM}3.33}$$</div>\n<h4>Comparison with the market price</h4>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$V_0 = \\text{RM}3.33 &lt; P_0 = \\text{RM}3.60$$</div>\n<p>The market price is $\\text{RM}0.27$ above the estimated value, about $8\\%$ higher. The share appears <strong>overvalued</strong> under these assumptions.</p>\n<p>The same conclusion appears in the return test. Buying at $\\text{RM}3.60$ and receiving $\\text{RM}0.30$ for ever gives an implied return of</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$k_{\\text{implied}} = \\dfrac{D}{P_0} = \\dfrac{0.30}{3.60} = 8.33\\% &lt; 9\\%$$</div>\n<p>so the investor earns less than the required return.</p>\n<h4>Interpretation</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>At $\\text{RM}3.60$ the share is not attractive for an investor who needs $9\\%$; one would expect to pay no more than about $\\text{RM}3.33$.</li>\n<li>Because the dividend is fixed, the whole return is the dividend yield; there is no growth or capital gain.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Why the conclusion may be unreliable</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>The dividend may not stay level.</strong> Even a mature firm may grow its dividend, in which case the zero-growth value is understated and the share may be fairly priced. A growth of only $g = 1\\%$ would give $V_0 = \\tfrac{0.30(1.01)}{0.09 - 0.01} = \\text{RM}3.79 &gt; \\text{RM}3.60$.</li>\n<li>The required return of $9\\%$ is itself an estimate, and a small change in $k$ changes $V_0$ greatly.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Exam tip:</strong> the common mistake is to enter $9$ instead of $0.09$, or to use the formula for a dividend that is not truly level; check that it is a perpetuity with the first payment one year away.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question tut-q\" data-tut-ch=\"8 7\" data-tut-t=\"Calculation\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q9</span><div><div class=\"tut-tags\"><span class=\"tut-topic\">Topic 6 · Q9</span><span class=\"tut-type tut-calculation\">Calculation</span><span class=\"tut-also\">Also Ch 7</span></div><strong class=\"tut-title\">Sustainable growth and constant-growth valuation</strong></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body markdown-body compact\"><p>A company has just paid a dividend of $\\text{RM}0.20$ per share. Its expected ROE is $12\\%$ and its dividend payout ratio is $50\\%$. Assuming the retention-growth relationship applies, estimate the sustainable dividend growth rate. If the required return is $10\\%$, calculate intrinsic value using the constant-growth model. Compare it with a market price of $\\text{RM}4.80$ and explain the sensitivity of the answer to the growth estimate.</p><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal the exam-ready answer</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><h4>Part 1: Sustainable growth rate <em>(beyond the chapter notes)</em></h4>\n<p><strong>Given:</strong> $ROE = 12\\%$, payout $=50\\%$, so retention $b = 1 - 0.50 = 0.50$.</p>\n<p><strong>Formula</strong> (the retention-growth relationship: retained earnings, reinvested at the ROE, fund growth)</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$g = ROE \\times b$$</div>\n<p><strong>Substitution</strong></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$g = 0.12 \\times 0.50 = \\boxed{6\\%}$$</div>\n<h4>Part 2: Constant-growth value</h4>\n<p><strong>Given:</strong> $D_0 = \\text{RM}0.20$ (just paid), $g = 6\\%$, $k = 10\\%$, with $k &gt; g$.</p>\n<p><strong>Next dividend</strong></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$D_1 = D_0(1+g) = 0.20 \\times 1.06 = \\text{RM}0.212$$</div>\n<p><strong>Formula</strong></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$V_0 = \\dfrac{D_1}{k - g}$$</div>\n<p><strong>Substitution</strong></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$V_0 = \\dfrac{0.212}{0.10 - 0.06} = \\boxed{\\text{RM}5.30}$$</div>\n<h4>Part 3: Comparison with the market price</h4>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$V_0 = \\text{RM}5.30 &gt; P_0 = \\text{RM}4.80$$</div>\n<p>The share is <strong>undervalued</strong> by $\\text{RM}0.50$: the price is about $9.4\\%$ below the estimated value (the value is about $10.4\\%$ above the price). The market price implies a return of $\\tfrac{0.212}{4.80} + 0.06 = 10.42\\%$, slightly above the required $10\\%$.</p>\n<h4>Part 4: Sensitivity to the growth estimate</h4>\n<p>Holding $k = 10\\%$ and $D_0 = \\text{RM}0.20$:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Growth $g$</th>\n<th>$D_1$</th>\n<th>$k - g$</th>\n<th>$V_0$</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>$5\\%$</td>\n<td>$\\text{RM}0.210$</td>\n<td>$5\\%$</td>\n<td>$\\text{RM}4.20$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>$6\\%$</td>\n<td>$\\text{RM}0.212$</td>\n<td>$4\\%$</td>\n<td>$\\text{RM}5.30$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>$7\\%$</td>\n<td>$\\text{RM}0.214$</td>\n<td>$3\\%$</td>\n<td>$\\text{RM}7.13$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>$8\\%$</td>\n<td>$\\text{RM}0.216$</td>\n<td>$2\\%$</td>\n<td>$\\text{RM}10.80$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<ul>\n<li>A move of <strong>one percentage point</strong> in $g$ changes the value from $\\text{RM}5.30$ to $\\text{RM}4.20$ or $\\text{RM}7.13$, which is $-21\\%$ or $+35\\%$. The share is undervalued at $g = 6\\%$ but overvalued at $g = 5\\%$, since $\\text{RM}4.20 &lt; \\text{RM}4.80$.</li>\n<li>The sensitivity arises because $g$ sits in the denominator $k - g$, which is small. The nearer $g$ is to $k$, the more explosive the value.</li>\n<li>The retention-growth formula assumes a constant ROE and payout for ever, and that all growth comes from retained earnings.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Interpretation</h4>\n<p>The share looks cheap, but the margin of safety is small relative to the uncertainty in $g$. A prudent investor would test ROE and payout assumptions before buying.</p>\n<p><strong>Exam tip:</strong> the common mistake is to use $D_0 = \\text{RM}0.20$ in the numerator ($\\tfrac{0.20}{0.04} = \\text{RM}5.00$). The dividend has just been paid, so grow it once to $D_1$. Also use retention $b$ (not payout) in $g = ROE \\times b$.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question tut-q\" data-tut-ch=\"8\" data-tut-t=\"Calculation\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q10</span><div><div class=\"tut-tags\"><span class=\"tut-topic\">Topic 6 · Q10</span><span class=\"tut-type tut-calculation\">Calculation</span></div><strong class=\"tut-title\">Two-stage variable-growth valuation</strong></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body markdown-body compact\"><p>A growth company has just paid a dividend of $\\text{RM}0.20$. Dividends are expected to grow by $20\\%$ annually for three years and by $5\\%$ annually thereafter. Investors require $11\\%$. Forecast dividends for Years $1$ to $4$, calculate the terminal value at the end of Year $3$, and estimate today's intrinsic value using a two-stage dividend model. Compare it with a market price of $\\text{RM}5.40$ and state the key judgement behind the recommendation.</p><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal the exam-ready answer</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><h4>Part 1: Dividend forecasts</h4>\n<p><strong>Given:</strong> $D_0 = \\text{RM}0.20$, $g_1 = 20\\%$ for Years $1$ to $3$, $g_2 = 5\\%$ from Year $4$, $k = 11\\%$.</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$D_t = D_0(1+g_1)^t \\quad (t = 1, 2, 3), \\qquad D_4 = D_3(1+g_2)$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\begin{aligned}\nD_1 &amp;= 0.20 \\times 1.20 = \\text{RM}0.2400\\\\\nD_2 &amp;= 0.24 \\times 1.20 = \\text{RM}0.2880\\\\\nD_3 &amp;= 0.288 \\times 1.20 = \\text{RM}0.3456\\\\\nD_4 &amp;= 0.3456 \\times 1.05 = \\text{RM}0.3629\n\\end{aligned}$$</div>\n<h4>Part 2: Terminal value at the end of Year 3</h4>\n<p>$D_4$ is the first dividend of the stable stage, so the value at the transition date is</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$P_3 = \\dfrac{D_4}{k - g_2} = \\dfrac{0.3629}{0.11 - 0.05} = \\boxed{\\text{RM}6.05}$$</div>\n<p>$P_3$ is a value at the <strong>end of Year 3</strong>, not today. It covers the dividends from Year $4$ onward, and $D_3$ is not in it.</p>\n<h4>Part 3: Present value of everything today</h4>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Item</th>\n<th>Year</th>\n<th>Cash flow</th>\n<th>Discount factor</th>\n<th>Present value</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>$D_1$</td>\n<td>$1$</td>\n<td>$\\text{RM}0.2400$</td>\n<td>$\\tfrac{1}{1.11}$</td>\n<td>$\\text{RM}0.22$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>$D_2$</td>\n<td>$2$</td>\n<td>$\\text{RM}0.2880$</td>\n<td>$\\tfrac{1}{1.11^2}$</td>\n<td>$\\text{RM}0.23$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>$D_3$</td>\n<td>$3$</td>\n<td>$\\text{RM}0.3456$</td>\n<td>$\\tfrac{1}{1.11^3}$</td>\n<td>$\\text{RM}0.25$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>$P_3$</td>\n<td>$3$</td>\n<td>$\\text{RM}6.0480$</td>\n<td>$\\tfrac{1}{1.11^3}$</td>\n<td>$\\text{RM}4.42$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Formula</strong></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$V_0 = \\dfrac{D_1}{1+k} + \\dfrac{D_2}{(1+k)^2} + \\dfrac{D_3 + P_3}{(1+k)^3}$$</div>\n<p><strong>Substitution</strong> (unrounded figures)</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\begin{aligned}\nV_0 &amp;= \\dfrac{0.2400}{1.11} + \\dfrac{0.2880}{1.2321} + \\dfrac{0.3456}{1.3676} + \\dfrac{6.0480}{1.3676}\\\\\n&amp;= 0.2162 + 0.2337 + 0.2527 + 4.4222\\\\\n&amp;= \\boxed{\\text{RM}5.12}\n\\end{aligned}$$</div>\n<p>The dividends in Years $1$ to $3$ are worth $\\text{RM}0.70$ and the terminal value is worth $\\text{RM}4.42$.</p>\n<h4>Part 4: Comparison and judgement</h4>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$V_0 = \\text{RM}5.12 &lt; P_0 = \\text{RM}5.40$$</div>\n<p>The share is <strong>slightly overvalued</strong> by about $\\text{RM}0.28$, or $5\\%$ of the market price. The recommendation is <strong>hold or avoid</strong> rather than buy, unless the investor is more optimistic than these assumptions.</p>\n<p><strong>Key judgement:</strong> the terminal value carries $\\tfrac{4.42}{5.12} = 86\\%$ of the value, so the answer depends mainly on the <strong>long-run growth rate</strong> and on whether growth really falls from $20\\%$ to $5\\%$ in Year $4$.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Assumption changed</th>\n<th>$V_0$</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>$g_2 = 4\\%$</td>\n<td>$\\text{RM}4.46$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>$g_2 = 5\\%$ (base)</td>\n<td>$\\text{RM}5.12$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>$g_2 = 6\\%$</td>\n<td>$\\text{RM}6.06$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>$k = 10\\%$</td>\n<td>$\\text{RM}6.17$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>$k = 12\\%$</td>\n<td>$\\text{RM}4.38$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>One percentage point in either rate moves the value by more than the gap to the market price. The market price of $\\text{RM}5.40$ is justified if the long-run growth is about $5.33\\%$.</p>\n<p><strong>Exam tip:</strong> the common mistake is to discount $P_3$ by only two years, to build it from $D_3$ instead of $D_4$, or to add $D_4$, $D_5$, ... again after including $P_3$. Discount $P_3$ three years, together with $D_3$.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question tut-q\" data-tut-ch=\"8\" data-tut-t=\"Reflection\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">R1</span><div><div class=\"tut-tags\"><span class=\"tut-topic\">Topic 6 · Reflection 1</span><span class=\"tut-type tut-reflection\">Reflection</span></div><strong class=\"tut-title\">The most influential valuation input</strong></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body markdown-body compact\"><p>Which valuation input has the greatest effect on your conclusions?</p><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal the exam-ready answer</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><p>The growth rate and the required return have the greatest effect, because they sit in the denominator $k - g$ and also set the terminal value. In Question 9, moving $g$ by one percentage point changed the value from $\\text{RM}5.30$ to $\\text{RM}4.20$ or $\\text{RM}7.13$, turning an undervalued share into an overvalued one. In Question 10 the terminal value was $86\\%$ of the total, so the long-run growth rate and $k$ dominate the near-term dividends. The effect is largest when $k$ and $g$ are close together. The sensible response is to test a range of values and to ask which assumptions the market price is relying on.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question tut-q\" data-tut-ch=\"8\" data-tut-t=\"Reflection\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">R2</span><div><div class=\"tut-tags\"><span class=\"tut-topic\">Topic 6 · Reflection 2</span><span class=\"tut-type tut-reflection\">Reflection</span></div><strong class=\"tut-title\">Precise calculation, unreliable valuation</strong></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body markdown-body compact\"><p>Why can a precise calculation still produce an unreliable valuation?</p><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal the exam-ready answer</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><p>A formula is exact, but its inputs are forecasts. The model returns $\\text{RM}5.30$ to the sen only because the assumed $g = 6\\%$ and $k = 10\\%$ were entered, and neither is known. Errors in sales, margins, payout, the terminal price and the beta compound along the chain and are magnified when $k - g$ is small. The model can also be wrong in structure: for example, a zero-growth model for a company whose dividend will grow, or a constant-growth model for one still in a high-growth stage. Decimal places show arithmetic precision, not accuracy, so a valuation should be presented as a range and tested for sensitivity.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question tut-q\" data-tut-ch=\"8\" data-tut-t=\"Reflection\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">R3</span><div><div class=\"tut-tags\"><span class=\"tut-topic\">Topic 6 · Reflection 3</span><span class=\"tut-type tut-reflection\">Reflection</span></div><strong class=\"tut-title\">Evidence to support the Question 10 recommendation</strong></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body markdown-body compact\"><p>What evidence would make your recommendation in Question 10 more credible?</p><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal the exam-ready answer</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><p>The recommendation (the value is $\\text{RM}5.12$, slightly below the price of $\\text{RM}5.40$) rests on a three-year growth of $20\\%$ and long-run growth of $5\\%$, so the evidence should test those figures. Useful evidence includes the company's past sales and earnings growth, its order book and capacity plans, and whether its return on equity and retention can fund $20\\%$ dividend growth. Industry growth and competitor margins help judge whether $5\\%$ long-run growth is reasonable, as it should not exceed the economy's growth. Cash-flow forecasts would show whether the dividends are affordable, and a cross-check with P/E or free cash flow would add independent support. Finally, scenario analysis showing that the conclusion holds across plausible values of $g$ and $k$ would make the recommendation more credible.</p></div></details></div></details></div></div><div class=\"tut-group theme-l9\" data-tut-group=\"9\"><h3 class=\"tut-group-title\"><span>Chapter 9</span> Market Efficiency and Behavioral Finance <small>13 questions</small></h3><div class=\"question-bank\"><details class=\"bank-question tut-q\" data-tut-ch=\"9\" data-tut-t=\"Concept\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q1</span><div><div class=\"tut-tags\"><span class=\"tut-topic\">Topic 7 · Q1</span><span class=\"tut-type tut-concept\">Concept</span></div><strong class=\"tut-title\">Efficient markets and the random walk</strong></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body markdown-body compact\"><p>Explain what makes a securities market efficient. Why can rapid incorporation of information cause share-price changes to appear random? Clarify why \"prices move randomly\" does not mean that prices are irrational or that every security is correctly priced at every moment.</p><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal the exam-ready answer</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><h4>What makes a market efficient</h4>\n<p>An <strong>informationally efficient market</strong> is one in which prices reflect a specified set of information so quickly and fully that investors cannot <em>reliably</em> earn abnormal, risk-adjusted returns by trading on that information after costs. Efficiency is always relative to an <strong>information set</strong> and a model of expected return; it does not require investors to be perfectly informed.</p>\n<p>The conditions that produce it are competitive:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>many informed, profit-seeking investors analysing the same public information;</li>\n<li>cheap, fast access to information and low transaction costs;</li>\n<li>freedom to trade, so that a perceived bargain is bought and an overpriced share is sold.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Why price changes look random</h4>\n<p>Prices move when <strong>new information changes expectations</strong>, and the price reacts to the <em>surprise</em> (actual versus expected), not to the news itself. If the information is already in the price, today's change can only come from tomorrow's news. News is by nature unpredictable, so the price change it causes cannot be forecast from yesterday's price change. Fast incorporation therefore removes any pattern that could be exploited, leaving changes that look like a <strong>random walk</strong>: successive changes are hard to predict from past changes.</p>\n<h4>What \"random\" does and does not mean</h4>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Claim</th>\n<th>Verdict</th>\n<th>Reason</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Price <em>changes</em> are unpredictable from past prices</td>\n<td>True</td>\n<td>Competition removes forecastable patterns</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Prices are set irrationally or without reasons</td>\n<td>False</td>\n<td>Each move has a cause: a new piece of information</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Every share equals its \"true\" value at every moment</td>\n<td>False</td>\n<td>Efficiency means no <em>reliably exploitable</em> mispricing after costs, not zero error</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Every share has the same expected return</td>\n<td>False</td>\n<td>Expected return still compensates for risk</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Errors can exist, but if they cannot be identified in advance and exploited after costs, the market is still efficient for practical purposes.</p>\n<p><strong>Limitation:</strong> efficiency is tested jointly with a risk model, so an apparent pattern may be mispricing, an omitted risk or chance.</p>\n<p><strong>Conclusion:</strong> efficient markets are <em>unpredictable because they are informative</em>, not irrational.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question tut-q\" data-tut-ch=\"9\" data-tut-t=\"Concept\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q2</span><div><div class=\"tut-tags\"><span class=\"tut-topic\">Topic 7 · Q2</span><span class=\"tut-type tut-concept\">Concept</span></div><strong class=\"tut-title\">Three forms of the Efficient Markets Hypothesis</strong></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body markdown-body compact\"><p>Distinguish weak-form, semi-strong-form and strong-form market efficiency by the information set reflected in prices. For each form, identify one investment strategy whose ability to earn consistent abnormal returns is being challenged.</p><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal the exam-ready answer</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><h4>Definitions by information set</h4>\n<p>The three forms are <strong>nested</strong>: the strong-form set contains the semi-strong set, which contains the weak-form set.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Form</th>\n<th>Information reflected in prices</th>\n<th>Strategy being challenged</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Weak</strong></td>\n<td>Past prices and trading data (returns, volume)</td>\n<td><strong>Technical analysis</strong>: chart patterns, moving-average and momentum rules based only on past market data</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Semi-strong</strong></td>\n<td>All public information: past prices, accounts, announcements, news</td>\n<td><strong>Fundamental analysis and news trading</strong>: buying on published earnings surprises or screening on public ratios such as P/E</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Strong</strong></td>\n<td>Public <em>and</em> private (inside) information</td>\n<td><strong>Trading on inside information</strong>, and also professional fund management that claims a persistent information edge</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h4>Reading the table</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Under <strong>weak form</strong>, a rule that uses only past prices should not reliably beat the market after costs, because those prices are already known to everyone.</li>\n<li>Under <strong>semi-strong form</strong>, public news is incorporated within minutes, so a trader who reacts <em>after</em> the announcement finds the price already adjusted.</li>\n<li>Under <strong>strong form</strong>, even a person with private information could not profit reliably. This is the most demanding form and is generally regarded as the least well supported; insiders can earn abnormal returns, which is why trading on material non-public information is illegal and must never be treated as a strategy.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Evaluation</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Evidence for one form says nothing about stronger forms: rapid reaction to an announcement supports semi-strong efficiency but does not establish strong efficiency.</li>\n<li>Weak-form efficiency does <em>not</em> mean that buy-and-hold is compulsory or that past data are meaningless; it means that rules built only on past prices should not consistently deliver abnormal returns after costs.</li>\n<li>A fair test asks: what information did the strategy use, was it available at the time, and was the excess return measured after costs and adjusted for risk?</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Conclusion:</strong> the forms differ only in the information set, and each form rules out a different family of strategies.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question tut-q\" data-tut-ch=\"9 5\" data-tut-t=\"Concept\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q3</span><div><div class=\"tut-tags\"><span class=\"tut-topic\">Topic 7 · Q3</span><span class=\"tut-type tut-concept\">Concept</span><span class=\"tut-also\">Also Ch 5</span></div><strong class=\"tut-title\">Expected return, abnormal return and market efficiency</strong></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body markdown-body compact\"><p>Define expected return and abnormal return (alpha). Explain why a positive realised alpha on one investment does not by itself disprove market efficiency, and state what evidence would present a stronger challenge to the Efficient Markets Hypothesis.</p><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal the exam-ready answer</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><h4>Definitions</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Expected return</strong> is the return an investor should require for the risk taken, given a risk model. Under the CAPM:</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$E(R)=R_f+\\beta\\,[E(R_m)-R_f].$$</div>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Abnormal return (alpha)</strong> is the realised return minus the expected return for the same risk:</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\alpha = R_{\\text{actual}} - E(R).$$</div>\n<p>A positive return is therefore not automatically abnormal: $12\\%$ is only $2$ percentage points abnormal if the model predicts $10\\%$.</p>\n<h4>Why one positive alpha does not disprove efficiency</h4>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Luck.</strong> Returns include unexpected news with an expected value of zero. Among thousands of securities, some will beat the benchmark by chance, just as some coin-tossers produce long runs of heads.</li>\n<li><strong>Joint-hypothesis problem.</strong> Any test examines efficiency <em>and</em> the risk model together. A positive $\\alpha$ may reflect an omitted risk factor or a poor benchmark rather than mispricing.</li>\n<li><strong>Costs.</strong> Spreads, fees, taxes and market impact can erase a small excess return.</li>\n<li><strong>Hindsight.</strong> The share was identified as a winner <em>after</em> the result; investors could not have bought it knowing the outcome.</li>\n</ol>\n<h4>Stronger evidence against the EMH</h4>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Feature</th>\n<th>Why it matters</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Persistent</strong> over many periods and securities</td>\n<td>Rules out luck</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Out-of-sample</strong>: a rule fixed in advance and tested on later data</td>\n<td>Rules out data-mining</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Risk-adjusted</strong> with an appropriate model</td>\n<td>Rules out compensation for risk</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>After costs</strong>, at tradeable prices and sizes</td>\n<td>Shows investors could really capture it</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Statistically significant</strong> across a broad sample</td>\n<td>Distinguishes pattern from noise</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>An example is a systematic drift after public earnings news that survives these tests, or fund managers whose alpha persists from one period to the next.</p>\n<p><strong>Conclusion:</strong> one realised alpha is an observation; a refutation needs a repeatable, risk-adjusted, after-cost excess return.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question tut-q\" data-tut-ch=\"9\" data-tut-t=\"Concept\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q4</span><div><div class=\"tut-tags\"><span class=\"tut-topic\">Topic 7 · Q4</span><span class=\"tut-type tut-concept\">Concept</span></div><strong class=\"tut-title\">Arbitrage as an efficiency mechanism</strong></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body markdown-body compact\"><p>Explain how arbitrage can bring equivalent-asset prices back into line. Why might an apparent price difference persist in practice even when informed investors recognise it? Discuss transaction costs, timing, liquidity, short-selling constraints and model risk.</p><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal the exam-ready answer</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><p><em>(Beyond the chapter notes: standard textbook treatment.)</em></p>\n<h4>How arbitrage works</h4>\n<p><strong>Arbitrage</strong> is buying an asset and simultaneously selling an equivalent asset at a higher price, locking in a profit with no net investment and no risk. Suppose the same asset trades at two prices. Arbitrageurs buy the cheap one and sell the dear one:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>buying raises the cheap price;</li>\n<li>selling lowers the dear price;</li>\n<li>trading continues until the gap is too small to profit from.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>This is the mechanism that links similar assets (the same share on two markets, a futures contract and its underlying, an ETF and its basket) and gives efficient pricing its enforcement: informed money pushes prices back towards equivalence.</p>\n<h4>Why gaps can persist</h4>\n<p>Real arbitrage is seldom risk-free, so informed investors may recognise a gap and still leave it open.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Limit</th>\n<th>Explanation</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Transaction costs</strong></td>\n<td>Commissions, spreads and market impact on both legs. If a gap is $0.5\\%$ but round-trip costs are $0.8\\%$, trading loses money</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Timing</strong></td>\n<td>The legs may not execute simultaneously, and the gap can widen before it narrows; the investor may be forced to close early</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Liquidity</strong></td>\n<td>Thin markets mean large trades move the price against the arbitrageur, and positions may be hard to unwind</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Short-selling constraints</strong></td>\n<td>Shares may be unavailable to borrow, borrowing costs can be high, lenders can recall shares, and regulators may restrict short sales; the \"sell the dear asset\" leg may be impossible</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Model risk</strong></td>\n<td>The assets may not be truly equivalent, or the valuation model may be wrong; the \"gap\" may reflect missing risk, not mispricing</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Arbitrageurs also face <strong>capital and horizon risk</strong>: a gap can widen first, trigger margin calls and force liquidation at a loss, even if it would eventually close.</p>\n<p><strong>Evaluation:</strong> these limits explain why small mispricings can last; once a gap exceeds costs and risks, arbitrage pressure still limits how large it can grow.</p>\n<p><strong>Conclusion:</strong> arbitrage pulls prices together, but costs and risks mean that \"no profitable arbitrage after costs\" is the realistic efficiency test.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question tut-q\" data-tut-ch=\"9\" data-tut-t=\"Concept\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q5</span><div><div class=\"tut-tags\"><span class=\"tut-topic\">Topic 7 · Q5</span><span class=\"tut-type tut-concept\">Concept</span></div><strong class=\"tut-title\">Anomalies and competing explanations</strong></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body markdown-body compact\"><p>Describe the January effect, small-firm effect, post-earnings-announcement drift or momentum, and value effect. For any two anomalies, compare three possible explanations: compensation for omitted risk, a chance pattern or data-mining result, and systematic investor behaviour. Does an anomaly automatically provide a profitable trading rule?</p><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal the exam-ready answer</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><h4>The anomalies</h4>\n<p>An <strong>anomaly</strong> is an observed pattern that is hard to reconcile with an efficient-market model of expected returns.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Anomaly</th>\n<th>Pattern reported</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>January effect</strong></td>\n<td>Returns in January have historically been higher than in other months</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Small-firm effect</strong></td>\n<td>Smaller companies have historically earned more than their beta (CAPM) risk predicted</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Post-earnings-announcement drift (PEAD) / momentum</strong></td>\n<td>Prices keep moving in the direction of an earnings surprise, or of recent returns, for some time after the news</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Value effect</strong></td>\n<td>Low P/E (value) shares have historically outperformed high P/E (growth) shares</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h4>Three explanations compared for two anomalies</h4>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Explanation</th>\n<th>Small-firm effect</th>\n<th>PEAD / momentum</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Omitted risk</strong></td>\n<td>Small firms are riskier: illiquid, financially fragile, higher failure risk; a model using beta alone misses this</td>\n<td>Surprise firms may carry higher information or distress risk that the model does not price</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Chance / data-mining</strong></td>\n<td>Found by testing many characteristics on the same data; may vanish in other samples or periods</td>\n<td>Small samples or selected periods; a strategy chosen after seeing the data</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Investor behaviour</strong></td>\n<td>Neglect of small firms, limited institutional coverage</td>\n<td><strong>Underreaction</strong>: investors anchor on old views and adjust to news gradually; later <strong>herding</strong> can extend the trend</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>No single explanation is established; each is a hypothesis to be tested.</p>\n<h4>Is an anomaly a profitable trading rule?</h4>\n<p><strong>No, not automatically.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Costs, spreads, taxes and market impact may exceed the average excess return; small firms are especially illiquid.</li>\n<li>A pattern can be a chance result or disappear once it is publicised and traded.</li>\n<li>It may simply be compensation for risk, in which case the higher return is not \"free\".</li>\n<li>Averages hide losing years and periods when the rule fails.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Conclusion:</strong> an anomaly is a <em>question</em> about efficiency; it becomes a rule only if it survives risk adjustment, costs and out-of-sample testing.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question tut-q\" data-tut-ch=\"9 5\" data-tut-t=\"Calculation\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q6</span><div><div class=\"tut-tags\"><span class=\"tut-topic\">Topic 7 · Q6</span><span class=\"tut-type tut-calculation\">Calculation</span><span class=\"tut-also\">Also Ch 5</span></div><strong class=\"tut-title\">CAPM benchmark and abnormal return</strong></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body markdown-body compact\"><p>The risk-free rate is $3\\%$, the expected market return is $9\\%$, and a share has a beta of $1.25$. During the year the share earns $13.5\\%$. Calculate its CAPM expected return and abnormal return. Interpret the result and explain whether this single observation rejects semi-strong-form efficiency.</p><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal the exam-ready answer</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><p><strong>Given:</strong> $R_f=3\\%$, $E(R_m)=9\\%$, $\\beta=1.25$, actual return $R=13.5\\%$.</p>\n<h4>Step 1. CAPM expected return</h4>\n<p><strong>Formula:</strong></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$E(R)=R_f+\\beta\\,[E(R_m)-R_f]$$</div>\n<p><strong>Substitution:</strong></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$E(R)=3\\%+1.25\\times(9\\%-3\\%)=3\\%+1.25\\times6\\%=3\\%+7.5\\%$$</div>\n<p><strong>Result:</strong></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{E(R)=10.50\\%}$$</div>\n<h4>Step 2. Abnormal return</h4>\n<p><strong>Formula:</strong></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\alpha=R_{\\text{actual}}-E(R)$$</div>\n<p><strong>Substitution:</strong></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\alpha=13.5\\%-10.5\\%$$</div>\n<p><strong>Result:</strong></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\alpha=+3.00\\%\\ (3\\text{ percentage points})}$$</div>\n<h4>Interpretation</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>The share earned $3.00$ percentage points more than the CAPM says its market risk deserved, so it <em>outperformed</em> its benchmark. The $13.5\\%$ is only abnormal relative to $10.5\\%$, not relative to zero.</li>\n<li>This single observation does <strong>not</strong> reject semi-strong-form efficiency:</li>\n<li><strong>Chance:</strong> a return includes unexpected news with zero expected value; among many shares some will beat the CAPM benchmark in any year.</li>\n<li><strong>Model error:</strong> semi-strong efficiency is tested jointly with the CAPM, so $\\alpha$ may reflect an omitted risk factor or a poor beta estimate.</li>\n<li><strong>Hindsight and costs:</strong> the share was identified after the result, and trading costs would reduce any real gain.</li>\n<li>Rejection needs <em>many</em> securities or periods, a pre-specified strategy based on public information, a statistically significant average abnormal return, and an after-cost profit.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Exam tip:</strong> the most common mistake is calling $13.5\\%$ \"abnormal\" because it is high. Abnormal return is <em>actual minus CAPM expected</em>, here $13.5\\%-10.5\\%$.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question tut-q\" data-tut-ch=\"9\" data-tut-t=\"Concept\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q7</span><div><div class=\"tut-tags\"><span class=\"tut-topic\">Topic 7 · Q7</span><span class=\"tut-type tut-concept\">Concept</span></div><strong class=\"tut-title\">Classifying reactions to public news</strong></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body markdown-body compact\"><p>A Bursa Malaysia company announces earnings that are substantially above the market consensus at 10:00 a.m. Consider three possible price paths: (A) the price jumps within minutes and then fluctuates around the new level; (B) the price rises gradually for ten trading days; and (C) the price rises sharply, overshoots, and reverses over the next week. For each path, assess its consistency with semi-strong-form efficiency and identify the anomaly or behavioural process that may be present.</p><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal the exam-ready answer</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><p>There are no numbers to calculate; the task is classification. The test is: <em>does the price adjust fully and quickly to the surprise, with no predictable move afterwards?</em></p>\n<h4>Assessment of the three paths</h4>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Path</th>\n<th>Price behaviour</th>\n<th>Consistent with semi-strong efficiency?</th>\n<th>Anomaly or behavioural process</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>A</strong></td>\n<td>Jumps within minutes, then fluctuates randomly around the new level</td>\n<td><strong>Yes</strong>. The surprise is incorporated almost at once; nothing predictable follows</td>\n<td>None needed; this is the textbook efficient reaction</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>B</strong></td>\n<td>Rises gradually over ten trading days</td>\n<td><strong>No, if systematic.</strong> Part of the news is absorbed slowly, so a buyer <em>after</em> the announcement could still earn a predictable gain</td>\n<td><strong>Post-earnings-announcement drift</strong>; behavioural source: <strong>underreaction</strong>, anchoring on prior views and slow adjustment</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>C</strong></td>\n<td>Rises sharply, overshoots, then reverses over the next week</td>\n<td><strong>No, if systematic.</strong> The initial price went beyond fundamental value and then corrected, a predictable reversal</td>\n<td><strong>Overreaction</strong>; behavioural source: <strong>representativeness</strong> (extrapolating one strong result), <strong>overconfidence</strong> and <strong>herding</strong> into a rising price</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h4>Interpretation</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Path A is the only path that fits semi-strong efficiency without qualification.</li>\n<li>Paths B and C are opposite errors: <em>underreaction</em> (too slow) and <em>overreaction</em> (too far).</li>\n<li>One announcement proves nothing. A single drift or reversal can be chance or an unexpected later news item. The paths become evidence against efficiency only if the pattern is <strong>systematic across many announcements</strong>, measured against a risk-adjusted benchmark and <strong>persists after costs</strong>.</li>\n<li>Timing matters: in A, an investor buying at 10:30 a.m. has missed the move; in B an investor could, in principle, buy after the news and earn the drift, which is why B is a challenge.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Exam tip:</strong> do not treat a price rise after good news as proof of inefficiency. Rapid adjustment (A) is efficient; <em>predictable</em> continuation (B) or reversal (C) is the challenge.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question tut-q\" data-tut-ch=\"9\" data-tut-t=\"Calculation\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q8</span><div><div class=\"tut-tags\"><span class=\"tut-topic\">Topic 7 · Q8</span><span class=\"tut-type tut-calculation\">Calculation</span></div><strong class=\"tut-title\">Market breadth, new highs-new lows and on-balance volume</strong></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body markdown-body compact\"><p>For a market with $900$ listed counters, the following data are observed. Day 1: $520$ advances, $380$ declines, $60$ new highs, $25$ new lows. Day 2: $560$ advances, $340$ declines, $70$ highs, $20$ lows. Day 3: $480$ advances, $420$ declines, $45$ highs, $35$ lows. Day 4: $390$ advances, $510$ declines, $30$ highs, $55$ lows. For one representative stock, closing prices and volume are Day 1 $\\text{RM}4.00$ and $100{,}000$ shares, Day 2 $\\text{RM}4.10$ and $120{,}000$, Day 3 $\\text{RM}4.15$ and $90{,}000$, and Day 4 $\\text{RM}4.05$ and $150{,}000$.\n(a) Calculate the daily advance-decline value and a cumulative A-D line starting at zero.\n(b) Calculate daily new highs minus new lows.\n(c) Calculate on-balance volume from a Day 1 starting value of zero, adding volume after an up-close and subtracting it after a down-close.\n(d) Interpret the combined signals.</p><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal the exam-ready answer</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><h4>(a) Advance-decline value and A-D line</h4>\n<p><strong>Given:</strong> advances and declines each day (every day sums to $900$ counters, so there are no unchanged issues); line starts at $0$.</p>\n<p><strong>Formula:</strong></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Net A-D}_t=\\text{Advances}_t-\\text{Declines}_t,\\qquad \\text{A-D line}_t=\\text{A-D line}_{t-1}+\\text{Net A-D}_t$$</div>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Day</th>\n<th>Advances</th>\n<th>Declines</th>\n<th>Net A-D</th>\n<th>Cumulative A-D line</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>$520$</td>\n<td>$380$</td>\n<td>$+140$</td>\n<td>$140$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>$560$</td>\n<td>$340$</td>\n<td>$+220$</td>\n<td>$360$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>$480$</td>\n<td>$420$</td>\n<td>$+60$</td>\n<td>$420$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>$390$</td>\n<td>$510$</td>\n<td>$-120$</td>\n<td>$300$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Result:</strong></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\begin{aligned}\\text{Net A-D}&amp;=+140,\\ +220,\\ +60,\\ -120\\\\ \\text{A-D line}&amp;=140,\\ 360,\\ 420,\\ 300\\end{aligned}}$$</div>\n<h4>(b) New highs minus new lows</h4>\n<p><strong>Formula:</strong></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{NH}-\\text{NL}=\\text{new highs}-\\text{new lows}$$</div>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Day</th>\n<th>New highs</th>\n<th>New lows</th>\n<th>NH - NL</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>$60$</td>\n<td>$25$</td>\n<td>$+35$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>$70$</td>\n<td>$20$</td>\n<td>$+50$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>$45$</td>\n<td>$35$</td>\n<td>$+10$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>$30$</td>\n<td>$55$</td>\n<td>$-25$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Result:</strong></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{NH}-\\text{NL}=+35,\\ +50,\\ +10,\\ -25}$$</div>\n<h4>(c) On-balance volume</h4>\n<p><strong>Rule:</strong> the close is higher: add volume; lower: subtract volume; unchanged: no change. Day 1 has no previous close, so OBV starts at $0$.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Day</th>\n<th>Close</th>\n<th>Change</th>\n<th>Volume</th>\n<th>OBV</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>$\\text{RM}4.00$</td>\n<td>start</td>\n<td>$100{,}000$</td>\n<td>$0$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>$\\text{RM}4.10$</td>\n<td>up</td>\n<td>$120{,}000$</td>\n<td>$0+120{,}000=120{,}000$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>$\\text{RM}4.15$</td>\n<td>up</td>\n<td>$90{,}000$</td>\n<td>$120{,}000+90{,}000=210{,}000$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>$\\text{RM}4.05$</td>\n<td>down</td>\n<td>$150{,}000$</td>\n<td>$210{,}000-150{,}000=60{,}000$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Result:</strong></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{OBV}=0,\\ 120{,}000,\\ 210{,}000,\\ 60{,}000\\ \\text{shares}}$$</div>\n<h4>(d) Interpretation of the combined signals</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Days 1-3: broad strength.</strong> Net A-D is positive, the A-D line climbs to $420$, NH - NL is positive and the stock's OBV rises on up-closes. More stocks than not are participating.</li>\n<li><strong>Peak on Day 3.</strong> The gain in breadth slows sharply (net A-D falls from $+220$ to $+60$) and NH - NL shrinks from $+50$ to $+10$ even though the stock's price still rises. <strong>Volume</strong> also warns: the Day 3 rise came on the <em>lowest</em> volume of the four days ($90{,}000$ against $120{,}000$ on Day 2), so OBV rose by less ($+90{,}000$ against $+120{,}000$) and the advance was only weakly confirmed before the high-volume fall on Day 4.</li>\n<li><strong>Day 4: all three deteriorate together.</strong> Declines outnumber advances ($510$ against $390$), the A-D line falls to $300$, NH - NL turns negative ($-25$, so more stocks hit 52-week lows than highs), and the stock closes down $\\text{RM}0.10$ ($2.41\\%$) on its heaviest volume ($150{,}000$), cutting OBV from $210{,}000$ to $60{,}000$. Heavy volume on a fall suggests selling pressure.</li>\n<li><strong>Overall:</strong> a warning that momentum is weakening, with two breadth measures (A-D and NH - NL, both computed on the same counters) and the stock's OBV agreeing.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Limits:</strong> this is four days of data and one stock. OBV and breadth describe what happened; they do not forecast the next day, and a falling A-D line does not guarantee a reversal. Under weak-form efficiency such signals should not yield reliable abnormal profits after costs. The A-D line remains above its starting value of $0$, so the deterioration is a pullback signal, not a confirmed downtrend.</p>\n<p><strong>Exam tip:</strong> the common mistake is reporting the daily net A-D instead of the <em>cumulative</em> line, or forgetting that OBV subtracts the whole day's volume on a down-close.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question tut-q\" data-tut-ch=\"9\" data-tut-t=\"Calculation\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q9</span><div><div class=\"tut-tags\"><span class=\"tut-topic\">Topic 7 · Q9</span><span class=\"tut-type tut-calculation\">Calculation</span></div><strong class=\"tut-title\">Relative Strength Index</strong></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body markdown-body compact\"><p>Over the selected RSI measurement period, a share has an average price gain on up-days of $\\text{RM}0.18$ and an average price decline on down-days of $\\text{RM}0.06$. Calculate relative strength and RSI using $\\text{RSI}=100-[100/(1+\\text{RS})]$. Interpret the reading using $30$ and $70$ as reference levels. Explain why the result should be treated as a warning signal rather than an automatic trade.</p><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal the exam-ready answer</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><p><em>(Beyond the chapter notes: standard textbook treatment.)</em></p>\n<p><strong>Given:</strong> average gain on up-days $=\\text{RM}0.18$; average decline on down-days $=\\text{RM}0.06$.</p>\n<h4>Relative strength</h4>\n<p><strong>Formula:</strong></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{RS}=\\dfrac{\\text{average gain}}{\\text{average loss}}$$</div>\n<p><strong>Substitution:</strong></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{RS}=\\dfrac{0.18}{0.06}$$</div>\n<p><strong>Result:</strong></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{RS}=3.00}$$</div>\n<h4>RSI</h4>\n<p><strong>Formula:</strong></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{RSI}=100-\\dfrac{100}{1+\\text{RS}}$$</div>\n<p><strong>Substitution:</strong></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{RSI}=100-\\dfrac{100}{1+3}=100-25$$</div>\n<p><strong>Result:</strong></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{RSI}=75}$$</div>\n<p>Check: $\\text{RSI}=100\\times\\dfrac{0.18}{0.18+0.06}=75$.</p>\n<h4>Interpretation</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>$\\text{RS}=3$ means that, over the period, the average up-move was three times the average down-move.</li>\n<li>An RSI of $75$ is <strong>above $70$</strong>, the usual <em>overbought</em> reference: recent gains have been strong relative to losses, so the share may be due for a pause or pullback. A reading below $30$ would be <em>oversold</em>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Why it is a warning, not an automatic trade</h4>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>It describes the past.</strong> RSI is built from past prices; it does not tell the next move.</li>\n<li><strong>Strong trends stay overbought.</strong> A share in a persistent uptrend can stay above $70$ for a long time; selling at $75$ may forgo further gains.</li>\n<li><strong>Parameter dependence.</strong> The result changes with the period length (commonly $14$ days) and the smoothing method; the $30$/$70$ levels are conventions, not laws.</li>\n<li><strong>Costs and risk.</strong> Trading on every signal incurs costs and generates false signals; a stop-loss and confirmation with other evidence (volume, fundamentals, breadth) are needed.</li>\n<li><strong>Weak-form efficiency.</strong> If prices already reflect past price data, a rule using only past prices should not deliver reliable abnormal returns after costs.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Exam tip:</strong> the common mistake is dividing the loss by the gain (giving $\\text{RS}=0.33$) or leaving RSI as $\\text{RS}$. RS is <em>gain over loss</em>, and RSI is $100-100/(1+\\text{RS})$. Also note that textbooks average gains and losses over <em>all</em> days in the period; here the question gives the averages directly, so use them as given.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question tut-q\" data-tut-ch=\"9\" data-tut-t=\"Calculation\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q10</span><div><div class=\"tut-tags\"><span class=\"tut-topic\">Topic 7 · Q10</span><span class=\"tut-type tut-calculation\">Calculation</span></div><strong class=\"tut-title\">Moving averages, support and resistance</strong></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body markdown-body compact\"><p>A share records six daily closing prices: $\\text{RM}4.00$, $\\text{RM}4.10$, $\\text{RM}4.20$, $\\text{RM}4.10$, $\\text{RM}4.30$ and $\\text{RM}4.40$.\n(a) Calculate the three-day moving average for Days 3 to 6.\n(b) Identify price/moving-average crossover signals under the rule \"buy when price crosses above; sell when price crosses below.\"\n(c) Suppose $\\text{RM}4.45$ has repeatedly acted as resistance and $\\text{RM}4.00$ as support. Discuss what a high-volume close at $\\text{RM}4.50$ would suggest and how a disciplined investor could guard against a false breakout.\n(d) Relate this strategy to weak-form efficiency.</p><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal the exam-ready answer</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><h4>(a) Three-day simple moving average</h4>\n<p><strong>Formula:</strong></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{MA}_3(t)=\\dfrac{P_t+P_{t-1}+P_{t-2}}{3}$$</div>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Day</th>\n<th>Close</th>\n<th>Working</th>\n<th>MA(3)</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>$\\text{RM}4.20$</td>\n<td>$(4.00+4.10+4.20)/3$</td>\n<td>$\\text{RM}4.10$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>$\\text{RM}4.10$</td>\n<td>$(4.10+4.20+4.10)/3$</td>\n<td>$\\text{RM}4.13$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>5</td>\n<td>$\\text{RM}4.30$</td>\n<td>$(4.20+4.10+4.30)/3$</td>\n<td>$\\text{RM}4.20$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>6</td>\n<td>$\\text{RM}4.40$</td>\n<td>$(4.10+4.30+4.40)/3$</td>\n<td>$\\text{RM}4.27$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{MA}_3=\\text{RM}4.10,\\ \\text{RM}4.13,\\ \\text{RM}4.20,\\ \\text{RM}4.27}$$</div>\n<p>These are the values for Days 3 to 6.</p>\n<p>(Unrounded, Day 4 is $\\text{RM}4.1333$ and Day 6 is $\\text{RM}4.2667$.)</p>\n<h4>(b) Crossover signals</h4>\n<p>Compare each close with its moving average:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Day</th>\n<th>Close</th>\n<th>MA(3)</th>\n<th>Close - MA</th>\n<th>Position</th>\n<th>Signal</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>$\\text{RM}4.20$</td>\n<td>$\\text{RM}4.10$</td>\n<td>$+0.10$</td>\n<td>above</td>\n<td>none: first MA, no earlier comparison</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>$\\text{RM}4.10$</td>\n<td>$\\text{RM}4.13$</td>\n<td>$-0.03$</td>\n<td>below</td>\n<td><strong>Sell</strong>: price crosses below</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>5</td>\n<td>$\\text{RM}4.30$</td>\n<td>$\\text{RM}4.20$</td>\n<td>$+0.10$</td>\n<td>above</td>\n<td><strong>Buy</strong>: price crosses above</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>6</td>\n<td>$\\text{RM}4.40$</td>\n<td>$\\text{RM}4.27$</td>\n<td>$+0.13$</td>\n<td>above</td>\n<td>none: stays above, hold</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Result:</strong> a <strong>sell at $\\text{RM}4.10$ on Day 4</strong> and a <strong>buy at $\\text{RM}4.30$ on Day 5</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Interpretation:</strong> the Day 4 sell was a <em>whipsaw</em>: the investor sold at $\\text{RM}4.10$ and re-entered at $\\text{RM}4.30$, paying $\\text{RM}0.20$ ($4.88\\%$) more, plus two sets of costs. Compared with holding from Day 3 at $\\text{RM}4.20$ (gain $\\text{RM}0.20$ by Day 6), assuming a position held from Day 3, the rule nets $\\text{RM}0.00$ ($-0.10+0.10$: a loss of $\\text{RM}0.10$ on the sale, a gain of $\\text{RM}0.10$ from $\\text{RM}4.30$ to $\\text{RM}4.40$) against $\\text{RM}0.20$ for holding, so it underperforms holding by $\\text{RM}0.20$ before costs. Moving averages lag, and shorter windows generate more noisy crossings.</p>\n<h4>(c) Support, resistance and a breakout at $\\text{RM}4.50$</h4>\n<p><strong>Concepts:</strong> <em>resistance</em> is a price area where earlier advances met selling ($\\text{RM}4.45$); <em>support</em> is an area where earlier declines met buying ($\\text{RM}4.00$). A close beyond either is a <em>breakout</em>.</p>\n<p><strong>What the close suggests:</strong> $\\text{RM}4.50$ is $\\text{RM}0.05$ (about $1.12\\%$) above resistance. With <strong>high volume</strong>, buyers have overwhelmed the supply that previously capped the price, so technicians read it as a bullish breakout, with $\\text{RM}4.45$ becoming the new support. High volume adds conviction, but a single close does not prove a lasting trend.</p>\n<p><strong>Guarding against a false breakout:</strong>\n- <strong>Wait for confirmation:</strong> a close above resistance on two or three consecutive days, or a minimum margin such as $1\\%$ to $3\\%$ above $\\text{RM}4.45$.\n- <strong>Check volume stays high</strong> and look for a <em>retest</em> of $\\text{RM}4.45$ that holds.\n- <strong>Use a stop-loss</strong> just below the old resistance, for example $\\text{RM}4.40$ (a loss of about $2.22\\%$ from $\\text{RM}4.50$), and a modest position size.\n- <strong>Check news and fundamentals</strong> and include costs, so the trade is not based on the chart alone.\n- Pre-specify the rules before the trade.</p>\n<h4>(d) Relation to weak-form efficiency</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Moving-average crossovers, support and resistance use <strong>only past prices and volume</strong>: exactly the information set of weak-form efficiency.</li>\n<li>If the weak form holds, such rules should <strong>not reliably earn abnormal risk-adjusted returns after costs</strong>. The whipsaw in (b) shows the weakness: lag, false signals and trading costs.</li>\n<li>Rules sometimes look good on one chart, but that is hindsight. The challenge to weak-form efficiency requires a pre-specified rule that profits across many shares and periods, out of sample, after costs.</li>\n<li>Technical analysis can still <em>describe</em> trends and support risk control, but that is not proof of predictive power.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Exam tip:</strong> the common mistake is averaging the wrong three prices (use the <em>latest</em> three closes) or treating the first MA day as a crossover. Also do not claim that the rule \"works\" from six days of data.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question tut-q\" data-tut-ch=\"9\" data-tut-t=\"Reflection\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">R1</span><div><div class=\"tut-tags\"><span class=\"tut-topic\">Topic 7 · Reflection 1</span><span class=\"tut-type tut-reflection\">Reflection</span></div><strong class=\"tut-title\">The strongest challenge to market efficiency</strong></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body markdown-body compact\"><p>Which observation in this tutorial provides the strongest challenge to market efficiency, and why?</p><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal the exam-ready answer</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><p>The strongest challenge is a <strong>systematic post-earnings-announcement drift</strong> (Path B in Q7, and the PEAD anomaly in Q5). It uses <em>public</em> information that every investor could see, it is <em>predictable</em> in direction, and a trader acting after the news could in principle profit, which is exactly what semi-strong efficiency rules out. By contrast, the single positive alpha in Q6 is weak evidence because it can be luck, model error or hindsight, and the indicator signals in Q8 to Q10 are descriptive and face costs and whipsaws. Even drift remains only a <em>challenge</em> unless it survives risk adjustment, transaction costs and out-of-sample testing.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question tut-q\" data-tut-ch=\"9\" data-tut-t=\"Reflection\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">R2</span><div><div class=\"tut-tags\"><span class=\"tut-topic\">Topic 7 · Reflection 2</span><span class=\"tut-type tut-reflection\">Reflection</span></div><strong class=\"tut-title\">Accurate calculation, poor trade</strong></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body markdown-body compact\"><p>Why can an accurate technical-indicator calculation still lead to a poor trade?</p><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal the exam-ready answer</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><p>An indicator calculation is only arithmetic on <strong>past</strong> data; being correct does not make it predictive. An $\\text{RSI}$ of $75$ or a moving-average crossover can be computed perfectly yet still be a false signal: strong trends stay overbought, and lagging averages produce whipsaws, as in Q10. The signal also ignores transaction costs, liquidity, news and the investor's risk limits, and results depend on arbitrary choices such as the window length and the $30$ and $70$ thresholds. If markets are weak-form efficient, such signals should not reliably beat the market after costs, so a correct calculation needs risk control and confirming evidence.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question tut-q\" data-tut-ch=\"9\" data-tut-t=\"Reflection\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">R3</span><div><div class=\"tut-tags\"><span class=\"tut-topic\">Topic 7 · Reflection 3</span><span class=\"tut-type tut-reflection\">Reflection</span></div><strong class=\"tut-title\">Evidence needed before calling a pattern exploitable</strong></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body markdown-body compact\"><p>What evidence is required before calling a market pattern an exploitable anomaly?</p><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal the exam-ready answer</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><p>Four conditions are needed before a pattern counts as <em>exploitable</em>. It must be:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Pre-specified and out-of-sample</strong>, which rules out data-mining: the rule was fixed before testing and works on later data and in other markets or periods.</li>\n<li><strong>Risk-adjusted</strong>: the excess return survives an appropriate expected-return model, so it is not just compensation for omitted risk.</li>\n<li><strong>Net of costs and tradeable</strong>: spreads, fees, taxes, market impact, short-sale limits and liquidity are included, at realistic prices and sizes.</li>\n<li><strong>Statistically robust and persistent</strong>: significant across many securities and years, after allowing for the number of strategies tested, and still present after it becomes widely known.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>A plausible economic or behavioural reason strengthens the case. Without these, the pattern is only an anomaly, not a profitable rule.</p></div></details></div></details></div></div><div class=\"tut-group theme-l10\" data-tut-group=\"10\"><h3 class=\"tut-group-title\"><span>Chapter 10</span> Fixed-Income Securities <small>6 questions</small></h3><div class=\"question-bank\"><details class=\"bank-question tut-q\" data-tut-ch=\"10\" data-tut-t=\"Concept\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q1</span><div><div class=\"tut-tags\"><span class=\"tut-topic\">Topics 8 and 9 · Q1</span><span class=\"tut-type tut-concept\">Concept</span></div><strong class=\"tut-title\">The role of bonds in an investment portfolio</strong></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body markdown-body compact\"><p>Explain why bonds are described as fixed-income securities.</p>\n<p>Compare the sources of bond return with those of ordinary shares, and discuss how bonds can contribute current income, stability and diversification to a portfolio.</p>\n<p>Why does lower risk usually imply a lower expected return?</p><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal the exam-ready answer</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><h4>Why \"fixed income\"</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>A bond is a <strong>contract</strong>, not a share of ownership. The issuer borrows the <strong>par (principal) value</strong> and promises a <strong>coupon</strong> on stated dates and repayment of par at <strong>maturity</strong>.</li>\n<li>The investor is a <strong>creditor</strong> with contractual claims that generally rank ahead of common equity. For example, a par $\\text{RM}1{,}000$ bond with a $6\\%$ coupon promises $\\text{RM}60$ a year and $\\text{RM}1{,}000$ at maturity.</li>\n<li>\"Fixed\" refers to the <strong>promised cash flows</strong>, not to the market price or the investor's total return. Prices move with market yields, and the issuer can default.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Sources of return compared</h4>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th></th>\n<th>Bonds</th>\n<th>Ordinary shares</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Income</td>\n<td>Coupon: contractual, set at issue</td>\n<td>Dividend: discretionary, can be cut</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Capital gain or loss</td>\n<td>Price change from market yields and credit quality; par is repaid at maturity</td>\n<td>Price change from earnings growth and sentiment; no maturity value</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Claim</td>\n<td>Creditor, paid before shareholders</td>\n<td>Residual claimant, paid last</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Return pattern</td>\n<td>More predictable</td>\n<td>More variable, with higher upside and downside</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>For both, total return is income plus price change. A bond's income is far more certain.</p>\n<h4>Contribution to a portfolio</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Current income:</strong> regular coupons suit investors who need cash flow.</li>\n<li><strong>Stability:</strong> a senior contractual claim and a maturity value usually mean lower volatility than shares.</li>\n<li><strong>Diversification:</strong> bond returns are driven by interest rates and credit conditions rather than by company growth, so they are imperfectly correlated with shares and reduce portfolio risk.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Why lower risk means lower expected return</h4>\n<p>Investors are risk averse, so the required return is roughly the real return plus an inflation premium plus a <strong>risk premium</strong> (Chapter 11). A safer, senior, contractual claim needs a smaller premium, so its market price rises until its expected return falls. If a safer asset offered the same return as a riskier one, investors would bid its price up and push its return down.</p>\n<h4>Limitation</h4>\n<p>Not every bond is safe. A long-maturity or low-rated bond can lose substantial value, and diversification benefits can weaken if yields and share prices fall together.</p>\n<p><strong>Conclusion:</strong> bonds are fixed-income because their cash flows are contractual. They add income, stability and diversification, and their lower expected return is the price of lower risk.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question tut-q\" data-tut-ch=\"10 11\" data-tut-t=\"Concept\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q2</span><div><div class=\"tut-tags\"><span class=\"tut-topic\">Topics 8 and 9 · Q2</span><span class=\"tut-type tut-concept\">Concept</span><span class=\"tut-also\">Also Ch 11</span></div><strong class=\"tut-title\">Bond features, yields and price behaviour</strong></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body markdown-body compact\"><p>Distinguish par value, coupon, coupon rate and current yield.</p>\n<p>Explain when a bond sells at par, at a premium or at a discount.</p>\n<p>Why do bond prices move inversely to market yields, and why are longer-maturity bonds generally more price-sensitive than shorter-maturity bonds?</p><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal the exam-ready answer</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><h4>Key terms</h4>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Term</th>\n<th>Meaning</th>\n<th>Example</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Par value</td>\n<td>Principal repaid at maturity; the base for the coupon</td>\n<td>$\\text{RM}1{,}000$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Coupon</td>\n<td>Ringgit interest paid each year: coupon rate $\\times$ par</td>\n<td>$6\\% \\times 1{,}000 = \\text{RM}60$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Coupon rate</td>\n<td>Coupon as a percentage of <strong>par</strong>; fixed at issue</td>\n<td>$6\\%$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Current yield</td>\n<td>Annual coupon $\\div$ <strong>current market price</strong></td>\n<td>$60/900 = 6.67\\%$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The coupon rate uses par and never changes. Current yield uses today's price and changes whenever the price changes. Neither includes the gain or loss to maturity, which YTM captures.</p>\n<h4>Par, premium and discount</h4>\n<p>Compare the coupon rate with the required market yield $y$ on comparable bonds.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Condition</th>\n<th>Price</th>\n<th>Current yield</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Coupon rate $= y$</td>\n<td>At par</td>\n<td>Equals the coupon rate</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Coupon rate $&gt; y$</td>\n<td>Premium (above par)</td>\n<td>Below the coupon rate</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Coupon rate $&lt; y$</td>\n<td>Discount (below par)</td>\n<td>Above the coupon rate</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h4>Why prices move inversely to yields</h4>\n<p>The coupons are fixed, so a bond's price is the present value of those fixed cash flows discounted at the market yield.\n- When market yields <strong>rise</strong>, new bonds pay more than the old bond's fixed coupon. Buyers will pay less for it, so its price <strong>falls</strong> until its yield is competitive.\n- When market yields <strong>fall</strong>, its fixed coupons become more attractive and its price <strong>rises</strong>.</p>\n<h4>Why longer maturities are more sensitive</h4>\n<p>Much of a long bond's value sits in cash flows many years away. A change in $y$ is compounded over more periods in the discount factor $(1+y)^{-N}$, so those distant payments lose more present value. For two $6\\%$ bonds, a rise in yield from $4\\%$ to $5\\%$ cuts the price of a $5$-year bond by about $4.2\\%$ but a $15$-year bond by about $9.7\\%$ (see Question 7).</p>\n<h4>Limitation</h4>\n<p>Maturity alone is not a complete measure. Coupon size, yield level and embedded options such as calls also matter, and duration (Chapter 11) summarises sensitivity more directly.</p>\n<p><strong>Conclusion:</strong> the coupon rate is fixed on par, the price moves with market yield, and a longer maturity magnifies the price change.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question tut-q\" data-tut-ch=\"10 11\" data-tut-t=\"Concept\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q3</span><div><div class=\"tut-tags\"><span class=\"tut-topic\">Topics 8 and 9 · Q3</span><span class=\"tut-type tut-concept\">Concept</span><span class=\"tut-also\">Also Ch 11</span></div><strong class=\"tut-title\">Major bond risks and credit ratings</strong></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body markdown-body compact\"><p>Explain interest-rate risk, purchasing-power risk, business or financial risk, liquidity risk and call risk.</p>\n<p>How do rating agencies assess credit risk, and why do lower-rated corporate bonds normally offer a wider yield or credit spread over government bonds?</p><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal the exam-ready answer</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><h4>The five risks</h4>\n<p>Match each risk to the question it answers.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Risk</th>\n<th>The question</th>\n<th>Who is most exposed</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Interest-rate risk</td>\n<td>What happens to the bond's price when market yields change?</td>\n<td>Long-maturity, low-coupon bonds</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Purchasing-power risk</td>\n<td>What will the fixed nominal cash flows buy after inflation?</td>\n<td>Long-term holders of fixed coupons</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Business or financial risk</td>\n<td>Can and will the issuer pay coupons and principal?</td>\n<td>Lower-rated, highly leveraged issuers</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Liquidity risk</td>\n<td>Can I sell promptly at a reasonable price?</td>\n<td>Thinly traded issues</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Call risk</td>\n<td>Will the issuer repay early, when rates have fallen?</td>\n<td>Callable, high-coupon bonds</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>A bond can be strong on one risk and weak on another. A highly rated long bond has low default risk but high interest-rate risk. Call risk also brings <strong>reinvestment risk</strong>, because the proceeds must be reinvested at lower rates.</p>\n<h4>How rating agencies assess credit risk</h4>\n<p>Agencies give an <strong>opinion</strong>, not a guarantee, of the issuer's ability and willingness to meet its obligations. They examine:\n- the issuer's cash flow, leverage and ability to cover interest, and its refinancing needs;\n- the contract: security or collateral, seniority and covenants;\n- the business and economic environment.</p>\n<p>The result is a letter grade. In Malaysia the main agencies are RAM and MARC; internationally they include S&amp;P and Moody's. Ratings of $\\text{BBB}-$ (S&amp;P) or $\\text{Baa3}$ (Moody's) and above are <strong>investment grade</strong>; anything lower is <strong>below investment grade</strong> (high yield). Agencies can disagree (a split rating) and ratings can change.</p>\n<h4>Why lower-rated bonds have wider spreads</h4>\n<p>The <strong>credit spread</strong> is the yield on the corporate bond minus the yield on a government bond of the same maturity. For example, $5.5\\% - 4.0\\% = 1.5$ percentage points, or $150$ basis points.</p>\n<p>A weaker issuer has a higher chance of default and a lower recovery. Investors therefore demand a higher promised yield as compensation, which means a wider spread. The spread also reflects liquidity, tax and options, so it is not a pure default measure. When the market sees more credit risk, spreads widen and prices fall.</p>\n<h4>Limitation</h4>\n<p>A high promised yield is not a high expected return. Defaults, recoveries and trading costs can cancel it.</p>\n<p><strong>Conclusion:</strong> each risk answers a different question, and lower credit quality is paid for with a wider spread because investors need a larger risk premium.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question tut-q\" data-tut-ch=\"10 11\" data-tut-t=\"Calculation\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q6</span><div><div class=\"tut-tags\"><span class=\"tut-topic\">Topics 8 and 9 · Q6</span><span class=\"tut-type tut-calculation\">Calculation</span><span class=\"tut-also\">Also Ch 11</span></div><strong class=\"tut-title\">Current yield and holding-period return</strong></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body markdown-body compact\"><p>A bond has a par value of $\\text{RM}1{,}000$ and a $6\\%$ annual coupon rate. It currently sells for $\\text{RM}960$. An investor buys it, receives one annual coupon and sells it one year later for $\\text{RM}985$.</p>\n<p>Calculate:</p>\n<p>(a) the annual coupon,</p>\n<p>(b) current yield at purchase,</p>\n<p>(c) capital gain, and</p>\n<p>(d) one-year holding-period return.</p>\n<p>Explain why current yield differs from total holding-period return.</p><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal the exam-ready answer</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><p><strong>Given:</strong> par $=\\text{RM}1{,}000$, coupon rate $=6\\%$, purchase price $P_0=\\text{RM}960$, sale price $P_1=\\text{RM}985$, one coupon received.</p>\n<h4>(a) Annual coupon</h4>\n<p><strong>Formula:</strong></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$C=\\text{coupon rate}\\times\\text{par}$$</div>\n<p><strong>Substitution:</strong></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$C=0.06\\times 1{,}000$$</div>\n<p><strong>Result:</strong></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{C=\\text{RM}60}$$</div>\n<h4>(b) Current yield at purchase</h4>\n<p><strong>Formula:</strong></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Current yield}=\\dfrac{C}{P_0}$$</div>\n<p><strong>Substitution:</strong></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Current yield}=\\dfrac{60}{960}$$</div>\n<p><strong>Result:</strong></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{Current yield}=6.25\\%}$$</div>\n<h4>(c) Capital gain</h4>\n<p><strong>Formula:</strong></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Capital gain}=P_1-P_0$$</div>\n<p><strong>Substitution:</strong></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Capital gain}=985-960$$</div>\n<p><strong>Result:</strong></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{Capital gain}=\\text{RM}25}$$</div>\n<h4>(d) One-year holding-period return</h4>\n<p><strong>Formula:</strong></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{HPR}=\\dfrac{C+(P_1-P_0)}{P_0}$$</div>\n<p><strong>Substitution:</strong></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{HPR}=\\dfrac{60+25}{960}=\\dfrac{85}{960}$$</div>\n<p><strong>Result:</strong></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{HPR}=8.85\\%}$$</div>\n<p>The income and price parts add up: $6.25\\%+2.60\\%=8.85\\%$.</p>\n<h4>Interpretation</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Current yield measures only the <strong>coupon income per ringgit of price paid</strong>. It is above the $6\\%$ coupon rate because the bond was bought at a discount.</li>\n<li>The HPR is the <strong>total return</strong>. It adds the price gain of $\\text{RM}25$, which current yield ignores.</li>\n<li>The HPR also depends on the <strong>sale price</strong>, which is uncertain when the investor buys. Had the bond sold for less than $\\text{RM}960$, the HPR would fall below the current yield. A positive coupon does not guarantee a positive total return.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Exam tip:</strong> do not call the current yield the investor's return. Divide the coupon by the price, not by par, and include the capital gain in the HPR.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question tut-q\" data-tut-ch=\"10\" data-tut-t=\"Reflection\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">R2</span><div><div class=\"tut-tags\"><span class=\"tut-topic\">Topics 8 and 9 · Reflection 2</span><span class=\"tut-type tut-reflection\">Reflection</span></div><strong class=\"tut-title\">Current yield and an incomplete picture of return</strong></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body markdown-body compact\"><p>Why can a high current yield give an incomplete picture of return?</p><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal the exam-ready answer</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><p>Current yield equals the annual coupon divided by today's price, so it ignores any gain or loss between purchase and sale or maturity and the timing of cash flows. A bond bought at a deep discount shows a high current yield, but a price fall or default can wipe out the income. In Question 6 the current yield was $6.25\\%$ but the holding-period return was $8.85\\%$ because of the price gain. The opposite can also happen. A high current yield often signals higher risk, which is why a bond's total return and yield to maturity should be examined as well.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question tut-q\" data-tut-ch=\"10 11\" data-tut-t=\"Reflection\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">R3</span><div><div class=\"tut-tags\"><span class=\"tut-topic\">Topics 8 and 9 · Reflection 3</span><span class=\"tut-type tut-reflection\">Reflection</span><span class=\"tut-also\">Also Ch 11</span></div><strong class=\"tut-title\">The valuation input to investigate before buying a corporate bond</strong></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body markdown-body compact\"><p>Which valuation input would you investigate most carefully before purchasing a corporate bond?</p><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal the exam-ready answer</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><p>I would investigate the <strong>required yield</strong>, which means the issuer's <strong>credit risk</strong>, most carefully. The coupon, par value and maturity are fixed in the contract and can be read from it. The required yield is the judgement that sets the price, and it depends on the issuer's ability to pay: cash flow, leverage and refinancing needs, plus the security, seniority and call features and the rating. A credit spread that looks attractive may simply compensate for a real chance of default. An error here changes the price much more than a small change in maturity.</p></div></details></div></details></div></div><div class=\"tut-group theme-l11\" data-tut-group=\"11\"><h3 class=\"tut-group-title\"><span>Chapter 11</span> Bond Valuation <small>7 questions</small></h3><div class=\"question-bank\"><details class=\"bank-question tut-q\" data-tut-ch=\"11\" data-tut-t=\"Concept\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q4</span><div><div class=\"tut-tags\"><span class=\"tut-topic\">Topics 8 and 9 · Q4</span><span class=\"tut-type tut-concept\">Concept</span></div><strong class=\"tut-title\">Yield curves and the term structure of interest rates</strong></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body markdown-body compact\"><p>Define the term structure of interest rates and a yield curve.</p>\n<p>Describe upward-sloping, inverted, flat and humped yield curves.</p>\n<p>Compare the expectations hypothesis, liquidity preference theory and market segmentation theory as explanations of the curve's shape.</p><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal the exam-ready answer</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><h4>Definitions</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>The <strong>term structure of interest rates</strong> is the relationship between the yield on a bond and its time to maturity.</li>\n<li>A <strong>yield curve</strong> is its graph: yields of comparable bonds, for example government securities, plotted against maturity <strong>at one date</strong>. It is a snapshot, not the history of one bond.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Shapes</h4>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Shape</th>\n<th>Description</th>\n<th>What it typically signals</th>\n<th>What it does not prove</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Upward-sloping (normal)</td>\n<td>Long yields above short yields</td>\n<td>Rate rises expected, or a term premium</td>\n<td>That rates must rise</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Inverted</td>\n<td>Short yields above long yields</td>\n<td>Rate falls or a slowdown expected, or tight short-term money</td>\n<td>That a recession is certain</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Flat</td>\n<td>Similar yields at every maturity</td>\n<td>A transition, with little change expected</td>\n<td>That rates will stay unchanged</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Humped</td>\n<td>Middle maturities yield the most <em>(beyond the chapter notes)</em></td>\n<td>Rates expected to rise and then fall (mid-term expectations)</td>\n<td>An exact rate path</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h4>Three theories</h4>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Theory</th>\n<th>Explanation</th>\n<th>Upward slope arises when</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Expectations hypothesis</td>\n<td>A long yield is related to the expected future short rates</td>\n<td>Short rates are expected to rise</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Liquidity preference</td>\n<td>Investors want extra compensation for holding longer, more price-uncertain bonds, a positive term premium</td>\n<td>There is a term premium, even if rates are expected to be stable</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Market segmentation</td>\n<td>Borrowers and lenders prefer particular maturities, so each maturity's supply and demand sets its yield</td>\n<td>Investor demand for long-term bonds is weak or the supply of long-term bonds is large</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Under strict segmentation the curve need not reveal expectations. In practice, all three mechanisms can operate together.</p>\n<h4>Evaluation</h4>\n<p>An upward slope is consistent with expected rate rises, but a term premium or maturity-specific supply and demand can produce it too. So the curve alone does not establish future rates.</p>\n<p><strong>Conclusion:</strong> the curve is one date's snapshot of yields by maturity. Its shape is evidence about expectations, premiums and segmentation, not a certain forecast.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question tut-q\" data-tut-ch=\"11\" data-tut-t=\"Concept\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q5</span><div><div class=\"tut-tags\"><span class=\"tut-topic\">Topics 8 and 9 · Q5</span><span class=\"tut-type tut-concept\">Concept</span></div><strong class=\"tut-title\">Bond valuation and compounding conventions</strong></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body markdown-body compact\"><p>Explain why a bond's price equals the present value of its future coupon payments and principal.</p>\n<p>Identify the required inputs for valuation.</p>\n<p>Describe how the calculation changes when coupons are paid semiannually rather than annually, and explain why consistency between cash-flow frequency and discount-rate frequency is essential.</p><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal the exam-ready answer</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><h4>Why price is a present value</h4>\n<p>A bond is a claim on a known set of future cash flows. A ringgit received later is worth less than one today, so an investor will pay only the present value of those flows at the return the market requires:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$P_0=\\sum_{t=1}^{N}\\dfrac{C}{(1+y)^t}+\\dfrac{F}{(1+y)^N}$$</div>\n<p>In annuity form this is:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$P_0=C\\left[\\dfrac{1-(1+y)^{-N}}{y}\\right]+\\dfrac{F}{(1+y)^N}.$$</div>\n<p>The price is the present value of the coupon annuity plus the present value of par.</p>\n<h4>Required inputs</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>$C$: the coupon, equal to the coupon rate $\\times$ par;</li>\n<li>$F$: the par value repaid at maturity;</li>\n<li>$N$: the number of years to maturity;</li>\n<li>$y$: the required market yield for a bond of that risk, together with the payment frequency.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The calculation assumes the payments are made as promised and the bond is noncallable.</p>\n<h4>Annual versus semiannual coupons</h4>\n<p>Change all three inputs together: <strong>halve, halve, double</strong>.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Input</th>\n<th>Annual</th>\n<th>Semiannual</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Coupon per period</td>\n<td>$C$</td>\n<td>$C/2$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Yield per period</td>\n<td>$y$</td>\n<td>$y/2$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Number of periods</td>\n<td>$N$</td>\n<td>$2N$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$P_0=\\sum_{t=1}^{2N}\\dfrac{C/2}{(1+y/2)^t}+\\dfrac{F}{(1+y/2)^{2N}}.$$</div>\n<h4>Why consistency matters</h4>\n<p>The discount rate must apply over the same interval as the cash flow it discounts. A mismatch gives a wrong price. For the $4.5\\%$ bond in Question 10, halving the coupon to $\\text{RM}22.50$ but discounting $40$ periods at the full $5\\%$ gives only $\\text{RM}528.13$ instead of $\\text{RM}937.24$. If a question gives an effective annual yield, the six-month rate is $(1+y_{\\text{eff}})^{1/2}-1$, not half the annual rate.</p>\n<p><strong>Conclusion:</strong> price is the present value of coupons and par, and every input must be on the same time basis as the cash flows.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question tut-q\" data-tut-ch=\"11 10\" data-tut-t=\"Calculation\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q7</span><div><div class=\"tut-tags\"><span class=\"tut-topic\">Topics 8 and 9 · Q7</span><span class=\"tut-type tut-calculation\">Calculation</span><span class=\"tut-also\">Also Ch 10</span></div><strong class=\"tut-title\">Interest-rate sensitivity and maturity</strong></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body markdown-body compact\"><p>Bond A and Bond B each have $\\text{RM}1{,}000$ par value and a $6\\%$ annual coupon. Bond A matures in $5$ years and Bond B in $15$ years.</p>\n<p>(a) Estimate each price when the market yield is $4\\%$.</p>\n<p>(b) Revalue each bond when the yield rises to $5\\%$.</p>\n<p>(c) Calculate each percentage price change.</p>\n<p>(d) Explain the difference and identify the bond that exposes the investor to greater interest-rate risk.</p><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal the exam-ready answer</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><p><strong>Given:</strong> $F=\\text{RM}1{,}000$, $C=0.06\\times 1{,}000=\\text{RM}60$, annual coupons. Bond A has $N=5$ and Bond B has $N=15$. The yield is $y=4\\%$ and then $y=5\\%$.</p>\n<p><strong>Formula</strong> (annuity-factor form):</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$P_0=C\\left[\\dfrac{1-(1+y)^{-N}}{y}\\right]+\\dfrac{F}{(1+y)^N}$$</div>\n<h4>(a) Prices at $y=4\\%$</h4>\n<p><strong>Bond A</strong> ($N=5$):</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\begin{aligned}\\text{annuity term}&amp;=60\\left[\\dfrac{1-1.04^{-5}}{0.04}\\right]=60(4.4518)=267.1093\\\\ \\text{par term}&amp;=\\dfrac{1{,}000}{1.04^{5}}=1{,}000(0.8219)=821.9271\\\\ P_A&amp;=267.1093+821.9271\\end{aligned}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{P_A=\\text{RM}1{,}089.04}$$</div>\n<p><strong>Bond B</strong> ($N=15$):</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\begin{aligned}\\text{annuity term}&amp;=60\\left[\\dfrac{1-1.04^{-15}}{0.04}\\right]=60(11.1184)=667.1032\\\\ \\text{par term}&amp;=\\dfrac{1{,}000}{1.04^{15}}=1{,}000(0.5553)=555.2645\\\\ P_B&amp;=667.1032+555.2645\\end{aligned}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{P_B=\\text{RM}1{,}222.37}$$</div>\n<h4>(b) Prices at $y=5\\%$</h4>\n<p><strong>Bond A:</strong></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\begin{aligned}\\text{annuity term}&amp;=60\\left[\\dfrac{1-1.05^{-5}}{0.05}\\right]=60(4.3295)=259.7686\\\\ \\text{par term}&amp;=\\dfrac{1{,}000}{1.05^{5}}=1{,}000(0.7835)=783.5262\\\\ P_A&amp;=259.7686+783.5262\\end{aligned}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{P_A=\\text{RM}1{,}043.29}$$</div>\n<p><strong>Bond B:</strong></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\begin{aligned}\\text{annuity term}&amp;=60\\left[\\dfrac{1-1.05^{-15}}{0.05}\\right]=60(10.3797)=622.7795\\\\ \\text{par term}&amp;=\\dfrac{1{,}000}{1.05^{15}}=1{,}000(0.4810)=481.0171\\\\ P_B&amp;=622.7795+481.0171\\end{aligned}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{P_B=\\text{RM}1{,}103.80}$$</div>\n<p>The annuity and discount factors are shown to four decimals; each term is computed from the unrounded factor, then the price is rounded to two decimals. The changes in part (c) use the rounded prices.</p>\n<h4>(c) Percentage price change</h4>\n<p><strong>Formula:</strong></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\%\\Delta P=\\dfrac{P_{5\\%}-P_{4\\%}}{P_{4\\%}}$$</div>\n<p><strong>Substitution and result:</strong></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\%\\Delta P_A=\\dfrac{1{,}043.29-1{,}089.04}{1{,}089.04}=\\dfrac{-45.75}{1{,}089.04}\\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad\\boxed{-4.20\\%}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\%\\Delta P_B=\\dfrac{1{,}103.80-1{,}222.37}{1{,}222.37}=\\dfrac{-118.57}{1{,}222.37}\\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad\\boxed{-9.70\\%}$$</div>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th></th>\n<th class=\"ta-r\">Price at $4\\%$</th>\n<th class=\"ta-r\">Price at $5\\%$</th>\n<th class=\"ta-r\">Change</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Bond A ($5$ years)</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">$\\text{RM}1{,}089.04$</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">$\\text{RM}1{,}043.29$</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">$-4.20\\%$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Bond B ($15$ years)</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">$\\text{RM}1{,}222.37$</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">$\\text{RM}1{,}103.80$</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">$-9.70\\%$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h4>(d) Interpretation</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Both prices <strong>fall</strong> when the yield rises, because the fixed $\\text{RM}60$ coupons are discounted at a higher rate. Both bonds sell at a <strong>premium</strong> at these yields, since the $6\\%$ coupon rate exceeds $y$.</li>\n<li>Bond B falls by about $2.3$ times as much as Bond A. Its cash flows, including the $\\text{RM}1{,}000$ principal, are further away, so the higher rate is compounded over $15$ periods rather than $5$.</li>\n<li><strong>Bond B has the greater interest-rate risk.</strong> The investor who holds it faces a larger loss if yields rise, and a larger gain if yields fall.</li>\n<li>Holding to maturity returns par, but an investor who must sell early bears the price loss.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Exam tip:</strong> the common mistake is to change the coupon when the yield changes. The coupon stays $\\text{RM}60$ and only the discount rate moves. Also keep the final cash flow as the coupon plus par.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question tut-q\" data-tut-ch=\"11\" data-tut-t=\"Concept\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q8</span><div><div class=\"tut-tags\"><span class=\"tut-topic\">Topics 8 and 9 · Q8</span><span class=\"tut-type tut-concept\">Concept</span></div><strong class=\"tut-title\">Interpreting a yield curve for investment decisions</strong></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body markdown-body compact\"><p>Government securities currently yield $3.2\\%$ at $1$ year, $3.6\\%$ at $3$ years, $4.0\\%$ at $5$ years and $4.5\\%$ at $10$ years.</p>\n<p>(a) Identify the curve's shape.</p>\n<p>(b) Explain the curve using each of the three term-structure theories.</p>\n<p>(c) If an investor expects inflation and market yields to rise soon, recommend a maturity range and justify it.</p>\n<p>(d) State one limitation of treating the yield curve as a certain forecast.</p><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal the exam-ready answer</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><p><strong>Given:</strong></p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Maturity</th>\n<th class=\"ta-r\">$1$ year</th>\n<th class=\"ta-r\">$3$ years</th>\n<th class=\"ta-r\">$5$ years</th>\n<th class=\"ta-r\">$10$ years</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Yield</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">$3.2\\%$</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">$3.6\\%$</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">$4.0\\%$</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">$4.5\\%$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h4>(a) Shape</h4>\n<p>Yields rise steadily with maturity, so the curve is <strong>upward-sloping (normal)</strong>. Over the whole curve:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$4.5\\%-3.2\\%=1.3\\text{ percentage points}\\;=\\;130\\text{ basis points}$$</div>\n<p>The slope flattens at the long end: $0.4$ percentage points from year $1$ to year $3$ and from year $3$ to year $5$, then $0.5$ over the five years from year $5$ to year $10$.</p>\n<h4>(b) Explanation by theory</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Expectations hypothesis:</strong> long yields reflect expected future short rates. The rising curve implies the market expects short rates to rise. <em>(beyond the chapter notes)</em> With annual compounding, the implied average rate between years $1$ and $3$ is $\\left[\\dfrac{1.036^{3}}{1.032}\\right]^{1/2}-1\\approx 3.8\\%$, and the implied average rate rises further for later periods.</li>\n<li><strong>Liquidity preference:</strong> investors need a term premium for the greater price risk of longer bonds. Part of the higher yield at $10$ years may be this premium, even if short rates are not expected to rise.</li>\n<li><strong>Market segmentation:</strong> each maturity has its own supply and demand. Heavy demand for short securities from liquidity-seeking institutions and more supply at long maturities would produce lower short and higher long yields.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>(c) Recommendation</h4>\n<p>For an investor who expects inflation and yields to rise soon, <strong>favour the short end, about $1$ to $3$ years.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Smaller price loss.</strong> Short bonds are less price-sensitive. As an illustration, a $1$ percentage point rise in each yield cuts the price of a zero-coupon bond by about $0.96\\%$ at $1$ year, $2.84\\%$ at $3$ years and $9.08\\%$ at $10$ years.</li>\n<li><strong>Reinvestment at higher rates.</strong> Short bonds mature soon, and the proceeds can be reinvested at the higher yields.</li>\n<li><strong>Cost.</strong> The investor gives up yield: $3.2\\%$ to $3.6\\%$ instead of $4.5\\%$. This is the price of protection if the forecast is right.</li>\n<li>If the forecast is wrong and yields fall, the investor misses the price gains on long bonds.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>(d) Limitation</h4>\n<p>The curve is not a certain forecast. Its slope can reflect a term premium or segmentation as well as expected rate rises, so the market's expected path cannot be separated from risk premiums. A single curve also reflects the market's average view, which can be wrong.</p>\n<h4>Interpretation</h4>\n<p>The curve is normal, which fits all three theories. A defensive investor expecting higher yields should shorten maturity or duration, accepting a lower yield now for less price risk.</p>\n<p><strong>Exam tip:</strong> do not say an upward-sloping curve proves rates will rise, and justify the maturity choice with both price risk and reinvestment.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question tut-q\" data-tut-ch=\"11\" data-tut-t=\"Calculation\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q9</span><div><div class=\"tut-tags\"><span class=\"tut-topic\">Topics 8 and 9 · Q9</span><span class=\"tut-type tut-calculation\">Calculation</span></div><strong class=\"tut-title\">Valuing an annual-coupon bond</strong></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body markdown-body compact\"><p>A bond has $\\text{RM}1{,}000$ par value, $20$ years to maturity and a $4.5\\%$ annual coupon rate. The required market yield is $5\\%$ with annual compounding.</p>\n<p>Calculate the annual coupon, the present value of the coupon annuity, the present value of principal and the bond's estimated price.</p>\n<p>State whether it should trade at a premium or discount and explain why.</p><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal the exam-ready answer</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><p><strong>Given:</strong> $F=\\text{RM}1{,}000$, $N=20$, coupon rate $=4.5\\%$, $y=5\\%$, annual coupons.</p>\n<h4>Annual coupon</h4>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$C=\\text{coupon rate}\\times F=0.045\\times 1{,}000$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{C=\\text{RM}45}$$</div>\n<h4>Present value of the coupon annuity</h4>\n<p><strong>Formula:</strong></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$PV_{\\text{coupons}}=C\\left[\\dfrac{1-(1+y)^{-N}}{y}\\right]$$</div>\n<p><strong>Substitution:</strong></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$PV_{\\text{coupons}}=45\\left[\\dfrac{1-1.05^{-20}}{0.05}\\right]=45(12.4622)$$</div>\n<p><strong>Result:</strong></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{PV_{\\text{coupons}}=\\text{RM}560.80}$$</div>\n<h4>Present value of principal</h4>\n<p><strong>Formula:</strong></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$PV_{\\text{par}}=\\dfrac{F}{(1+y)^N}$$</div>\n<p><strong>Substitution:</strong></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$PV_{\\text{par}}=\\dfrac{1{,}000}{1.05^{20}}=1{,}000(0.37689)$$</div>\n<p><strong>Result:</strong></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{PV_{\\text{par}}=\\text{RM}376.89}$$</div>\n<h4>Estimated price</h4>\n<p><strong>Formula:</strong></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$P_0=PV_{\\text{coupons}}+PV_{\\text{par}}$$</div>\n<p><strong>Substitution:</strong></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$P_0=560.80+376.89$$</div>\n<p><strong>Result:</strong></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{P_0=\\text{RM}937.69}$$</div>\n<h4>Interpretation</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>The bond should trade at a <strong>discount</strong>: $\\text{RM}937.69$ is below par, by $\\text{RM}62.31$.</li>\n<li>The reason is that the coupon rate of $4.5\\%$ is <strong>below</strong> the required yield of $5\\%$. The bond pays less than comparable bonds, so buyers will pay only a price below par. The discount is the extra return from the capital gain to par that makes up the shortfall.</li>\n<li>Check the ordering for a discount bond: coupon rate $4.5\\% &lt;$ current yield $\\tfrac{45}{937.69}=4.80\\% &lt;$ yield to maturity $5\\%$.</li>\n<li>A discount is compensation for a lower coupon, not automatically a bargain.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Exam tip:</strong> the common mistake is to discount only the coupons and forget the $\\text{RM}1{,}000$ principal at year $20$, or to discount par with the annuity factor. Use the annuity factor for the coupons and the single discount factor for par.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question tut-q\" data-tut-ch=\"11\" data-tut-t=\"Calculation\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q10</span><div><div class=\"tut-tags\"><span class=\"tut-topic\">Topics 8 and 9 · Q10</span><span class=\"tut-type tut-calculation\">Calculation</span></div><strong class=\"tut-title\">Valuing a semiannual-coupon bond</strong></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body markdown-body compact\"><p>Use the same $\\text{RM}1{,}000$ par value, $20$-year maturity, $4.5\\%$ coupon bond, but assume coupons are paid semiannually and the quoted market yield is $5\\%$ compounded semiannually.</p>\n<p>(a) Identify the coupon per half-year, periodic yield and number of periods.</p>\n<p>(b) Calculate the bond price.</p>\n<p>(c) Compare it with the annual-coupon value in Question 9.</p>\n<p>(d) Explain what happens to the price as maturity approaches if the required yield remains at $5\\%$.</p><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal the exam-ready answer</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><p><strong>Given:</strong> $F=\\text{RM}1{,}000$, $N=20$ years, annual coupon $C=\\text{RM}45$, nominal yield $y=5\\%$ compounded semiannually.</p>\n<h4>(a) Periodic inputs (halve, halve, double)</h4>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\dfrac{C}{2}=\\dfrac{45}{2}=\\text{RM}22.50,\\qquad \\dfrac{y}{2}=\\dfrac{5\\%}{2}=2.5\\%,\\qquad 2N=2\\times 20=40\\text{ periods}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\dfrac{C}{2}=\\text{RM}22.50}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\dfrac{y}{2}=2.5\\%}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{2N=40\\text{ half-years}}$$</div>\n<h4>(b) Bond price</h4>\n<p><strong>Formula</strong> (annuity-factor form):</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$P_0=\\dfrac{C}{2}\\left[\\dfrac{1-(1+y/2)^{-2N}}{y/2}\\right]+\\dfrac{F}{(1+y/2)^{2N}}$$</div>\n<p><strong>Substitution:</strong></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\begin{aligned}\\text{annuity term}&amp;=22.50\\left[\\dfrac{1-1.025^{-40}}{0.025}\\right]=22.50(25.1028)=564.81\\\\ \\text{par term}&amp;=\\dfrac{1{,}000}{1.025^{40}}=1{,}000(0.37243)=372.43\\\\ P_0&amp;=564.81+372.43\\end{aligned}$$</div>\n<p><strong>Result:</strong></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{P_0=\\text{RM}937.24}$$</div>\n<h4>(c) Comparison with Question 9</h4>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th></th>\n<th class=\"ta-r\">Annual coupons (Q9)</th>\n<th class=\"ta-r\">Semiannual coupons (Q10)</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Coupon per period</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">$\\text{RM}45$</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">$\\text{RM}22.50$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Yield per period</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">$5\\%$</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">$2.5\\%$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Periods</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">$20$</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">$40$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Price</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">$\\text{RM}937.69$</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">$\\text{RM}937.24$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The semiannual bond is worth $\\text{RM}0.45$ less. A nominal $5\\%$ compounded semiannually is an effective annual yield of $1.025^{2}-1=5.0625\\%$, slightly higher than $5\\%$. The higher required yield lowers the price by more than the earlier coupons raise it. Both bonds sell at a <strong>discount</strong>, because the coupon rate of $4.5\\%$ is below the yield.</p>\n<h4>(d) Price as maturity approaches</h4>\n<p>If the required yield stays at $5\\%$, the discount <strong>shrinks and the price rises towards par</strong>. Fewer periods remain over which the coupon shortfall of $0.5$ percentage points applies, so the present value of that shortfall falls. At maturity the price equals par:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Years to maturity</th>\n<th class=\"ta-r\">$20$</th>\n<th class=\"ta-r\">$15$</th>\n<th class=\"ta-r\">$10$</th>\n<th class=\"ta-r\">$5$</th>\n<th class=\"ta-r\">$1$</th>\n<th class=\"ta-r\">$0$</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Price at $y=5\\%$</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">$\\text{RM}937.24$</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">$\\text{RM}947.67$</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">$\\text{RM}961.03$</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">$\\text{RM}978.12$</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">$\\text{RM}995.18$</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">$\\text{RM}1{,}000$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>This \"pull to par\" is the capital gain that, together with the coupons, gives the investor the $5\\%$ yield. It assumes the required yield is unchanged, the issuer pays in full and the bond is not called.</p>\n<p><strong>Exam tip:</strong> the common mistake is to halve the coupon but keep $5\\%$ and $20$ periods. Halve the coupon and the yield, and double the periods.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question tut-q\" data-tut-ch=\"11 10\" data-tut-t=\"Reflection\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">R1</span><div><div class=\"tut-tags\"><span class=\"tut-topic\">Topics 8 and 9 · Reflection 1</span><span class=\"tut-type tut-reflection\">Reflection</span><span class=\"tut-also\">Also Ch 10</span></div><strong class=\"tut-title\">Bond risk when yields are expected to rise</strong></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body markdown-body compact\"><p>Which bond risk matters most when market yields are expected to rise?</p><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal the exam-ready answer</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><p><strong>Interest-rate risk</strong> matters most. A rise in market yields lowers the price of existing fixed-coupon bonds, because their fixed cash flows are discounted at a higher rate, and the effect is larger for long maturities (Question 7: $-9.70\\%$ for a $15$-year bond against $-4.20\\%$ for a $5$-year bond). An investor who may have to sell before maturity bears this loss directly. Rising yields are often driven by higher expected inflation, so purchasing-power risk grows too. The risk can be reduced by shortening maturity or duration, while rising rates also let maturing funds be reinvested at higher yields.</p></div></details></div></details></div></div><div class=\"tut-group theme-l12\" data-tut-group=\"12\"><h3 class=\"tut-group-title\"><span>Chapter 12</span> Mutual Funds and Exchange-Traded Funds <small>6 questions</small></h3><div class=\"question-bank\"><details class=\"bank-question tut-q\" data-tut-ch=\"12 5\" data-tut-t=\"Concept\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q1</span><div><div class=\"tut-tags\"><span class=\"tut-topic\">Topics 10 and 11 · Q1</span><span class=\"tut-type tut-concept\">Concept</span><span class=\"tut-also\">Also Ch 5</span></div><strong class=\"tut-title\">The mutual fund concept and management style</strong></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body markdown-body compact\"><p>Explain pooled diversification and how an investor acquires a proportionate interest in a mutual fund portfolio. Compare active and passive management in terms of objective, security selection, expected costs and the benchmark used to judge performance. Why may diversification still leave an investor exposed to market risk?</p><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal the exam-ready answer</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><h4>Pooled diversification and proportionate ownership</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>A <strong>mutual fund</strong> pools money from many investors and invests it under a stated mandate. If $100$ investors each contribute $\\text{RM}1{,}000$, the fund holds $\\text{RM}100{,}000$ and each investor, with equal holdings, has a claim on $1\\%$ of the fund's net assets.</li>\n<li>The investor buys <strong>shares (units) in the fund</strong>, not direct legal title to each underlying stock or bond. The number of fund shares bought is the amount invested divided by the net asset value (NAV) per share, so ownership is proportionate:</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{investor's share of the fund} = \\dfrac{\\text{fund shares held}}{\\text{total fund shares outstanding}}$$</div>\n<ul>\n<li>Pooling gives access to many securities at a small ticket size, professional management and administration, which a small investor could not replicate directly. Holding many securities spreads <strong>company-specific (unsystematic) risk</strong>, because one firm's failure is only a small part of the pool.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Active versus passive management</h4>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Feature</th>\n<th>Active management</th>\n<th>Passive (index) management</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Objective</td>\n<td>Beat a benchmark after costs through manager decisions</td>\n<td>Match the return of a defined index, less unavoidable costs</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Security selection</td>\n<td>Research, forecasting and security selection or timing by the manager</td>\n<td>Rules-based holding of the index constituents (or a sample)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Expected costs</td>\n<td>Higher: research, turnover, trading costs and a higher expense ratio</td>\n<td>Lower: little research, low turnover and a low expense ratio</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Benchmark</td>\n<td>An index matching the fund's mandate and risk, judged after costs</td>\n<td>The index it tracks; the gap is the <strong>tracking difference</strong></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<ul>\n<li>Outperformance after costs is <strong>not assured</strong> for an active fund, and an index fund is expected to trail its index by roughly its costs (tracking difference from sampling, cash holdings and timing can push it either way). A broad equity index is a poor benchmark for a short-term bond fund, so the benchmark must match the mandate.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Why diversification does not remove market risk</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Diversification removes <strong>unsystematic</strong> risk only. <strong>Systematic (market) risk</strong> is common to all holdings, so in a broad market downturn all holdings tend to fall together and the fund's NAV falls.</li>\n<li>A fund can also hold many securities yet be concentrated, for example a sector fund whose companies share one risk. The number of holdings is not the same as effective diversification.</li>\n<li>A bond fund carries interest-rate and credit risk, and a fund is not a guarantee of return.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Conclusion:</strong> a fund gives proportionate, diversified, professionally managed ownership. Active and passive styles differ in objective, cost and benchmark, and neither removes market risk.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question tut-q\" data-tut-ch=\"12\" data-tut-t=\"Concept\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q2</span><div><div class=\"tut-tags\"><span class=\"tut-topic\">Topics 10 and 11 · Q2</span><span class=\"tut-type tut-concept\">Concept</span></div><strong class=\"tut-title\">Open-end funds, closed-end funds and ETFs</strong></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body markdown-body compact\"><p>Compare open-end mutual funds, closed-end funds and exchange-traded funds. Address how shares are created or redeemed, where and when investors trade, whether transaction price must equal net asset value, and how investor withdrawals can affect portfolio management. Explain the meaning of a premium or discount to NAV.</p><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal the exam-ready answer</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><h4>Key definitions</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>NAV per share</strong> $=\\dfrac{\\text{assets} - \\text{liabilities}}{\\text{shares outstanding}}$, the value of one fund share's claim on net assets.</li>\n<li><strong>Open-end fund:</strong> the fund itself issues and redeems shares. <strong>Closed-end fund:</strong> a fixed pool of shares that trade on an exchange. <strong>ETF:</strong> a fund whose shares trade on an exchange and are created or redeemed in large blocks by authorized participants.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Comparison</h4>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Feature</th>\n<th>Open-end fund</th>\n<th>Closed-end fund</th>\n<th>ETF</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Creation and redemption</td>\n<td>The fund issues new shares for new money and redeems shares on request</td>\n<td>Share count is largely fixed; no routine redemption</td>\n<td>Authorized participants create or redeem large creation units for a basket of securities and/or cash</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Where and when</td>\n<td>Bought from the fund or an intermediary, priced at the next calculated NAV</td>\n<td>On an exchange during market hours</td>\n<td>On an exchange during market hours</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Price versus NAV</td>\n<td>Transactions at NAV; the purchase price may be NAV plus a load</td>\n<td>Market price set by supply and demand; may differ materially from NAV</td>\n<td>Market price can differ from NAV, but creation and redemption keep it close</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Effect of withdrawals</td>\n<td>Redemptions may force the manager to hold cash or sell securities, which can disrupt strategy</td>\n<td>The manager is not forced to sell, as investors sell to other investors</td>\n<td>Large redemptions are usually met by delivering securities, so forced selling is limited</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h4>Premium and discount to NAV</h4>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{premium or discount} = \\dfrac{\\text{market price} - NAV}{NAV}$$</div>\n<ul>\n<li>A positive value is a <strong>premium</strong> and a negative value is a <strong>discount</strong>. For example, NAV $\\text{RM}10$ and price $\\text{RM}9$ gives a $10\\%$ discount.</li>\n<li>It applies to closed-end funds and ETFs, because only these have an exchange price that can drift from NAV. For an ETF, an authorized participant can profit from a gap by creating or redeeming, which tends to close it.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Limitation:</strong> a discount is not proof of a bargain. It can persist or widen, so check fees, holdings and risks. Liquidity depends on the spread and volume, not on the structure alone.</p>\n<p><strong>Conclusion:</strong> open-end funds deal at NAV with the fund, while closed-end funds and ETFs trade on exchanges and can show a premium or discount to NAV.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question tut-q\" data-tut-ch=\"12\" data-tut-t=\"Concept\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q3</span><div><div class=\"tut-tags\"><span class=\"tut-topic\">Topics 10 and 11 · Q3</span><span class=\"tut-type tut-concept\">Concept</span></div><strong class=\"tut-title\">Fund benefits, costs and investor suitability</strong></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body markdown-body compact\"><p>Evaluate the main attractions and drawbacks of professionally managed funds. Distinguish sales loads, back-end loads, management fees, administrative expenses and the expense ratio. Explain why a fund with a strong gross return may still produce a disappointing investor return, and match growth, value, equity-income, balanced, bond and index funds to suitable investor objectives.</p><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal the exam-ready answer</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><h4>Attractions and drawbacks</h4>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Attractions</th>\n<th>Drawbacks</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Diversification across many securities for a small amount</td>\n<td>Fees and expenses reduce return, and a load cuts the amount invested</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Professional management and research</td>\n<td>Skill is not guaranteed; the fund may lag a suitable benchmark</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Convenience: record keeping, reinvestment, automatic plans</td>\n<td>No control over holdings or the timing of distributions</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Liquidity and low minimum investments</td>\n<td>Fund risk remains: market, credit, interest-rate and concentration risk</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Verdict:</strong> professionally managed funds are worth it when diversification and convenience outweigh the costs; judge performance net of fees against a matching benchmark.</p>\n<h4>Fund charges</h4>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Charge</th>\n<th>Meaning</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Sales (front-end) load</td>\n<td>Commission deducted when buying, so only $\\text{amount paid} \\times (1 - L)$ is invested</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Back-end load</td>\n<td>Deferred sales charge paid on selling, according to the fund's terms</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Management fee</td>\n<td>Pays the investment adviser for running the portfolio</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Administrative expenses</td>\n<td>Operating costs: record keeping, custody, legal and audit</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Expense ratio</td>\n<td>Annual operating expenses as a percentage of average net assets; it is reflected in NAV and the investor does not see a separate bill</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>A <strong>no-load</strong> fund has no sales load but still charges ongoing expenses.</p>\n<h4>Why a strong gross return can disappoint</h4>\n<p>The investor earns the return <strong>after</strong> the load and the annual expense ratio, measured on the money paid. Example from the notes: $\\text{RM}10{,}000$ with a $3\\%$ load invests $\\text{RM}9{,}700$. With $5\\%$ growth it becomes $\\text{RM}10{,}185$, so the investor's return is $1.85\\%$, not $5\\%$. Small annual cost differences also compound over time.</p>\n<h4>Matching funds to objectives <em>(the categories are in the chapter notes; the suitability column is beyond the chapter notes)</em></h4>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Fund type</th>\n<th>Suitable objective</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Growth</td>\n<td>Long-term capital appreciation; tolerates high volatility</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Value</td>\n<td>A patient, long-horizon investor seeking capital growth from under-priced shares who can tolerate a long wait, accepting that cheapness may be deserved</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Equity income</td>\n<td>Regular income from dividend-paying shares, such as a retiree</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Balanced</td>\n<td>A moderate mix of growth and income in one fund</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Bond</td>\n<td>Income and lower volatility; check duration and credit quality</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Index</td>\n<td>Low-cost market exposure for a long-term, passive investor</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Conclusion:</strong> judge a fund by its return <strong>net of all costs</strong> against the right benchmark, and by fit with the investor's goal, horizon and risk tolerance.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question tut-q\" data-tut-ch=\"12\" data-tut-t=\"Calculation\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q6</span><div><div class=\"tut-tags\"><span class=\"tut-topic\">Topics 10 and 11 · Q6</span><span class=\"tut-type tut-calculation\">Calculation</span></div><strong class=\"tut-title\">NAV and closed-end fund discount</strong></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body markdown-body compact\"><p>A fund holds securities with a market value of $\\text{RM}52$ million, has liabilities of $\\text{RM}2$ million and $5$ million shares outstanding.</p>\n<p>(a) Calculate NAV per share.</p>\n<p>(b) If it is an open-end fund, state the approximate transaction value before any sales charge.</p>\n<p>(c) If it is a closed-end fund trading at $\\text{RM}9.40$, calculate the percentage discount to NAV.</p>\n<p>(d) Explain why market price may remain below NAV.</p><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal the exam-ready answer</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><h4>(a) NAV per share</h4>\n<p><strong>Given:</strong> assets $\\text{RM}52$ million, liabilities $\\text{RM}2$ million, $5$ million shares.</p>\n<p><strong>Formula:</strong></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$NAV = \\dfrac{\\text{assets} - \\text{liabilities}}{\\text{shares outstanding}}$$</div>\n<p><strong>Substitution:</strong></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$NAV = \\dfrac{\\text{RM}52\\text{m} - \\text{RM}2\\text{m}}{5\\text{m}} = \\dfrac{\\text{RM}50\\text{m}}{5\\text{m}}$$</div>\n<p><strong>Result:</strong></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{NAV = \\text{RM}10.00\\text{ per share}}$$</div>\n<p><strong>Interpretation:</strong> net assets are $\\text{RM}50$ million, so each share has a $\\text{RM}10.00$ claim on them.</p>\n<h4>(b) Open-end transaction value</h4>\n<p>An open-end fund deals with investors at NAV. The approximate transaction value is therefore</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{RM}10.00\\text{ per share}}$$</div>\n<p>For example, $1{,}000$ shares are worth $1{,}000 \\times 10.00 = \\text{RM}10{,}000$. A front-end load would be charged on top, so the amount actually invested would be smaller than the amount paid.</p>\n<p><strong>Interpretation:</strong> the price is not set by supply and demand; it equals the fund's own valuation of its net assets, calculated at the next valuation point.</p>\n<h4>(c) Closed-end fund discount</h4>\n<p><strong>Given:</strong> market price $\\text{RM}9.40$, $NAV = \\text{RM}10.00$.</p>\n<p><strong>Formula:</strong></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{discount} = \\dfrac{\\text{market price} - NAV}{NAV}$$</div>\n<p><strong>Substitution:</strong></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{discount} = \\dfrac{9.40 - 10.00}{10.00} = \\dfrac{-0.60}{10.00}$$</div>\n<p><strong>Result:</strong></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{discount} = -6.00\\%\\ \\text{(a }6\\%\\text{ discount)}}$$</div>\n<p><strong>Interpretation:</strong> an investor can buy $\\text{RM}10.00$ of net assets per share for $\\text{RM}9.40$, a saving of $\\text{RM}0.60$ per share.</p>\n<h4>(d) Why price can stay below NAV</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>A closed-end fund cannot be redeemed at NAV, so nothing forces the price to rise to NAV. Price is set by <strong>supply and demand</strong>.</li>\n<li>Investors may mark the price down for fees and expenses, a poor manager record, illiquid or hard-to-value holdings, leverage, or a lack of investor interest.</li>\n<li>Weak sentiment can persist or widen the discount, so a discount is not proof of a bargain.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Exam tip:</strong> divide by NAV, not by the market price (dividing by the price gives $-6.38\\%$, which is wrong), and subtract liabilities before dividing by shares.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question tut-q\" data-tut-ch=\"12\" data-tut-t=\"Calculation\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q7</span><div><div class=\"tut-tags\"><span class=\"tut-topic\">Topics 10 and 11 · Q7</span><span class=\"tut-type tut-calculation\">Calculation</span></div><strong class=\"tut-title\">Fee-adjusted active versus index fund performance</strong></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body markdown-body compact\"><p>An investor has $\\text{RM}20{,}000$. Active Fund A charges a $3\\%$ front-end load and a $1.8\\%$ annual expense ratio; it earns a $9.5\\%$ gross portfolio return during the year. Index Fund B has no load, charges a $0.25\\%$ expense ratio and earns an $8.5\\%$ gross return. Assume the expense ratio is deducted from the value after the gross return. Calculate each year-end value and the investor's net return relative to the original $\\text{RM}20{,}000$. Which fund performs better for this year, and what further evidence is needed before choosing between them?</p><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal the exam-ready answer</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><p><strong>Convention:</strong> load first, then the gross return, then the expense ratio deducted from the resulting value.</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$V_{\\text{end}} = P\\,(1 - L)\\,(1 + r_g)\\,(1 - e),\\qquad \\text{net return} = \\dfrac{V_{\\text{end}} - P}{P}$$</div>\n<h4>Fund A</h4>\n<p><strong>Given:</strong> $P = \\text{RM}20{,}000$, $L = 3\\%$, $r_g = 9.5\\%$, $e = 1.8\\%$.</p>\n<p><strong>Substitution:</strong></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\begin{aligned}\n\\text{invested after load} &amp;= 20{,}000\\,(1 - 0.03) = \\text{RM}19{,}400.00\\\\\n\\text{value after gross return} &amp;= 19{,}400\\,(1.095) = \\text{RM}21{,}243.00\\\\\nV_A &amp;= 21{,}243\\,(1 - 0.018) = \\text{RM}20{,}860.63\n\\end{aligned}$$</div>\n<p><strong>Result:</strong></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{V_A = \\text{RM}20{,}860.63,\\qquad \\text{net return} = \\dfrac{860.63}{20{,}000} = 4.30\\%}$$</div>\n<h4>Fund B</h4>\n<p><strong>Given:</strong> $P = \\text{RM}20{,}000$, no load, $r_g = 8.5\\%$, $e = 0.25\\%$.</p>\n<p><strong>Substitution:</strong></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\begin{aligned}\n\\text{value after gross return} &amp;= 20{,}000\\,(1.085) = \\text{RM}21{,}700.00\\\\\nV_B &amp;= 21{,}700\\,(1 - 0.0025) = \\text{RM}21{,}645.75\n\\end{aligned}$$</div>\n<p><strong>Result:</strong></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{V_B = \\text{RM}21{,}645.75,\\qquad \\text{net return} = \\dfrac{1{,}645.75}{20{,}000} = 8.23\\%}$$</div>\n<h4>Which performs better?</h4>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th></th>\n<th>Fund A (active)</th>\n<th>Fund B (index)</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Gross return</td>\n<td>$9.5\\%$</td>\n<td>$8.5\\%$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Load</td>\n<td>$\\text{RM}600.00$</td>\n<td>$\\text{RM}0$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Expense ratio charge</td>\n<td>$\\text{RM}382.37$</td>\n<td>$\\text{RM}54.25$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Year-end value</td>\n<td>$\\text{RM}20{,}860.63$</td>\n<td>$\\text{RM}21{,}645.75$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Net return on $\\text{RM}20{,}000$</td>\n<td>$4.30\\%$</td>\n<td>$8.23\\%$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Interpretation:</strong> Fund B is better for this year by $\\text{RM}785.12$, about $3.9$ percentage points. A's $1$ percentage point of gross outperformance is far smaller than its extra costs: the $3\\%$ load and the $1.8\\%$ against $0.25\\%$ expense ratio. For A to match B in year one, its gross return would have to be about $13.62\\%$.</p>\n<p><strong>Further evidence needed before choosing:</strong>\n- Returns over <strong>many years</strong>, net of fees, against a matching benchmark. One year can be luck, and past performance is not a guarantee.\n- Whether A's manager shows <strong>persistent skill</strong> and a consistent style, rather than a one-year win.\n- Risk (volatility, beta), tracking difference of B, turnover and other costs such as brokerage and tax.\n- The investor's <strong>holding period</strong>: a load is a one-off cost that is spread over a longer holding, while the expense ratio recurs every year.</p>\n<p><strong>Exam tip:</strong> do not compare gross returns (A looks better) and do not charge the expense ratio on the original $\\text{RM}20{,}000$ or the load on the ending value. Measure the return on the full $\\text{RM}20{,}000$ paid.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question tut-q\" data-tut-ch=\"12\" data-tut-t=\"Reflection\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">R1</span><div><div class=\"tut-tags\"><span class=\"tut-topic\">Topics 10 and 11 · Reflection 1</span><span class=\"tut-type tut-reflection\">Reflection</span></div><strong class=\"tut-title\">When does professional fund management justify a higher fee?</strong></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body markdown-body compact\"><p>When does professional fund management justify a higher fee?</p><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal the exam-ready answer</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><p>A higher fee is justified only if the fund delivers better <strong>net-of-fee</strong> performance than a suitable lower-cost alternative, after adjusting for risk, <strong>consistently over a long period</strong>. Investors keep the return after the load and expense ratio, as Question 7 shows: Fund A's gross edge of $1$ percentage point left it $3.9$ percentage points behind. Past performance is not a guarantee, so evidence of skill must be persistent, not a one-year result. A higher fee can also be defended when the manager gives access to markets that are costly or difficult to index, such as less liquid or specialised assets, or when the service matches a particular investor need. Otherwise a low-cost index fund is the better default.</p></div></details></div></details></div></div><div class=\"tut-group theme-l14\" data-tut-group=\"14\"><h3 class=\"tut-group-title\"><span>Chapter 14</span> Options: Puts and Calls <small>7 questions</small></h3><div class=\"question-bank\"><details class=\"bank-question tut-q\" data-tut-ch=\"14\" data-tut-t=\"Concept\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q4</span><div><div class=\"tut-tags\"><span class=\"tut-topic\">Topics 10 and 11 · Q4</span><span class=\"tut-type tut-concept\">Concept</span></div><strong class=\"tut-title\">Option contracts, rights and obligations</strong></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body markdown-body compact\"><p>Distinguish a call from a put and an option buyer from an option writer. Explain strike price, premium and expiration date, and compare American and European exercise provisions. Why is the buyer's maximum loss limited to the premium while a naked call writer can face very large losses?</p><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal the exam-ready answer</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><h4>Definitions</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>A <strong>call</strong> gives the holder the <strong>right to buy</strong> the underlying at the strike price. A <strong>put</strong> gives the holder the <strong>right to sell</strong> it at the strike price.</li>\n<li>The <strong>buyer (holder, long)</strong> pays the premium and has the right but no obligation. The <strong>writer (seller, short)</strong> receives the premium and must perform if assigned.</li>\n<li><strong>Strike price</strong> $K$ is the contractual price at which the underlying is bought (call) or sold (put). <strong>Premium</strong> is the price of the option, paid up front, usually quoted per share. <strong>Expiration date</strong> is when the right ends.</li>\n<li>An option is a <strong>derivative</strong>: it gives no ownership, voting rights or dividends by itself.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>The four positions</h4>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Position</th>\n<th>Right or obligation</th>\n<th>Premium</th>\n<th>Gains when</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Long call</td>\n<td>Right to buy</td>\n<td>Pays</td>\n<td>Price rises sufficiently</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Short call</td>\n<td>Obligation to sell if assigned</td>\n<td>Receives</td>\n<td>Price stays below the strike</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Long put</td>\n<td>Right to sell</td>\n<td>Pays</td>\n<td>Price falls sufficiently</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Short put</td>\n<td>Obligation to buy if assigned</td>\n<td>Receives</td>\n<td>Price stays above the strike</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h4>American versus European exercise</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>American-style:</strong> may be exercised at any time up to expiry. <strong>European-style:</strong> exercisable only at expiry, though the option itself can still be traded earlier.</li>\n<li>American style is more flexible for the holder, so the exercise style must be checked for each contract. A holder can also <strong>close</strong> a position by an offsetting trade instead of exercising.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Why buyer and writer risks differ</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>The buyer holds a right, so the worst case is to let the option expire unused. The option loss is limited to the premium paid: for a call, the profit is $\\max(S_T - K, 0) - c \\geq -c$.</li>\n<li>A <strong>naked call writer</strong> has the obligation to sell shares they do not own. If the share price rises without limit, the payoff owed, $S_T - K$, rises without limit, while the gain is capped at the premium received. The loss is theoretically <strong>unlimited</strong>.</li>\n<li>A short put on a non-negative share has a large but <strong>bounded</strong> loss, at most $K - p$ per share.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Conclusion:</strong> the buyer pays a limited premium for a right, and the writer takes the obligation and the risk, which is why the writer is paid the premium.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question tut-q\" data-tut-ch=\"14\" data-tut-t=\"Concept\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q5</span><div><div class=\"tut-tags\"><span class=\"tut-topic\">Topics 10 and 11 · Q5</span><span class=\"tut-type tut-concept\">Concept</span></div><strong class=\"tut-title\">Option value, leverage and price drivers</strong></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body markdown-body compact\"><p>Distinguish intrinsic value from time value and classify calls and puts as in-the-money, at-the-money or out-of-the-money. Explain how the underlying share price, strike price, time to expiration, volatility, risk-free rate and dividend yield can influence option premiums. Why does leverage magnify percentage gains and losses?</p><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal the exam-ready answer</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><h4>Intrinsic value, time value and moneyness</h4>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{call intrinsic value} = \\max(S - K, 0),\\qquad \\text{put intrinsic value} = \\max(K - S, 0)$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{time value} = \\text{premium} - \\text{intrinsic value}$$</div>\n<p>Intrinsic value is never negative. At expiry, time value is zero and the option is worth its intrinsic value.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Status</th>\n<th>Call</th>\n<th>Put</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>In-the-money (ITM)</td>\n<td>$S &gt; K$</td>\n<td>$S &lt; K$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>At-the-money (ATM)</td>\n<td>$S \\approx K$</td>\n<td>$S \\approx K$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Out-of-the-money (OTM)</td>\n<td>$S &lt; K$</td>\n<td>$S &gt; K$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Moneyness does not subtract the premium, so an ITM option can still lose money overall.</p>\n<h4>What drives the premium (other things equal)</h4>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Factor</th>\n<th>Call</th>\n<th>Put</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Higher share price $S$</td>\n<td>Higher</td>\n<td>Lower</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Higher strike $K$</td>\n<td>Lower</td>\n<td>Higher</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Longer time to expiry</td>\n<td>Higher (more time for a favourable move)</td>\n<td>Higher</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Higher volatility</td>\n<td>Higher (the asymmetric payoff is worth more)</td>\n<td>Higher</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Higher risk-free rate <em>(beyond the chapter notes)</em></td>\n<td>Higher</td>\n<td>Lower</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Higher dividend yield <em>(beyond the chapter notes)</em></td>\n<td>Lower</td>\n<td>Higher</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<ul>\n<li>Volatility raises value because the holder keeps the upside but loses at most the premium; it says nothing about the direction of the next move.</li>\n<li>The Black-Scholes model uses share price, strike, time, risk-free rate and volatility. Dividends need an adjustment. It gives a conditional theoretical price, not a forecast of profit.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Leverage</h4>\n<p>An option costs a small premium for exposure to the full share. The payoff is the same amount in ringgit, but it is divided by a much smaller outlay, so the percentage return is magnified. Example from the notes: share $\\text{RM}50$ to $\\text{RM}75$ is a $50\\%$ stock return, but a call bought at $\\text{RM}5$ pays $\\text{RM}25$ at expiry, a profit of $\\text{RM}20$, which is a $400\\%$ return on the premium. The same leverage works in reverse: if the share ends at or below the strike, the buyer loses $100\\%$ of the premium.</p>\n<p><strong>Conclusion:</strong> premium is intrinsic value plus time value; it rises with volatility and time, and leverage makes option returns much larger in percentage terms, in both directions.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question tut-q\" data-tut-ch=\"14\" data-tut-t=\"Calculation\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q8</span><div><div class=\"tut-tags\"><span class=\"tut-topic\">Topics 10 and 11 · Q8</span><span class=\"tut-type tut-calculation\">Calculation</span></div><strong class=\"tut-title\">Intrinsic value and time value of calls and puts</strong></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body markdown-body compact\"><p>A share trades at $\\text{RM}52$ and options with a $\\text{RM}50$ strike have three months to expiration. The call premium is $\\text{RM}4.50$ and the put premium is $\\text{RM}1.20$.</p>\n<p>(a) Calculate the intrinsic value and time value of each option.</p>\n<p>(b) Classify each option as in-, at- or out-of-the-money.</p>\n<p>(c) Explain why an out-of-the-money option can still have a positive premium before expiration.</p><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal the exam-ready answer</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><h4>(a) Intrinsic value and time value</h4>\n<p><strong>Given:</strong> $S = \\text{RM}52$, $K = \\text{RM}50$, call premium $c = \\text{RM}4.50$, put premium $p = \\text{RM}1.20$.</p>\n<p><strong>Formula:</strong></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\begin{aligned}\n\\text{call intrinsic} &amp;= \\max(S - K, 0)\\\\\n\\text{put intrinsic} &amp;= \\max(K - S, 0)\\\\\n\\text{time value} &amp;= \\text{premium} - \\text{intrinsic}\n\\end{aligned}$$</div>\n<p><strong>Substitution:</strong></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\begin{aligned}\n\\text{Call: } &amp;\\max(52 - 50, 0) = 2.00, &amp; \\text{time value} &amp;= 4.50 - 2.00\\\\\n\\text{Put: } &amp;\\max(50 - 52, 0) = 0.00, &amp; \\text{time value} &amp;= 1.20 - 0.00\n\\end{aligned}$$</div>\n<p><strong>Result:</strong></p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th></th>\n<th>Premium</th>\n<th>Intrinsic value</th>\n<th>Time value</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Call</td>\n<td>$\\text{RM}4.50$</td>\n<td>$\\text{RM}2.00$</td>\n<td>$\\text{RM}2.50$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Put</td>\n<td>$\\text{RM}1.20$</td>\n<td>$\\text{RM}0.00$</td>\n<td>$\\text{RM}1.20$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{Call: } 2.00 + 2.50 = 4.50;\\qquad \\text{Put: } 0.00 + 1.20 = 1.20}$$</div>\n<p><strong>Interpretation:</strong> more than half of the call premium is time value ($2.50$ of $4.50$), which decays to zero by expiry. The put premium is entirely time value, so it is the price of the chance that the share falls below $\\text{RM}50$.</p>\n<h4>(b) Moneyness</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Call:</strong> $S = 52 &gt; K = 50$, so it is <strong>in-the-money</strong> by $\\text{RM}2$.</li>\n<li><strong>Put:</strong> $S = 52 &gt; K = 50$, so it is <strong>out-of-the-money</strong> by $\\text{RM}2$. Neither option is at-the-money, because the share is not close to the strike.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Interpretation:</strong> moneyness ignores the premium. The call buyer breaks even only if the share finishes above $K + c = \\text{RM}54.50$, and the put buyer only if it finishes below $K - p = \\text{RM}48.80$.</p>\n<h4>(c) Why an OTM option has a positive premium</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>An OTM option has <strong>no intrinsic value</strong>, but its whole premium is time value: with three months left the share price can still move across the strike.</li>\n<li>The buyer's loss is limited to the premium, while the profit if the share moves far enough is large (and unlimited for a call). This asymmetry has positive value, and a more volatile share or longer time raises it.</li>\n<li>The writer must be compensated for the obligation of being assigned, so the writer demands a premium.</li>\n<li>At expiry no time remains, so an OTM option is worth zero.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Exam tip:</strong> intrinsic value is never negative, so the put's intrinsic value is $0$, not $-2$. Time value is premium <strong>minus</strong> intrinsic value, not the premium alone.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question tut-q\" data-tut-ch=\"14\" data-tut-t=\"Calculation\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q9</span><div><div class=\"tut-tags\"><span class=\"tut-topic\">Topics 10 and 11 · Q9</span><span class=\"tut-type tut-calculation\">Calculation</span></div><strong class=\"tut-title\">Speculating with a long call</strong></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body markdown-body compact\"><p>A share currently trades at $\\text{RM}42$. An investor buys one call with a $\\text{RM}40$ strike for a premium of $\\text{RM}3$ per share and holds it to expiration. Ignore transaction costs.</p>\n<p>(a) Calculate the call payoff, profit and percentage return on the premium if the share price at expiration is $\\text{RM}50$.</p>\n<p>(b) Calculate the break-even share price.</p>\n<p>(c) State the maximum possible loss and profit potential.</p>\n<p>(d) Compare the option return with the return from buying the share at $\\text{RM}42$ and selling it at $\\text{RM}50$.</p><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal the exam-ready answer</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><p>All figures are per share. For one $100$-share contract, multiply by $100$.</p>\n<h4>(a) Payoff, profit and return at $S_T = 50$</h4>\n<p><strong>Given:</strong> $K = \\text{RM}40$, $c = \\text{RM}3$, $S_T = \\text{RM}50$.</p>\n<p><strong>Formula:</strong></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\begin{aligned}\n\\text{payoff} &amp;= \\max(S_T - K, 0)\\\n\\text{profit} &amp;= \\text{payoff} - c\\\n\\text{return} &amp;= \\dfrac{\\text{profit}}{c}\n\\end{aligned}$$</div>\n<p><strong>Substitution:</strong></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\begin{aligned}\n\\text{payoff} &amp;= \\max(50 - 40, 0) = 10\\\n\\text{profit} &amp;= 10 - 3 = 7\\\n\\text{return} &amp;= \\dfrac{7}{3}\n\\end{aligned}$$</div>\n<p><strong>Result:</strong></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{payoff} = \\text{RM}10,\\quad \\text{profit} = \\text{RM}7,\\quad \\text{return} = 233.33\\%}$$</div>\n<p><strong>Interpretation:</strong> the $\\text{RM}3$ premium turns into $\\text{RM}10$. On one contract, $\\text{RM}300$ becomes $\\text{RM}1{,}000$ for a $\\text{RM}700$ profit.</p>\n<h4>(b) Break-even</h4>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$S_{BE} = K + c = 40 + 3$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{S_{BE} = \\text{RM}43}$$</div>\n<p><strong>Interpretation:</strong> the share must finish above $\\text{RM}43$, which is $\\text{RM}1$ above today's price, for the buyer to profit. Between $\\text{RM}40$ and $\\text{RM}43$ the call has intrinsic value but still loses money overall.</p>\n<h4>(c) Maximum loss and profit potential</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Maximum loss</strong> $= c = \\text{RM}3$ per share ($\\text{RM}300$ per contract), which is $100\\%$ of the premium. It happens if $S_T \\leq 40$.</li>\n<li><strong>Profit potential</strong> is theoretically <strong>unlimited</strong>: profit $= S_T - 43$ rises $\\text{RM}1$ for every $\\text{RM}1$ the share rises above $\\text{RM}43$.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{max loss} = \\text{RM}3\\text{ per share};\\qquad \\text{max profit: unlimited}}$$</div>\n<h4>(d) Option versus buying the share</h4>\n<p><strong>Share:</strong></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{return} = \\dfrac{50 - 42}{42} = \\dfrac{8}{42} = 19.05\\%,\\qquad \\text{profit} = \\text{RM}8\\text{ per share}$$</div>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Expiry price</th>\n<th>Option profit</th>\n<th>Option return</th>\n<th>Share profit</th>\n<th>Share return</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>$\\text{RM}40$ or less</td>\n<td>$-\\text{RM}3$</td>\n<td>$-100\\%$</td>\n<td>$-\\text{RM}2$ at $\\text{RM}40$</td>\n<td>$-4.76\\%$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>$\\text{RM}43$</td>\n<td>$\\text{RM}0$</td>\n<td>$0\\%$</td>\n<td>$+\\text{RM}1$</td>\n<td>$+2.38\\%$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>$\\text{RM}50$</td>\n<td>$+\\text{RM}7$</td>\n<td>$+233.33\\%$</td>\n<td>$+\\text{RM}8$</td>\n<td>$+19.05\\%$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{option } 233.33\\%\\ \\text{versus share } 19.05\\%}$$</div>\n<p><strong>Interpretation:</strong>\n- The option returns about $12$ times as much in percentage terms because it needs only $\\text{RM}3$ of capital against $\\text{RM}42$, which is leverage.\n- In ringgit, the share earns more ($\\text{RM}8$ against $\\text{RM}7$) because the call was bought with $\\text{RM}2$ of intrinsic value ($42 - 40$) and $\\text{RM}1$ of time value. That $\\text{RM}1$ of time value is lost by expiry, which is exactly the $\\text{RM}1$ gap between the share profit and the option profit.\n- The leverage works both ways: the option can lose $100\\%$ of the premium, while the share loses only $4.76\\%$ if it falls to $\\text{RM}40$. The option holder also receives no dividends.</p>\n<p><strong>Exam tip:</strong> profit is payoff minus premium, so $\\text{RM}10$ is not the profit. Compute the return on the premium paid, and compare it with the share return on $\\text{RM}42$.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question tut-q\" data-tut-ch=\"14 5\" data-tut-t=\"Calculation\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q10</span><div><div class=\"tut-tags\"><span class=\"tut-topic\">Topics 10 and 11 · Q10</span><span class=\"tut-type tut-calculation\">Calculation</span><span class=\"tut-also\">Also Ch 5</span></div><strong class=\"tut-title\">Protecting a portfolio with index puts</strong></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body markdown-body compact\"><p>A $\\text{RM}200{,}000$ equity portfolio closely tracks an index currently at $100$. The investor buys $10$ index put contracts with strike $100$, premium $4$ index points and a multiplier of $\\text{RM}200$ per point.</p>\n<p>(a) Calculate the hedge premium.</p>\n<p>(b) If the index falls to $80$ at expiration and the portfolio falls by the same percentage, calculate the unhedged portfolio loss, put payoff and hedged ending wealth after the premium.</p>\n<p>(c) Repeat the ending-wealth calculation if the index rises to $115$.</p>\n<p>(d) Evaluate the protection, opportunity cost and basis risk of the strategy.</p><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal the exam-ready answer</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><h4>(a) Hedge premium</h4>\n<p><strong>Given:</strong> $10$ contracts, premium $4$ points, multiplier $\\text{RM}200$ per point.</p>\n<p><strong>Formula:</strong></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{premium} = \\text{contracts} \\times \\text{points} \\times \\text{multiplier}$$</div>\n<p><strong>Substitution and result:</strong></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{premium} = 10 \\times 4 \\times 200$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{premium} = \\text{RM}8{,}000}$$</div>\n<p><strong>Interpretation:</strong> this is $4\\%$ of the portfolio. The put covers $10 \\times 100 \\times 200 = \\text{RM}200{,}000$, so the hedge matches the portfolio exactly.</p>\n<h4>(b) Index falls to $80$</h4>\n<p><strong>Formula:</strong></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\begin{aligned}\n\\text{put payoff} &amp;= n \\times \\max(K - S_T, 0) \\times M\\\\\n\\text{ending wealth} &amp;= V_T + \\text{put payoff} - \\text{premium}\n\\end{aligned}$$</div>\n<p><strong>Substitution:</strong></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\begin{aligned}\n\\text{index return} &amp;= \\dfrac{80 - 100}{100} = -20\\%\\\\\n\\text{unhedged loss} &amp;= 200{,}000 \\times 0.20 = \\text{RM}40{,}000,\\quad V_T = \\text{RM}160{,}000\\\\\n\\text{put payoff} &amp;= 10 \\times (100 - 80) \\times 200 = \\text{RM}40{,}000\\\\\n\\text{ending wealth} &amp;= 160{,}000 + 40{,}000 - 8{,}000\n\\end{aligned}$$</div>\n<p><strong>Result:</strong></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{loss} = \\text{RM}40{,}000,\\quad \\text{put payoff} = \\text{RM}40{,}000,\\quad \\text{hedged wealth} = \\text{RM}192{,}000}$$</div>\n<p><strong>Interpretation:</strong> the put pays exactly the portfolio loss, so only the premium is lost: the hedged result is $\\text{RM}8{,}000$ below $\\text{RM}200{,}000$, a $4\\%$ loss, against a $20\\%$ loss unhedged.</p>\n<h4>(c) Index rises to $115$</h4>\n<p><strong>Substitution:</strong></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\begin{aligned}\nV_T &amp;= 200{,}000 \\times 1.15 = \\text{RM}230{,}000\\\\\n\\text{put payoff} &amp;= 10 \\times \\max(100 - 115, 0) \\times 200 = \\text{RM}0\\\\\n\\text{ending wealth} &amp;= 230{,}000 + 0 - 8{,}000\n\\end{aligned}$$</div>\n<p><strong>Result:</strong></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{hedged wealth} = \\text{RM}222{,}000}$$</div>\n<p><strong>Interpretation:</strong> the put expires worthless, so the hedged investor gains $\\text{RM}22{,}000$ ($11\\%$) against $\\text{RM}30{,}000$ ($15\\%$) unhedged. The $\\text{RM}8{,}000$ difference is the cost of the insurance.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Index at expiry</th>\n<th>Unhedged wealth</th>\n<th>Put payoff</th>\n<th>Hedged wealth</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>$80$</td>\n<td>$\\text{RM}160{,}000$</td>\n<td>$\\text{RM}40{,}000$</td>\n<td>$\\text{RM}192{,}000$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>$100$</td>\n<td>$\\text{RM}200{,}000$</td>\n<td>$\\text{RM}0$</td>\n<td>$\\text{RM}192{,}000$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>$115$</td>\n<td>$\\text{RM}230{,}000$</td>\n<td>$\\text{RM}0$</td>\n<td>$\\text{RM}222{,}000$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h4>(d) Evaluation</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Protection:</strong> at or below the strike, hedged wealth is fixed at $\\text{RM}200{,}000 - \\text{RM}8{,}000 = \\text{RM}192{,}000$, so the worst-case loss is $\\text{RM}8{,}000$ ($4\\%$). Protection lasts only until the puts expire.</li>\n<li><strong>Opportunity cost:</strong> the premium is paid whatever happens, and it reduces every outcome by $\\text{RM}8{,}000$. The hedged portfolio only beats $\\text{RM}200{,}000$ if the index finishes above $100 + 4 = 104$, a $4\\%$ rise.</li>\n<li><strong>Basis risk:</strong> the result holds only because the portfolio falls by exactly the same percentage as the index. If the portfolio falls $25\\%$ while the index falls $20\\%$, the portfolio is worth $\\text{RM}150{,}000$ and hedged wealth is $150{,}000 + 40{,}000 - 8{,}000 = \\text{RM}182{,}000$, a $\\text{RM}18{,}000$ loss. A portfolio that differs from the index (stock selection, sector mix, beta) keeps unhedged risk.</li>\n<li>Other limits: the index put cash-settles, so it hedges the index and not the individual shares, and the hedge ends at expiry.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Exam tip:</strong> premium is contracts $\\times$ points $\\times$ multiplier, and the index put pays $\\max(K - S_T, 0)$ with the same multiplier. Subtract the premium <strong>once</strong>, and do not forget it in the rising-market case.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question tut-q\" data-tut-ch=\"14\" data-tut-t=\"Reflection\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">R2</span><div><div class=\"tut-tags\"><span class=\"tut-topic\">Topics 10 and 11 · Reflection 2</span><span class=\"tut-type tut-reflection\">Reflection</span></div><strong class=\"tut-title\">Why a limited ringgit loss can still be high risk</strong></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body markdown-body compact\"><p>Why can a limited ringgit loss still represent a high-risk option investment?</p><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal the exam-ready answer</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><p>The most a buyer can lose is the premium, but that is <strong>$100\\%$ of the money committed</strong> to the position, and an out-of-the-money option often expires worthless. Leverage makes the percentage outcome extreme: in Question 9 the premium of $\\text{RM}3$ can earn $233.33\\%$ or lose $100\\%$, whereas the share itself would lose only $4.76\\%$ at the same price. Time value also decays, so being right about direction is not enough: the move must be large enough and early enough to beat the break-even price. Repeated or oversized option purchases can therefore wipe out capital even though each trade's loss is limited. Writers face worse risk, as a naked call has theoretically unlimited loss.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question tut-q\" data-tut-ch=\"14 5\" data-tut-t=\"Reflection\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">R3</span><div><div class=\"tut-tags\"><span class=\"tut-topic\">Topics 10 and 11 · Reflection 3</span><span class=\"tut-type tut-reflection\">Reflection</span><span class=\"tut-also\">Also Ch 5</span></div><strong class=\"tut-title\">The key assumption when hedging with index puts</strong></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body markdown-body compact\"><p>Which assumption is most important when using index puts to hedge a portfolio?</p><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal the exam-ready answer</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><p>The most important assumption is that the portfolio <strong>moves in line with the index</strong>, so the put payoff offsets the portfolio loss: low <strong>basis risk</strong>, with the number of contracts matched to the portfolio value. Question 10 assumes the portfolio falls by exactly the same percentage as the index, which gives a worst-case loss of only the premium. If the portfolio is more volatile than the index, or holds different sectors or stocks, the put pays too little and the hedge fails: with a $25\\%$ portfolio fall against a $20\\%$ index fall, wealth is $\\text{RM}182{,}000$, not $\\text{RM}192{,}000$. A hedge also assumes that the expiry date and the strike fit the period and loss level the investor wants to protect.</p></div></details></div></details></div></div>");
